('ramayana-2-109-1', 'en', 'Rama decides to move on.', 'Ayodhya Kanda, chapter 109: Rama leaves Chitrakuta, which reminds him of his family and was trampled by Bharata''s army, and goes to the hermitage of the sage Atri. Atri''s wife Anasuya, a renowned ascetic, welcomes Sita. Anasuya praises Sita''s loyalty to Rama. Her teaching that a husband is a woman''s highest deity, whatever his faults, reflects the ideals of that age; readers today may take from it the value of faithfulness and loyalty in a relationship, not a duty to accept mistreatment.', '[{"word":"apayāta","meaning":"departed"},{"word":"vicintayan","meaning":"reflecting"},{"word":"arocayat","meaning":"liked"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
