etad dha sma vai tad vidvān kumārahārita āha -- bahavo maryā brāhmanāyanā nirindriyā visukṛto ''smāl lokāt prayanti ya idam avidvāṃso ''dhopahāsaṃ carantīti
bahu vā idaṃ suptasya vā jāgrato vā retaḥ skandati', null, 6004004),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-5', 5, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 5', 'तदभिमृशेदनु वा मन्त्रयेत -- यन्मेऽद्य रेतः पृथिवीमस्कान्त्सीद्यदोषधीरप्यसरद्यदपः।
इदमहं तद्रेत आददे।
पुनर्मामैतु इन्द्रियं पुनस्तेजः पुनर्भगः।
पुनरग्निर्धिष्ण्या यथास्थानं कल्पन्ताम्।
इत्यनामिकाङ्गुष्ठाभ्यामादायान्तरेण स्तनौ वा भ्रुवौ वा निमृज्यात्॥', 'tad abhimṛśed anu vā mantrayeta -- yan me ''dya retaḥ pṛthivīm askāntsīd yad oṣadhīr apy asarad yad apaḥ
idam ahaṃ tad reta ādade
punar mām aitu indriyaṃ punas tejaḥ punar bhagaḥ
punar agnir dhiṣṇyā yathāsthānaṃ kalpantām
ity anāmikāṅguṣṭhābhyām ādāyāntareṇa stanau vā bhruvau vā nimṛjyāt', null, 6004005),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-6', 6, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 6', 'अथ यद्युदक आत्मानं पश्येत्तदभिमन्त्रयेत -- मयि तेज इन्द्रियं यशो द्रविणं सुकृतमिति।
श्रीर्ह वा एषा स्त्रीणां यन्मलोद्वासाः।
तस्मान्मलोद्वाससं यशस्विनीमभिक्रम्योपमन्त्रयेत॥', 'atha yady udaka ātmānaṃ paśyet tad abhimantrayeta -- mayi teja indriyaṃ yaśo draviṇaṃ sukṛtam iti
śrīr ha vā eṣā strīṇāṃ yan malodvāsāḥ
tasmān malodvāsasaṃ yaśasvinīm abhikramyopamantrayeta', null, 6004006),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-7', 7, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 7', 'सा चेदस्मै न दद्यात्काममेनामवक्रिणीयात्।
सा चेदस्मै नैव दद्यात्काममेनां यष्ट्या वा पाणिना वोपहत्यातिक्रामेत्।
इन्द्रियेन ते यशसा यश आदद इति।
अयशा एव भवति॥', 'sā ced asmai na dadyāt kāmam enām avakriṇīyāt
sā ced asmai naiva dadyāt kāmam enāṃ yaṣṭyā vā pāṇinā vopahatyātikrāmet
indriyena te yaśasā yaśa ādada iti
ayaśā eva bhavati', null, 6004007),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-8', 8, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 8', 'सा चेदस्मै दद्यादिन्द्रियेण ते यशसा यश आदधामीति।
यशस्विनावेव भवतः॥', 'sā ced asmai dadyād indriyeṇa te yaśasā yaśa ādadhāmīti
yaśasvināv eva bhavataḥ', null, 6004008),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-9', 9, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 9', 'स यामिच्छेत्-- कामयेत मेति तस्यामर्थं निष्ठाय मुखेन मुखं संधायोपस्थमस्या अभिमृश्य जपेत्-- अङ्गादङ्गात्संभवसि हृदयादधिजायसे।
स त्वमङ्गकषायोऽसि दिग्धविद्धामिव मादयेमाममूं मयीति॥', 'sa yām icchet -- kāmayeta meti tasyām arthaṃ niṣṭhāya mukhena mukhaṃ saṃdhāyopastham asyā abhimṛśya japet -- aṅgād aṅgāt saṃbhavasi hṛdayād adhijāyase
sa tvam aṅgakaṣāyo ''si digdhaviddhām iva mādayemām amūṃ mayīti', null, 6004009),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-10', 10, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 10', 'अथ यामिच्छेन्न गर्भं दधीतेति तस्यामर्थं निष्ठाय मुखेन मुखं संधायाभिप्राण्यापान्यात्।
इन्द्रियेण ते रेतसा रेत आदद इति।
अरेता एव भवति॥', 'atha yām icchen na garbhaṃ dadhīteti tasyām arthaṃ niṣṭhāya mukhena mukhaṃ saṃdhāyābhiprāṇyāpānyāt
indriyeṇa te retasā reta ādada iti
aretā eva bhavati', null, 6004010),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-11', 11, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 11', 'अथ यामिच्छेद्दधीतेति तस्यामर्थं निष्ठाय मुखेन मुखं संधायापान्याभिप्राण्यात्।
इन्द्रियेण ते रेतसा रेत आदधामीति।
गर्भिण्येव भवति॥', 'atha yām icched dadhīteti tasyām arthaṃ niṣṭhāya mukhena mukhaṃ saṃdhāyāpānyābhiprāṇyāt
indriyeṇa te retasā reta ādadhāmīti
garbhiṇy eva bhavati', null, 6004011),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-12', 12, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 12', 'अथ यस्य जायायै जारः स्यात्तं चेद्द्विष्यादामपात्रेऽग्निमुपसमाधाय प्रतिलोमं शरबर्हिः स्तीर्त्वा तस्मिन्नेताः शरभृष्टीः प्रतिलोमाः सर्पिषाक्ता जुहुयात्।
मम समिद्धेऽहौषीः।
प्राणापानौ त आददेऽसाविति।
मम समिद्धेऽहौषीः।
पुत्रपशूंस्त आददेऽसाविति।
मम समिद्धेऽहौषीः।
इष्टासुकृते त आददेऽसाविति।
मम समिद्धेऽहौषीः।
अशापराकाशौ त आददेऽसाविति।
स वा एष निरिन्द्रियो विसुकृतोऽस्माल्लोकाद्प्रैति यमेवंविद्ब्राह्मणः शपति।
तस्मादेवंवित्श्रोत्रियस्य दारेण नोपहासमिच्छेत्।
उत ह्येवंवित्परो भवति॥', 'atha yasya jāyāyai jāraḥ syāt taṃ ced dviṣyād āmapātre ''gnim upasamādhāya pratilomaṃ śarabarhiḥ stīrtvā tasminn etāḥ śarabhṛṣṭīḥ pratilomāḥ sarpiṣāktā juhuyāt
mama samiddhe ''hauṣīḥ
prāṇāpānau ta ādade ''sāv iti
mama samiddhe ''hauṣīḥ
putrapaśūṃs ta ādade ''sāv iti
mama samiddhe ''hauṣīḥ
iṣṭāsukṛte ta ādade ''sāv iti
mama samiddhe ''hauṣīḥ
aśāparākāśau ta ādade ''sāv iti
sa vā eṣa nirindriyo visukṛto ''smāl lokād praiti yam evaṃvid brāhmaṇaḥ śapati
tasmād evaṃvitśrotriyasya dāreṇa nopahāsam icchet
uta hy evaṃvit paro bhavati', null, 6004012),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-13', 13, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 13', 'अथ यस्य जायामार्तवं विन्देत्त्र्यहं कंसे न पिबेत्।
अहतवासाः।
नैनां वृषलो न वृषल्यपहन्यात्।
त्रिरात्रान्त आप्लुत्य व्रीहीनवघातयेत्॥', 'atha yasya jāyām ārtavaṃ vindet tryahaṃ kaṃse na pibet
ahatavāsāḥ
naināṃ vṛṣalo na vṛṣaly apahanyāt
trirātrānta āplutya vrīhīn avaghātayet', null, 6004013),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-14', 14, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 14', 'स य इच्छेत्-- पुत्रो मे शुक्लो जायेत वेदमनुब्रुवीत सर्वमायुरियादिति क्षीरौदनं पाचयित्वा सर्पिष्मन्तमश्नीयाताम्।
ईश्वरौ जनयितवै॥', 'sa ya icchet -- putro me śuklo jāyeta vedam anubruvīta sarvam āyur iyād iti kṣīraudanaṃ pācayitvā sarpiṣmantam aśnīyātām
īśvarau janayitavai', null, 6004014),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-15', 15, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 15', 'अथ य इच्छेत्-- पुत्रो मे कपिलः पिङ्गलो जायेत द्वौ वेदावनुब्रुवीत सर्वमायुरियादिति दध्योदनं पाचयित्वा सर्पिष्मन्तमश्नीयाताम्।
ईश्वरौ जनयितवै॥', 'atha ya icchet -- putro me kapilaḥ piṅgalo jāyeta dvau vedāv anubruvīta sarvam āyur iyād iti dadhyodanaṃ pācayitvā sarpiṣmantam aśnīyātām
īśvarau janayitavai', null, 6004015),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-16', 16, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 16', 'अथ य इच्छेत्-- पुत्रो मे श्यामो लोहिताक्षो जायेत त्रीन्वेदाननुब्रुवीत सर्वमायुरियादिति उदौदनं पाचयित्वा सर्पिष्मन्तमश्नीयाताम्।
ईश्वरौ जनयितवै॥', 'atha ya icchet -- putro me śyāmo lohitākṣo jāyeta trīn vedān anubruvīta sarvam āyur iyād iti udaudanaṃ pācayitvā sarpiṣmantam aśnīyātām
īśvarau janayitavai', null, 6004016),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-17', 17, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 17', 'अथ य इच्छेद्-- दुहिता मे पण्डिता जायेत सर्वमायुरियादिति तिलौदनं पाचयित्वा सर्पिष्मन्तमश्नीयाताम्।
ईश्वरौ जनयितवै॥', 'atha ya icched -- duhitā me paṇḍitā jāyeta sarvam āyur iyād iti tilaudanaṃ pācayitvā sarpiṣmantam aśnīyātām
īśvarau janayitavai', null, 6004017),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-18', 18, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 18', 'अथ य इच्छेत्-- पुत्रो मे पण्डितो विगीतः समितिंगमः शुश्रूषितां वाचं भाषिता जायेत सर्वान्वेदाननुब्रुवीत सर्वमायुरियादिति मांसौदनं पाचयित्वा सर्पिष्मन्तमश्नीयाताम्।
ईश्वरौ जनयितवाइ।
औक्षेण वार्षभेण वा॥', 'atha ya icchet -- putro me paṇḍito vigītaḥ samitiṃgamaḥ śuśrūṣitāṃ vācaṃ bhāṣitā jāyeta sarvān vedān anubruvīta sarvam āyur iyād iti māṃsaudanaṃ pācayitvā sarpiṣmantam aśnīyātām
īśvarau janayitavāi
aukṣeṇa vārṣabheṇa vā', null, 6004018),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-19', 19, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 19', 'अथाभिप्रातरेव स्थालीपाकावृताज्यं चेष्टित्वा स्थालीपाकस्योपघातं जुहोत्यग्नये स्वाहानुमतये स्वाहा देवाय सवित्रे सत्यप्रसवाय स्वाहेति।
हुत्वोद्धृत्य प्राश्नाति।
प्राश्येतरस्याः प्रयच्छति।
प्रक्षाल्य पाणी उदपात्रं पूरयित्वा तेनैनां त्रिरभ्युक्षति -- उत्तिष्ठातो विश्वावसोऽन्यामिच्छ प्रपूर्व्याम्।
सं जायां पत्या सहेति॥', 'athābhiprātar eva sthālīpākāvṛtājyaṃ ceṣṭitvā sthālīpākasyopaghātaṃ juhoty agnaye svāhānumataye svāhā devāya savitre satyaprasavāya svāheti
hutvoddhṛtya prāśnāti
prāśyetarasyāḥ prayacchati
prakṣālya pāṇī udapātraṃ pūrayitvā tenaināṃ trir abhyukṣati -- uttiṣṭhāto viśvāvaso ''nyām iccha prapūrvyām
saṃ jāyāṃ patyā saheti', null, 6004019),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-20', 20, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 20', 'अथैनामभिपद्यते -- अमोऽहमस्मि सा त्वम्।
सा त्वमस्यमोऽहम्।
सामाहमस्मि ऋक्त्वम्।
द्यौरहम्पृथिवी त्वम्।
तावेहि संरभावहै सह रेतो दधावहै।
पुंसे पुत्राय वित्तय इति॥', 'athainām abhipadyate -- amo ''ham asmi sā tvam
sā tvam asy amo ''ham
sāmāham asmi ṛk tvam
dyaur aham pṛthivī tvam
tāv ehi saṃrabhāvahai saha reto dadhāvahai
puṃse putrāya vittaya iti', null, 6004020),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-21', 21, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 21', 'अथास्या ऊरू विहापयति -- विजिहीथां द्यावापृथिवी इति।
तस्यामर्थं निष्ठाय मुखेन मुखं संधाय त्रिरेनामनुलोमामनुमार्ष्टि -- विष्णुर्योनिं कल्पयतु त्वष्टा रूपाणि पिंशतु।
आ सिञ्चतु प्रजापतिर्धाता गर्भं दधातु ते।
गर्भं धेहि सिनीवालि गर्भं धेहि पृथुष्टुके।
गर्भं ते अश्विनौ देवावाधत्तां पुष्करस्रजौ॥', 'athāsyā ūrū vihāpayati -- vijihīthāṃ dyāvāpṛthivī iti
tasyām arthaṃ niṣṭhāya mukhena mukhaṃ saṃdhāya trir enām anulomām anumārṣṭi -- viṣṇur yoniṃ kalpayatu tvaṣṭā rūpāṇi piṃśatu
ā siñcatu prajāpatir dhātā garbhaṃ dadhātu te
garbhaṃ dhehi sinīvāli garbhaṃ dhehi pṛthuṣṭuke
garbhaṃ te aśvinau devāv ādhattāṃ puṣkarasrajau', null, 6004021),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-22', 22, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 22', 'हिरण्मयी अरणी याभ्यां निर्मन्थतामश्विनौ।
तं ते गर्भं हवामहे दशमे मासि सूतये।
यथाग्निगर्भा पृथिवी यथा द्यौरैन्द्रेण गर्भिणी।
वायुर्दिशां यथा गर्भ एवं गर्भं दधामि तेऽसाविति॥', 'hiraṇmayī araṇī yābhyāṃ nirmanthatām aśvinau
taṃ te garbhaṃ havāmahe daśame māsi sūtaye
yathāgnigarbhā pṛthivī yathā dyaur aindreṇa garbhiṇī
vāyur diśāṃ yathā garbha evaṃ garbhaṃ dadhāmi te ''sāv iti', null, 6004022),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-23', 23, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 23', 'सोष्यन्तीमद्भिरभ्युक्षति -- यथा वायुः पुष्करिणीं समिङ्गयति सर्वतः।
एवा ते गर्भ एजतु सहावैतु जरायुणा।
इन्द्रस्यायं व्रजः कृतः सार्गलः सपरिश्रयः।
तमिन्द्र निर्जहि गर्भेण सावरां सहेति॥', 'soṣyantīm adbhir abhyukṣati -- yathā vāyuḥ puṣkariṇīṃ samiṅgayati sarvataḥ
evā te garbha ejatu sahāvaitu jarāyuṇā
indrasyāyaṃ vrajaḥ kṛtaḥ sārgalaḥ sapariśrayaḥ
tam indra nirjahi garbheṇa sāvarāṃ saheti', null, 6004023),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-24', 24, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 24', 'जातेऽग्निमुपसमाधायाङ्क आधाय कंसे पृषदाज्यं संनीय पृषदाज्यस्योपघातं जुहोति -- अस्मिन्सहस्रं पुष्यासमेधमानः स्वे गृहे।
अस्योपसन्द्यां मा छैत्सीत्प्रजया च पशुभिश्च स्वाहा।
मयि प्राणांस्त्वयि मनसा जुहोमि स्वाहा।
यत्कर्मणात्यरीरिचम्यद्वा न्यूनमिहाकरम्।
अग्निष्टत्स्विष्टकृद्विद्वान्स्विष्टं सुहुतं करोतु नः स्वाहेति॥', 'jāte ''gnim upasamādhāyāṅka ādhāya kaṃse pṛṣadājyaṃ saṃnīya pṛṣadājyasyopaghātaṃ juhoti -- asminsahasraṃ puṣyāsam edhamānaḥ sve gṛhe
asyopasandyāṃ mā chaitsīt prajayā ca paśubhiś ca svāhā
mayi prāṇāṃs tvayi manasā juhomi svāhā
yat karmaṇātyarīricam yad vā nyūnam ihākaram
agniṣṭat sviṣṭakṛd vidvān sviṣṭaṃ suhutaṃ karotu naḥ svāheti', null, 6004024),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-25', 25, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 25', 'अथास्य दक्षिणं कर्णमभिनिधाय वाग्वागिति त्रिः।
अथ दधि मधु घृतं संनीयानन्तर्हितेन जातरूपेण प्राशयति।
भूस्ते दधामि भुवस्ते दधामि स्वस्ते दधामि भूर्भुवः स्वः सर्वं त्वयि दधामीति॥', 'athāsya dakṣiṇaṃ karṇam abhinidhāya vāg vāg iti triḥ
atha dadhi madhu ghṛtaṃ saṃnīyānantarhitena jātarūpeṇa prāśayati
bhūs te dadhāmi bhuvas te dadhāmi svas te dadhāmi bhūr bhuvaḥ svaḥ sarvaṃ tvayi dadhāmīti', null, 6004025),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-26', 26, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 26', 'अथास्य नाम करोति वेदोऽसीति।
तदस्यैतद्गुह्यमेव नाम भवति॥', 'athāsya nāma karoti vedo ''sīti
tad asyaitad guhyam eva nāma bhavati', null, 6004026),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-27', 27, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 27', 'अथैनं मात्रे प्रदाय स्तनं प्रयच्छति -- यस्ते स्तनः शशयो यो मयोभूर्यो रत्नधा वसुविद्यः सुदत्रः।
येन विश्वा पुष्यसि वार्याणि सरस्वति तमिह धातवे करिति॥', 'athainaṃ mātre pradāya stanaṃ prayacchati -- yas te stanaḥ śaśayo yo mayobhūr yo ratnadhā vasuvid yaḥ sudatraḥ
yena viśvā puṣyasi vāryāṇi sarasvati tam iha dhātave kar iti', null, 6004027),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-28', 28, 'Chapter 6, Section 4, Verse 28', 'अथास्य मातरमभिमन्त्रयते -- इलासि मैत्रावरुणी वीरे वीरमजीजनत्।
सा त्वं वीरवती भव यास्मान्वीरवतोऽकरदिति।
तं वा एतमाहुः -- अतिपिता बताभूः।
अतिपितामहो बताभूः।
परमां बत काष्ठां प्राप श्रिया यशसा ब्रह्मवर्चसेन।
य एवंविदो ब्राह्मणस्य पुत्रो जायत इति॥', 'athāsya mātaram abhimantrayate -- ilāsi maitrāvaruṇī vīre vīram ajījanat
sā tvaṃ vīravatī bhava yāsmān vīravato ''karad iti
taṃ vā etam āhuḥ -- atipitā batābhūḥ
atipitāmaho batābhūḥ
paramāṃ bata kāṣṭhāṃ prāpa śriyā yaśasā brahmavarcasena
ya evaṃvido brāhmaṇasya putro jāyata iti', null, 6004028)
) as x(id, n, reference, sanskrit, transliteration, speaker, sort_key);
insert into verses (id, scripture_id, section_id, number, reference, sanskrit, transliteration, speaker, sort_key, is_featured, source_id, status)
select x.id, 'brihadaranyaka-upanishad', (select id from sections where scripture_id = 'brihadaranyaka-upanishad' and level = 2 and number = 5 and parent_id = (select id from sections where scripture_id = 'brihadaranyaka-upanishad' and level = 1 and number = 6 and parent_id is null)), x.n, x.reference, x.sanskrit, x.transliteration, x.speaker, x.sort_key, false, 'gretil-upanishads', 'draft'
from (values
('brihadaranyaka-6-5-1', 1, 'Chapter 6, Section 5, Verse 1', 'अथ वंशः।
पौतिमाषीपुत्रः कात्यायनीपुत्रात्।
कात्यायनीपुत्रो गौतमीपुत्रात्।
गौतमीपुत्रो भारद्वाजीपुत्रात्।
भारद्वाजीपुत्रः पाराशरीपुत्रात्।
पाराशरीपुत्र औपस्वस्तीपुत्रात्।
औपस्वस्तीपुत्रः पाराशरीपुत्रात्।
पाराशरीपुत्रः कात्यायनीपुत्रात्।
कात्यायनीपुत्रः कौशिकीपुत्रात्।
कौशिकीपुत्र आलम्बीपुत्राच्च वैयाघ्रपदीपुत्राच्च।
वैयाघ्रपदीपुत्रह्काण्वीपुत्राच्च कापीपुत्राच्च।
कापीपुत्रः॥', 'atha vaṃśaḥ
pautimāṣīputraḥ kātyāyanīputrāt
kātyāyanīputro gautamīputrāt
gautamīputro bhāradvājīputrāt
bhāradvājīputraḥ pārāśarīputrāt
pārāśarīputra aupasvastīputrāt
aupasvastīputraḥ pārāśarīputrāt
pārāśarīputraḥ kātyāyanīputrāt
kātyāyanīputraḥ kauśikīputrāt
kauśikīputra ālambīputrāc ca vaiyāghrapadīputrāc ca
vaiyāghrapadīputrah kāṇvīputrāc ca kāpīputrāc ca
kāpīputraḥ', null, 6005001),
('brihadaranyaka-6-5-3', 3, 'Chapter 6, Section 5, Verse 3', 'याज्ञवल्क्यात्।
याज्ञवल्क्य उद्दालकात्।
उद्दालकोऽरुणात्।
अरुण उपवेशेः।
उपवेशिः कुश्रेः।
कुश्रिर्वाजश्रवसः।
वाजश्रवा जीह्वावतो बाध्योगात्।
जीह्वावान्बाध्योगोऽसिताद्वार्षगणात्।
असितो वार्षगणो हरितात्कश्यपात्।
हरितः कश्यपः शिल्पात्कश्यपात्।
शिल्पः कश्यपः कश्यपान्नैध्रुवेः।
कश्यपो नैध्रुविर्वाचः।
वागम्भिण्याः।
अम्भिण्यादित्यात्।
आदित्यानीमानि शुक्लानि यजूंषि वाजसनेयेन याज्ञवल्क्येनाख्ययन्ते॥', 'yājñavalkyāt
yājñavalkya uddālakāt
uddālako ''ruṇāt
aruṇa upaveśeḥ
upaveśiḥ kuśreḥ
kuśrir vājaśravasaḥ
vājaśravā jīhvāvato bādhyogāt
jīhvāvān bādhyogo ''sitād vārṣagaṇāt
asito vārṣagaṇo haritāt kaśyapāt
haritaḥ kaśyapaḥ śilpāt kaśyapāt
śilpaḥ kaśyapaḥ kaśyapān naidhruveḥ
kaśyapo naidhruvir vācaḥ
vāg ambhiṇyāḥ
ambhiṇy ādityāt
ādityānīmāni śuklāni yajūṃṣi vājasaneyena yājñavalkyenākhyayante', null, 6005003),
('brihadaranyaka-6-5-4', 4, 'Chapter 6, Section 5, Verse 4', 'समानमा साञ्जीवीपुत्रात्।
सञ्जिवीपुत्रो माण्डूकायनेः।
माण्डूकायनिर्माण्डव्यात्।
माण्डव्यः कौत्सात्।
कौत्सो माहित्थेः।
माहित्थिर्वामकक्षायणात्।
वामकक्षायणः शाण्डिल्यात्।
शाण्डिल्यो वात्स्यात्।
वात्स्यः कुश्रेः।
कुश्रिर्यज्ञवचसः राजस्तम्बायनात्।
यज्ञवचा राजस्तम्बायनः तुरात्कावषेयात्।
तुरः कावषेयः प्रजापतेः।
प्रजापतिर्ब्रह्मणः।
ब्रह्म स्वयंभु।
ब्रह्मणे नमः॥', 'samānam ā sāñjīvīputrāt
sañjivīputro māṇḍūkāyaneḥ
māṇḍūkāyanir māṇḍavyāt
māṇḍavyaḥ kautsāt
kautso māhittheḥ
māhitthir vāmakakṣāyaṇāt
vāmakakṣāyaṇaḥ śāṇḍilyāt
śāṇḍilyo vātsyāt
vātsyaḥ kuśreḥ
kuśrir yajñavacasaḥ rājastambāyanāt
yajñavacā rājastambāyanaḥ turāt kāvaṣeyāt
turaḥ kāvaṣeyaḥ prajāpateḥ
prajāpatir brahmaṇaḥ
brahma svayaṃbhu
brahmaṇe namaḥ', null, 6005004)
) as x(id, n, reference, sanskrit, transliteration, speaker, sort_key);
insert into translations (verse_id, language, text, source_id, is_primary) values
('brihadaranyaka-1-1-1', 'en', 'The dawn is the head of the sacrificial horse, the sun its eye, the wind its breath, the universal fire its open mouth, the year its body. Heaven is its back, the mid-air its belly, the earth its hoof-rest, the directions its sides, the in-between directions its ribs, the seasons its limbs, the months and half-months its joints, days and nights its feet, the stars its bones, the clouds its flesh. Sand is the food in its stomach, rivers its intestines, mountains its liver and lungs, plants and trees its hair. The rising sun is its front half, the setting sun its back half. When it yawns, lightning flashes; when it shakes, it thunders; when it urinates, it rains. Speech is its neigh.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-1-2', 'en', 'The day was born as the glory in front of the horse; its source is the eastern sea. The night was born as the glory behind it; its source is the western sea. These two glories came to be on either side of the horse. As ''haya'' it carried the gods, as ''vajin'' the gandharvas, as ''arvan'' the demons, as ''ashva'' humans. The sea is its kin, the sea its source.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-2-1', 'en', 'In the beginning there was nothing here at all. This was covered by death, by hunger, for hunger is death. Death thought, ''Let me have a self.'' It moved about worshipping. From its worship water was born. ''While I worshipped, water (ka) came to me,'' it thought. That is why fire is called arka. Water comes to one who knows this.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-2-2', 'en', 'Water is arka. The froth on the water hardened and became the earth. Death toiled on it, and from its toil and heat its essence of light came out as fire.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-2-3', 'en', 'It divided itself three ways: fire as a third, the sun as a third, wind as a third. This is breath, divided three ways. The east is its head; the north-east and south-east its arms; the west its tail; the north-west and south-west its thighs; south and north its sides; heaven its back; the mid-air its belly; this earth its chest. It rests on water. One who knows this has firm footing wherever he goes.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-2-4', 'en', 'It wished, ''Let a second self be born to me.'' Death, hunger, united speech with mind. The seed became the year; before that there was no year. It carried him for as long as a year, then gave birth. When he was born, death opened its mouth to eat him. He cried ''bhan!'' That became speech.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-2-5', 'en', 'He thought, ''If I kill him, I will have less food.'' With that speech and that self he created everything here: Rik verses, Yajus formulas, Saman chants, metres, sacrifices, people and animals. Whatever he created he began to eat. Because he eats (atti) everything, Aditi is called Aditi. One who knows this becomes the eater of everything, and everything becomes his food.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-2-6', 'en', 'He wished, ''Let me sacrifice again with a greater sacrifice.'' He toiled and practised austerity. From him, toiling and heated, glory and vigour went out. Glory and vigour are the life-breaths. When the breaths had left, his body began to swell. But his mind was still in his body.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-2-7', 'en', 'He wished, ''Let this body be fit for sacrifice; let me have a self through it.'' So it became a horse (ashva). Because it swelled (ashvat), it became fit for sacrifice (medhya); that is why the horse sacrifice is called ashvamedha. One who knows it so truly knows the horse sacrifice. He let it roam free and after a year sacrificed it to himself, giving the other animals to the gods. That one who shines is the horse sacrifice; the year is its body. This fire is arka; these worlds are its body. Fire and horse sacrifice are one deity, death. One who knows this conquers repeated death; death does not reach him, death becomes his self, he lives a full life and becomes one with these deities.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-1', 'en', 'There were two kinds of Prajapati''s children, gods and demons. The gods were the younger, the demons the elder. They competed for these worlds. The gods said, ''Let us overcome the demons through the udgitha chant at the sacrifice.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-2', 'en', 'Then they said to speech, ''Sing the chant for us.'' ''Very well,'' and speech sang for them. The enjoyment in speech it sang for the gods; the good says it kept for itself. The demons knew, ''Through this singer they will overcome us.'' They rushed at it and pierced it with evil. That evil is the wrong things one says.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-3', 'en', 'Then they said to the breath of smell, ''Sing the chant for us.'' ''Very well,'' and the breath of smell sang for them. The enjoyment in the breath of smell it sang for the gods; the good smells it kept for itself. The demons knew, ''Through this singer they will overcome us.'' They rushed at it and pierced it with evil. That evil is the wrong things one smells.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-4', 'en', 'Then they said to the eye, ''Sing the chant for us.'' ''Very well,'' and the eye sang for them. The enjoyment in the eye it sang for the gods; the good sees it kept for itself. The demons knew, ''Through this singer they will overcome us.'' They rushed at it and pierced it with evil. That evil is the wrong things one sees.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-5', 'en', 'Then they said to the ear, ''Sing the chant for us.'' ''Very well,'' and the ear sang for them. The enjoyment in the ear it sang for the gods; the good hears it kept for itself. The demons knew, ''Through this singer they will overcome us.'' They rushed at it and pierced it with evil. That evil is the wrong things one hears.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-6', 'en', 'Then they said to the mind, ''Sing the chant for us.'' ''Very well,'' and the mind sang for them. The enjoyment in the mind it sang for the gods; the good it imagines it kept for itself. The demons pierced it with evil; that evil is the wrong things one imagines. So they struck these deities with evils and pierced them with evil.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-7', 'en', 'Then they said to the breath in the mouth, ''Sing the chant for us.'' ''Very well,'' and this breath sang for them. The demons knew, ''Through this singer they will overcome us.'' They rushed at it, wanting to pierce it with evil. But as a clod of earth striking a rock is shattered, so they were shattered and scattered in all directions and perished. So the gods prospered and the demons were defeated. One who knows this prospers, and his hateful rival is defeated.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-8', 'en', 'They said, ''Where was the one who stood by us like this?'' ''He is here within the mouth (ayam asye).'' He is Ayasya Angirasa, for he is the essence (rasa) of the limbs (anga).', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-9', 'en', 'This deity is called Dur, for death is far (dura) from it. Death is far from one who knows this.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-10', 'en', 'This deity took away the evil, the death, of these deities and sent it to where the directions end, and left their evils there. So one should not go to people there or to that end, lest one meet evil, death.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-11', 'en', 'Having taken away the evil, the death, of these deities, this deity carried them beyond death.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-12', 'en', 'Then it carried speech first. When speech across. When speech first. When speech was freed from death, it became fire. That fire, having passed beyond death, shines.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-13', 'en', 'Then it carried breath (smell) across. When breath (smell) was freed from death, it became wind. That wind, having passed beyond death, blows.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-14', 'en', 'Then it carried the eye across. When the eye was freed from death, it became the sun. That the sun, having passed beyond death, burns.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-15', 'en', 'Then it carried the ear across. When the ear was freed from death, it became the directions. That the directions, having passed beyond death, stretch out.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-16', 'en', 'Then it carried the mind across. When the mind was freed from death, it became the moon. That moon, having passed beyond death, shines. So this deity carries beyond death one who knows this.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-17', 'en', 'Then it sang food for itself, for whatever food is eaten is eaten by breath alone, and it rests here.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-18', 'en', 'The gods said, ''All this is food, and you have sung it for yourself. Give us a share in this food.'' ''Then enter into me.'' ''Very well,'' and they settled all around it. So whatever food one eats with breath satisfies them. One who knows this becomes the support of his people, their best and their leader, an eater of food and a ruler. Whoever tries to rival such a knower among his own people is not able to support his dependants; but whoever follows him is able to support them.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-19', 'en', 'He is Ayasya Angirasa, for he is the essence of the limbs. Breath is the essence of the limbs. Yes, breath is the essence of the limbs. That is why whatever limb breath leaves dries up, for it is the essence of the limbs.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-20', 'en', 'He is also Brihaspati. Speech is brihati, and he is its lord (pati); so he is Brihaspati.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-21', 'en', 'He is also Brahmanaspati. Speech is brahman, and he is its lord; so he is Brahmanaspati.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-22', 'en', 'He is also the Saman. Speech is ''sa'' and he is ''ama''; that is why the Saman is called Saman. Or because he is equal (sama) to a gnat, equal to a mosquito, equal to an elephant, equal to these three worlds, equal to all this, he is the Saman. One who knows this Saman gains union with it and lives in its world.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-23', 'en', 'He is also the udgitha. Breath is ''ut'', for by breath all this is held up (uttabdha); speech is ''githa'', song. ''Ut'' and ''githa'' together make udgitha.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-24', 'en', 'So Brahmadatta Chaikitaneya, while drinking Soma, said, ''May this Soma make my head fall off if Ayasya Angirasa sang the chant with anything other than this; for he sang only with speech and breath.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-25', 'en', 'One who knows the wealth of this Saman gains wealth. Its wealth is tone. So one who is about to perform priestly duties should wish for a good tone in his voice, and perform them with a voice rich in tone. That is why people want to see a priest with a good voice at a sacrifice, like one who has wealth. One who knows this wealth of the Saman gains wealth.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-26', 'en', 'One who knows the gold of this Saman gains gold. Its gold is tone. One who knows this gold of the Saman gains gold.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-27', 'en', 'One who knows the foundation of this Saman stands firm. Its foundation is speech, for it is in speech that this breath is sung, firmly established. Some say it is food.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-1', 'en', 'In the beginning this was the Self alone, in the shape of a person. Looking around, he saw nothing but himself. He first said, ''I am.'' So the word ''I'' came about. That is why even now, when someone is called, he first says ''It is I,'' and then gives whatever other name he has. Because before all this he burned up (aushat) all evils, he is called purusha. One who knows this burns up whoever wants to come before him.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-2', 'en', 'He was afraid; so a person alone is afraid. Then he thought, ''Since there is nothing but me, what am I afraid of?'' Then his fear went away. What was there to fear? Fear comes only from a second.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-3', 'en', 'He had no joy; so a person alone has no joy. He wished for a second. He became as large as a man and woman in close embrace. He split himself in two; from that came husband and wife. So, as Yajnavalkya said, each of us is like half of a split pea. So this space is filled by a wife. He united with her, and from that humans were born.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-4', 'en', 'She thought, ''How can he unite with me after producing me from himself? Let me hide.'' She became a cow, he a bull; they united and cattle were born. She became a mare, he a stallion; she a female donkey, he a male donkey; they united and one-hoofed animals were born. She became a she-goat, he a he-goat; she a ewe, he a ram; they united and goats and sheep were born. So he created every pair there is, down to the ants.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-5', 'en', 'He knew, ''I am creation, for I created all this.'' So he became creation. One who knows this lives in his creation.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-6', 'en', 'Then he churned like this, and from his mouth, as from a womb, and with his hands, he created fire. So both of these are hairless on the inside. When people say ''Worship this god, worship that god'', each god is his creation, for he is all the gods. Whatever is moist he created from semen; that is Soma. All this is just food and eater: Soma is food, fire is eater. This is Brahman''s higher creation, for he created gods better than himself, and though mortal he created immortals.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-7', 'en', 'At that time all this was undifferentiated. It became differentiated by name and form: ''He has this name and this form.'' Even now it is differentiated by name and form. He entered into it up to the tips of the nails, like a razor in its case or fire in firewood. People do not see him, for he is incomplete as seen in parts. Breathing, he is called breath; speaking, speech; seeing, eye; hearing, ear; thinking, mind. These are only names for his actions. Whoever worships one of these does not know, for he is incomplete in each. One should worship him as the Self, for in it all these become one. The Self is the trace of all this, for by it one knows all this, as one finds an animal by its footprints. One who knows this finds fame and praise.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-8', 'en', 'This Self is dearer than a son, dearer than wealth, dearer than anything else, for it is innermost. If someone says something else is dearer than the Self, one may answer, ''What you hold dear will die,'' and so it will. One should worship the Self alone as dear. Whoever worships the Self alone as dear, what is dear to him does not perish.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-9', 'en', 'People say: ''Since people think they will become everything through knowledge of Brahman, what did Brahman know by which it became everything?''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-10', 'en', 'In the beginning this was Brahman. It knew itself alone: ''I am Brahman.'' So it became everything. Whoever among the gods awoke to this became it; so too among the sages and among humans. Seeing this, the sage Vamadeva declared, ''I was Manu, and the sun.'' Even now, whoever knows ''I am Brahman'' becomes all this. Even the gods cannot stop him, for he becomes their Self. But whoever worships another deity, thinking ''He is one and I am another'', does not know. He is like a domestic animal for the gods. As many animals serve a man, so each person serves the gods. If even one animal is taken away it is unpleasant, how much more if many! So the gods do not like people to know this.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-11', 'en', 'In the beginning this was Brahman, one only. Being one, it did not flourish. It created a higher form, the ruling power: those who rule among the gods, Indra, Varuna, Soma, Rudra, Parjanya, Yama, Mrityu and Ishana. So there is nothing higher than the ruling power; at the royal consecration the priest sits below the king. Yet the priestly power is the source of the ruling power. So even if a king rises to the top, in the end he relies on the priestly power as his source. Whoever harms it harms his own source and becomes worse, as one who harms someone greater.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-12', 'en', 'Still it did not flourish. It created the common people, the gods who are named in groups: the Vasus, the Rudras, the Adityas, the All-gods and the Maruts.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-13', 'en', 'Still it did not flourish. It created the Shudra class, Pushan. This earth is Pushan, for she nourishes everything there is.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-14', 'en', 'Still it did not flourish. It created a higher form, dharma, justice. Dharma is the power over the ruling power; so there is nothing higher than dharma. Through dharma even a weak man can prevail over a strong one, as with the help of a king. Dharma is truth. So when someone speaks the truth, people say ''He speaks dharma'', and when someone speaks dharma they say ''He speaks the truth''. Both are the same.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-15', 'en', 'So there are the priestly, ruling, common and Shudra classes. Among the gods Brahman became fire; among humans, the priest; and through the others, the others. So people seek a world among the gods through fire and among humans through the priest. But whoever leaves this world without seeing his own world, the Self, is not served by it, just as the Veda unrecited or a work undone. Even if someone who does not know this does a great holy work, it will finally run out. One should worship the Self alone as one''s world. Whoever does so, his work never runs out, for from this Self he creates whatever he desires.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-16', 'en', 'This Self is a world for all beings. When he offers and sacrifices, he is a world for the gods. When he recites the Veda, for the sages. When he offers to the ancestors and wishes for children, for the ancestors. When he gives shelter and food to people, for humans. When he finds grass and water for animals, for animals. When beasts and birds, down to ants, live off his house, he is a world for them. As one wishes safety for one''s own world, so all beings wish safety for one who knows this. This has been known and examined.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-17', 'en', 'In the beginning this was the Self alone. He wished, ''Let me have a wife, then children; let me have wealth, then do works.'' That is the extent of desire; even if one wished, one would not find more. So even now a single person wishes, ''Let me have a wife, children, wealth and works.'' Until he gets each one, he feels incomplete. His completeness is this: the mind is his self, speech his wife, breath his children, the eye his human wealth, for by the eye one finds it; the ear his divine wealth, for by it one hears; the body his work, for by it one works. This is the fivefold sacrifice; fivefold is the animal, fivefold the person, fivefold all this. Whoever knows this gains all this.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-1', 'en', '''When the father produced the seven foods by wisdom and austerity, one was common to all, two he gave the gods, three he made for himself, one he gave to the animals. On it rests everything, what breathes and what does not. Why do they not run out, though always eaten? Whoever knows their imperishability eats food with his mouth, goes to the gods and lives on strength.'' These are the verses.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-2', 'en', '''One was common to all'': this is the food that is eaten. Whoever worships it alone is not free from wrong, for it is mixed. ''Two he gave the gods'': the offering and the oblation; so people offer to the gods. Some say they are the new and full moon rites; so one should not sacrifice only for wishes. ''One he gave to animals'': that is milk, for people and animals first live on milk. So a newborn is first given ghee or the breast, and a calf is called ''not yet a grass-eater''. ''On it rests everything'': all rests on milk. Some say one who offers milk for a year conquers repeated death; do not think so: one who knows this conquers it the very day he offers. ''Why do they not run out?'': the person is their imperishability, for he produces food again and again by thought and work. ''He eats food with his mouth'': ''pratika'' means mouth. ''He goes to the gods and lives on strength'' is praise.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-3', 'en', '''Three he made for himself'': mind, speech and breath. People say, ''My mind was elsewhere, I did not see; my mind was elsewhere, I did not hear,'' for one sees and hears with the mind. Desire, will, doubt, faith, lack of faith, steadiness, unsteadiness, shame, understanding and fear are all mind. So even when touched from behind one knows it by the mind. Whatever sound there is, that is speech, for it reaches an end, but it is not an end. In-breath, out-breath, diffused breath, upward breath, balancing breath: all these are breath. The Self is made of these: of speech, mind and breath.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-4', 'en', 'These three are the three worlds: speech is this world, mind is the mid-air, breath is that world (heaven).', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-5', 'en', 'These three are the three Vedas: speech is the Rig Veda, mind the Yajur Veda, breath the Sama Veda.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-6', 'en', 'These three are gods, ancestors and humans: speech is the gods, mind the ancestors, breath humans.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-7', 'en', 'These three are father, mother and child: mind is the father, speech the mother, breath the child.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-8', 'en', 'Whatever is known is a form of speech, for speech is known. Speech protects him by becoming that.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-9', 'en', 'Whatever is still to be known is a form of mind, for mind is still to be known. Mind protects him by becoming that.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-10', 'en', 'Whatever is unknown is a form of breath, for breath is unknown. Breath protects him by becoming that.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-11', 'en', 'Earth is the body of speech; this fire is its form of light. As far as speech extends, so far extends the earth, so far this fire.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-12', 'en', 'Heaven is the body of the mind; that sun is its form of light. As far as the mind extends, so far heaven, so far the sun. These two united, and from them breath was born. That is Indra. He has no rival, for a rival is a second. One who knows this has no rival.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-13', 'en', 'Water is the body of breath; that moon is its form of light. As far as breath extends, so far water, so far the moon. All these are equal, all are endless. Whoever worships them as finite wins a finite world; whoever worships them as endless wins an endless world.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-14', 'en', 'This Prajapati is the year, with sixteen parts. His nights are fifteen parts; the sixteenth is constant. By the nights he grows and wanes. On the new-moon night he enters with that sixteenth part into everything that breathes, and is born again the next morning. So on that night one should not take the life of any creature, not even a lizard, in honour of that deity.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-15', 'en', 'The Prajapati who is the year with sixteen parts is a person who knows this. His wealth is fifteen parts; his Self is the sixteenth. By wealth he grows and wanes. The Self is the hub, wealth the rim. So even if he loses everything, if he lives with his Self, people say he has only lost his rim.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-16', 'en', 'There are three worlds: the world of humans, the world of ancestors and the world of gods. The human world is won only through a son, not by any other work; the ancestors'' world by works; the gods'' world by knowledge. The gods'' world is the best of worlds, so knowledge is praised.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-17', 'en', 'Now the handing over. When a man thinks he is about to die, he says to his son, ''You are Brahman; you are the sacrifice; you are the world.'' The son replies, ''I am Brahman; I am the sacrifice; I am the world.'' All that has been learned is one in ''Brahman''; all sacrifices are one in ''sacrifice''; all worlds are one in ''world''. ''This is all. Being all this, may he support me from this world.'' So a son who has been taught is said to lead to the world, and so they teach him. When one who knows this leaves this world, he enters his son with his breaths. If he has done anything wrong, the son frees him from it all; hence the name ''son'' (putra). Through his son he stands firm in this world, and the divine, immortal breaths enter him.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-18', 'en', 'Divine speech from earth and fire enters him. That is divine speech, by which whatever one says comes true.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-19', 'en', 'Divine mind from heaven and the sun enters him. That is divine mind, by which one is only joyful and never sorrows.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-20', 'en', 'Divine breath from water and the moon enters him. That is divine breath, which, moving or not moving, is never troubled or harmed. One who knows this becomes the Self of all beings. As that deity is, so is he. As all beings care for that deity, so all beings care for one who knows this. Whatever sorrow these creatures have stays with them; only good goes to him. Evil never goes to the gods.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-21', 'en', 'Now a reflection on vows. Prajapati created the functions. Once created, they competed with one another. Speech vowed, ''I will speak''; the eye, ''I will see''; the ear, ''I will hear''; and so the other functions. Death, as weariness, seized them and held them back. So speech grows weary, the eye grows weary, the ear grows weary. But death did not seize the breath in the middle. They wanted to know it: ''This is the best of us, which moving or not moving is never troubled or harmed. Let us all take its form.'' So they all took its form, and are called ''breaths''. The family in which one who knows this is born is named after him. Whoever competes with one who knows this withers and dies in the end. So much about the self.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-22', 'en', 'Now about the gods. Fire vowed, ''I will burn''; the sun, ''I will give heat''; the moon, ''I will shine''; and so the other gods. As breath is among the breaths, so wind is among these gods. The other gods set, but not the wind. The wind is the deity that never sets.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-23', 'en', 'There is this verse: ''From where the sun rises and where it sets.'' It rises from breath and sets in breath. ''The gods made it their law; it is the same today and tomorrow.'' What they kept then, they keep today. So one should keep one vow: breathe in and breathe out, thinking, ''May evil, death, not reach me.'' If one keeps it, one should keep it to the end. By it one wins union with that deity and a place in its world.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-6-1', 'en', 'This world is threefold: name, form and action. For names, speech is their source, for from it all names arise. It is their Saman, for it is equal to all names. It is their Brahman, for it supports all names.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-6-2', 'en', 'For forms, the eye is their source, for from it all forms arise. It is their Saman, for it is equal to all forms. It is their Brahman, for it supports all forms.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-1-6-3', 'en', 'For actions, the body is their source, for from it all actions arise. It is their Saman, for it is equal to all actions. It is their Brahman, for it supports all actions. These three are one, the Self; the Self, being one, is these three. This is the immortal covered by the real. Breath is the immortal; name and form are the real. This breath is covered by them.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-1', 'en', 'There was Dripta Balaki, a learned man of the Gargya clan. He said to Ajatashatru, king of Kashi, ''Let me tell you about Brahman.'' Ajatashatru said, ''I will give a thousand cows for such a talk! People run around saying, "Janaka, Janaka!"''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-2', 'en', 'Gargya said, ''The person in the sun: that is what I worship as Brahman.'' Ajatashatru said, ''Do not speak to me about that! I worship it as the one who stands above all beings, their head and king.'' Whoever worships it so becomes the one above all beings, their head and king.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-3', 'en', 'Gargya said, ''The person in the moon: that is what I worship as Brahman.'' Ajatashatru said, ''Do not speak to me about that! I worship it as great King Soma clothed in white.'' Whoever worships it so has Soma pressed for him every day, and his food never fails.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-4', 'en', 'Gargya said, ''The person in lightning: that is what I worship as Brahman.'' Ajatashatru said, ''Do not speak to me about that! I worship it as the radiant one.'' Whoever worships it so becomes radiant, and his children are radiant.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-5', 'en', 'Gargya said, ''The person in space: that is what I worship as Brahman.'' Ajatashatru said, ''Do not speak to me about that! I worship it as the full and unmoving.'' Whoever worships it so is filled with children and cattle, and his line never dies out.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-6', 'en', 'Gargya said, ''The person in the wind: that is what I worship as Brahman.'' Ajatashatru said, ''Do not speak to me about that! I worship it as Indra Vaikuntha, the unconquered army.'' Whoever worships it so becomes victorious, unconquerable, conquering his enemies.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-7', 'en', 'Gargya said, ''The person in fire: that is what I worship as Brahman.'' Ajatashatru said, ''Do not speak to me about that! I worship it as the all-conquering.'' Whoever worships it so becomes all-conquering, and so do his children.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-8', 'en', 'Gargya said, ''The person in water: that is what I worship as Brahman.'' Ajatashatru said, ''Do not speak to me about that! I worship it as the likeness.'' Whoever worships it so finds what is fitting coming to him, not what is unfitting, and fitting children are born to him.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-9', 'en', 'Gargya said, ''The person in a mirror: that is what I worship as Brahman.'' Ajatashatru said, ''Do not speak to me about that! I worship it as the shining one.'' Whoever worships it so becomes shining, as do his children, and outshines all he meets.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-10', 'en', 'Gargya said, ''The person in the sound that follows a person as he walks: that is what I worship as Brahman.'' Ajatashatru said, ''Do not speak to me about that! I worship it as life.'' Whoever worships it so lives his full span in this world, and his breath does not leave him before his time.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-11', 'en', 'Gargya said, ''The person in the directions: that is what I worship as Brahman.'' Ajatashatru said, ''Do not speak to me about that! I worship it as the inseparable companion.'' Whoever worships it so has companions, and his group is never broken.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-12', 'en', 'Gargya said, ''The person in the shadow: that is what I worship as Brahman.'' Ajatashatru said, ''Do not speak to me about that! I worship it as death.'' Whoever worships it so lives his full span, and death does not come to him before his time.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-13', 'en', 'Gargya said, ''The person in the body: that is what I worship as Brahman.'' Ajatashatru said, ''Do not speak to me about that! I worship it as the one who has a self.'' Whoever worships it so becomes self-possessed, and so do his children. Then Gargya fell silent.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-14', 'en', 'Ajatashatru said, ''Is that all?'' ''That is all.'' ''One does not know Brahman by that much.'' Gargya said, ''Let me come to you as a student.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-15', 'en', 'Ajatashatru said, ''It is against the natural order for a brahmin to come to a king to learn about Brahman. But I will make you understand.'' Taking him by the hand he got up. They came to a sleeping man. He called to him by these names: ''Great one, clothed in white, King Soma!'' The man did not get up. He woke him by pushing him with his hand, and he got up.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-16', 'en', 'Ajatashatru said, ''When this man was asleep, where was this person made of understanding? Where did he come back from?'' Gargya did not know.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-17', 'en', 'Ajatashatru said, ''When this man was asleep, this person made of understanding gathered the understanding of the senses by his understanding and lay in the space within the heart. When he takes them in, the person is said to be asleep. Then breath is held, speech is held, the eye is held, the ear is held, the mind is held.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-18', 'en', 'When he moves about in dreams, those are his worlds. He becomes like a great king or a great brahmin; he goes up and down. As a great king takes his people and moves about his own land as he pleases, so he takes his senses and moves about his own body as he pleases.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-19', 'en', 'When he is fast asleep and knows nothing at all, he slips through the seventy-two thousand channels called hita that go from the heart to the pericardium, and lies there. As a young man, a great king or a great brahmin might lie at the height of bliss, so he lies.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-20', 'en', 'As a spider moves up its thread, and as tiny sparks fly out from a fire, so from this Self come out all breaths, all worlds, all gods, all beings. Its secret name is ''the real behind the real''. The breaths are real, and it is their reality.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-2-1', 'en', 'Whoever knows the young one with its house, its covering, its post and its rope keeps off seven hateful rivals. The young one is the breath in the middle. This body is its house, the head its covering, breath its post, food its rope.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-2-2', 'en', 'Seven imperishable powers serve it. Through the red lines in the eye, Rudra serves it; through the water in the eye, the rain-god; through the pupil, the sun; through the dark of the eye, fire; through the white, Indra; through the lower eyelash, the earth; through the upper, heaven. One who knows this never lacks food.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-2-3', 'en', 'There is this verse: ''There is a bowl with its mouth below and its bottom above. In it is placed glory of every form. On its rim sit seven sages; speech is the eighth, joined with prayer.'' The bowl with its mouth below and its bottom above is the head. The glory of every form placed in it is the breaths. The seven sages on its rim are the breaths. Speech is the eighth, joined with prayer.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-2-4', 'en', 'These two (ears) are Gotama and Bharadvaja; this is Gotama, this Bharadvaja. These two (eyes) are Vishvamitra and Jamadagni. These two (nostrils) are Vasishtha and Kashyapa. Speech is Atri, for food is eaten with speech; Atri is really ''atti'', the eater. One who knows this eats everything, and everything becomes his food.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-3-1', 'en', 'Brahman has two forms: the formed and the formless, the mortal and the immortal, the still and the moving, the being and the beyond.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-3-2', 'en', 'The formed is everything other than wind and mid-air. It is mortal, still and being. The essence of this formed, mortal, still being is the sun that burns, for it is the essence of being.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-3-3', 'en', 'The formless is wind and mid-air. It is immortal, moving and beyond. Its essence is the person in the sun''s orb, for that is the essence of the beyond. So much about the gods.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-3-4', 'en', 'Now about the self. The formed is everything other than breath and the space within the self. It is mortal, still and being. Its essence is the eye, for it is the essence of being.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-3-5', 'en', 'The formless is breath and the space within the self. It is immortal, moving and beyond. Its essence is the person in the right eye, for it is the essence of the beyond.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-3-6', 'en', 'The form of this person is like a saffron-coloured cloth, like white wool, like a red beetle, like a flame of fire, like a white lotus, like a sudden flash of lightning. The glory of one who knows this flashes like lightning. Now the teaching: ''Not this, not this.'' For there is nothing higher than this ''not this''. Its name is ''the real behind the real''. The breaths are real, and it is their reality.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-1', 'en', '''Maitreyi,'' said Yajnavalkya, ''I am about to leave this life as a householder. Let me make a settlement between you and Katyayani.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-2', 'en', 'Maitreyi said, ''Sir, if this whole earth full of wealth were mine, would I become immortal by it?'' ''No,'' said Yajnavalkya. ''Your life would be like the life of the rich. There is no hope of immortality through wealth.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-3', 'en', 'Maitreyi said, ''What would I do with something that cannot make me immortal? Tell me, sir, what you know.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-4', 'en', 'Yajnavalkya said, ''You have always been dear to me, and now you speak what is dear. Come, sit; I will explain. As I explain, reflect on it deeply.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-5', 'en', 'He said: ''It is not for love of the husband that the husband is dear, but for love of the Self. It is not for love of the wife that the wife is dear, but for love of the Self. Not for love of children, wealth, the priestly or ruling class, the worlds, the gods, beings, or of all, are they dear, but for love of the Self. It is the Self that should be seen, heard, reflected on and meditated upon, Maitreyi. When the Self is seen, heard, reflected on and understood, all this is known.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-6', 'en', '''The priestly class abandons one who thinks it is other than the Self. The ruling class, the worlds, the gods, beings, everything abandon one who thinks they are other than the Self. This priestly class, this ruling class, these worlds, these gods, these beings, all this is the Self.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-7', 'en', 'As when a drum is played, one cannot catch its sounds separately, but by catching the drum or its player the sound is caught,', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-8', 'en', 'As when a conch is played, one cannot catch its sounds separately, but by catching the conch or its player the sound is caught,', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-9', 'en', 'As when a lute is played, one cannot catch its sounds separately, but by catching the lute or its player the sound is caught,', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-10', 'en', '''As smoke pours out separately from a fire lit with damp wood, so the Rig Veda, Yajur Veda, Sama Veda, Atharvangirasa, histories, ancient lore, sciences, Upanishads, verses, sutras, explanations and commentaries are the breath of this great Being. All these are its breath.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-11', 'en', '''As the ocean is the meeting place of all waters, so the skin is of all touches, the nose of all smells, the tongue of all tastes, the eye of all forms, the ear of all sounds, the mind of all intentions, the heart of all knowledge, the hands of all actions, the sexual organ of all pleasures, the anus of all excretions, the feet of all journeys, and speech of all the Vedas.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-12', 'en', '''As a lump of salt thrown into water dissolves and cannot be picked out, yet wherever you taste, it is salty, so this great Being, endless and boundless, is a mass of awareness. It rises from these elements and disappears back into them. After death there is no awareness. This is what I say,'' said Yajnavalkya.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-13', 'en', 'Maitreyi said, ''Here, sir, you have confused me by saying that after death there is no awareness.'' He said, ''I am not saying anything confusing. This is enough for understanding.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-14', 'en', '''For where there is duality, as it were, one smells another, one sees another, one hears another, one speaks to another, one thinks of another, one knows another. But where everything has become one''s own Self, then by what and whom should one smell, see, hear, speak to, think of, or know? By what can one know that by which one knows all this? By what, my dear, can one know the knower?''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-1', 'en', 'This earth is honey for all beings, and all beings are honey for it. The shining, immortal person in this earth and the shining, immortal person in the body in oneself are one: this Self. This is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-2', 'en', 'These waters is honey for all beings, and all beings are honey for these waters. The shining, immortal person in these waters and the shining, immortal person in the semen in oneself are one: this Self. This is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-3', 'en', 'This fire is honey for all beings, and all beings are honey for it. The shining, immortal person in this fire and the shining, immortal person made of speech in oneself are one: this Self. This is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-4', 'en', 'This wind is honey for all beings, and all beings are honey for it. The shining, immortal person in this wind and the shining, immortal person that is breath in oneself are one: this Self. This is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-5', 'en', 'This sun is honey for all beings, and all beings are honey for it. The shining, immortal person in this sun and the shining, immortal person in the eye are one: this Self. This is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-6', 'en', 'These directions is honey for all beings, and all beings are honey for it. The shining, immortal person in these directions and the shining, immortal person in the ear and in the echo are one: this Self. This is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-7', 'en', 'This moon is honey for all beings, and all beings are honey for it. The shining, immortal person in this moon and the shining, immortal person in the mind are one: this Self. This is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-8', 'en', 'This lightning is honey for all beings, and all beings are honey for it. The shining, immortal person in this lightning and the shining, immortal person in one''s own radiance are one: this Self. This is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-9', 'en', 'This thunder is honey for all beings, and all beings are honey for it. The shining, immortal person in this thunder and the shining, immortal person in sound and tone are one: this Self. This is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-10', 'en', 'This space is honey for all beings, and all beings are honey for it. The shining, immortal person in this space and the shining, immortal person in the space of the heart are one: this Self. This is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-11', 'en', 'This dharma is honey for all beings, and all beings are honey for it. The shining, immortal person in this dharma and the shining, immortal person in one''s own sense of right are one: this Self. This is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-12', 'en', 'This truth is honey for all beings, and all beings are honey for it. The shining, immortal person in this truth and the shining, immortal person in one''s own truthfulness are one: this Self. This is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-13', 'en', 'This humanity is honey for all beings, and all beings are honey for it. The shining, immortal person in this humanity and the shining, immortal person in oneself as a human being are one: this Self. This is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-14', 'en', 'This Self is honey for all beings, and all beings are honey for this Self. The shining, immortal person in this Self and the shining, immortal Self itself are one: this Self. This is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-15', 'en', 'This Self is the ruler of all, the king of all beings. As the spokes are fixed in the hub and rim of a wheel, so in this Self all beings, all gods, all worlds, all breaths, all these selves are fixed.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-16', 'en', 'This is the honey that Dadhyanch Atharvana taught the two Ashvins. Seeing this, the sage said: ''O heroes, I reveal your mighty deed for gain, as thunder reveals the rain: the honey that Dadhyanch Atharvana told you through a horse''s head.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-17', 'en', 'This is the honey that Dadhyanch Atharvana taught the two Ashvins. Seeing this, the sage said: ''O Ashvins, you put a horse''s head on Dadhyanch Atharvana. Keeping his word, he told you the honey of Tvashtri, your secret, O wonder-workers.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-18', 'en', 'This is the honey that Dadhyanch Atharvana taught the Ashvins. Seeing this, the sage said: ''He made bodies with two feet, he made bodies with four. Having first become a bird, the Person entered the bodies.'' This Person dwells in all bodies (puri-shaya). There is nothing not covered by him, nothing not filled by him.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-19', 'en', 'This is the honey that Dadhyanch Atharvana taught the Ashvins. Seeing this, the sage said: ''He took the form of every form; that form is his to be seen. Indra moves in many forms by his powers; his horses are yoked, a hundred and ten.'' He is the horses; he is the tens and thousands, many and endless. This Brahman has no before and no after, no inside and no outside. This Self is Brahman, experiencing all. This is the teaching.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-6-1', 'en', 'Now the line of teachers. Pautimashya received it from Gaupavana; Gaupavana from Paushtimashya; Paushtimashya from Gaupavana; Gaupavana from Kaushika; Kaushika from Kaundinya; Kaundinya from Shandilya; Shandilya from Kaushika and Gautama; Gautama', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-6-2', 'en', 'from Agniveshya; Agniveshya from Shandilya and Anabhimlata; Anabhimlata from Anabhimlata; Anabhimlata from Anabhimlata; Anabhimlata from Gautama; Gautama from Saitava and Prachinayogya; they from Parasharya; Parasharya from Bharadvaja; Bharadvaja from Bharadvaja and Gautama; Gautama from Bharadvaja; Bharadvaja from Parasharya; Parasharya from Vaijavapayana; Vaijavapayana from Kaushikayani; Kaushikayani', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-2-6-3', 'en', 'from Ghritakaushika; ... Atharvan Daiva from Mrityu Pradhvamsana; ... Parameshthin from Brahman. Brahman is self-existent. Homage to Brahman.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-1', 'en', 'Janaka, king of Videha, held a sacrifice with rich gifts. Brahmins from the Kuru and Panchala lands gathered there. Janaka wanted to know which of them was the most learned. He set apart a thousand cows, with ten gold coins tied to the horns of each.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-2', 'en', 'He said, ''Honoured brahmins, let the one who is the best knower of Brahman drive these cows away.'' The brahmins did not dare. Then Yajnavalkya said to his own student, ''Samashravas, dear, drive them away.'' He drove them away. The brahmins were angry: ''How can he call himself the best knower of Brahman among us?'' Janaka''s hotri priest Ashvala asked him, ''Yajnavalkya, are you really the best knower of Brahman among us?'' He replied, ''We bow to the best knower of Brahman. We just want the cows!'' Then Ashvala decided to question him.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-3', 'en', '''Yajnavalkya,'' he said, ''since all this is reached by death, overcome by death, by what means does the sacrificer free himself from death''s reach?'' ''By the hotri priest, by fire, by speech. Speech is the hotri of the sacrifice. This speech is fire, the hotri; that is freedom, that is complete freedom.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-4', 'en', '''Yajnavalkya, since all this is reached by day and night, by what means does the sacrificer free himself from them?'' ''By the adhvaryu priest, by the eye, by the sun. The eye is the adhvaryu of the sacrifice. This eye is the sun, the adhvaryu; that is freedom, that is complete freedom.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-5', 'en', '''Yajnavalkya, since all this is reached by the bright and dark halves of the month, by what means does the sacrificer free himself from them?'' ''By the udgatri priest, by wind, by breath. Breath is the udgatri of the sacrifice. This breath is wind, the udgatri; that is freedom, that is complete freedom.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-6', 'en', '''Yajnavalkya, since the mid-air seems to have no support, by what ladder does the sacrificer climb to heaven?'' ''By the brahman priest, by the mind, by the moon. The mind is the brahman of the sacrifice. This mind is the moon, the brahman; that is freedom, that is complete freedom.'' These are the freedoms. Now the attainments.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-7', 'en', '''Yajnavalkya, with how many Rik verses will the hotri perform in this sacrifice today?'' ''With three.'' ''Which three?'' ''The introductory verse, the offering verse, and the praise verse as the third.'' ''What does he win with them?'' ''Whatever has breath.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-8', 'en', '''Yajnavalkya, how many offerings will the adhvaryu make today?'' ''Three.'' ''Which three?'' ''Those that blaze up when offered, those that make a great noise, and those that sink down.'' ''What does he win with them?'' ''With those that blaze up, the world of the gods, for it shines; with those that make noise, the world of the ancestors, for it is noisy; with those that sink down, the human world, for it is below.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-9', 'en', '''Yajnavalkya, with how many deities does the brahman priest guard the sacrifice from the south today?'' ''With one.'' ''Which one?'' ''The mind. The mind is endless, and the All-gods are endless. With it he wins an endless world.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-10', 'en', '''Yajnavalkya, how many hymns of praise will the udgatri sing today?'' ''Three.'' ''Which three?'' ''The introductory, the offering, and the praise as the third.'' ''Which are they in the body?'' ''In-breath is the introductory, out-breath the offering, diffused breath the praise.'' ''What does he win?'' ''With the introductory, the earth; with the offering, the mid-air; with the praise, heaven.'' Then Ashvala fell silent.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-1', 'en', 'Then Jaratkarava Artabhaga questioned him. ''Yajnavalkya,'' he said, ''how many graspers are there, and how many over-graspers?'' ''Eight graspers and eight over-graspers.'' ''Which are they?''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-2', 'en', 'Breath (smell) is a grasper. It is grasped by odour, the out-breath, the over-grasper, for by breath (smell) one smells odours.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-3', 'en', 'Speech is a grasper. It is grasped by name, the over-grasper, for by speech one speaks names.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-4', 'en', 'The tongue is a grasper. It is grasped by taste, the over-grasper, for by the tongue one knows tastes.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-5', 'en', 'The eye is a grasper. It is grasped by form, the over-grasper, for by the eye one sees forms.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-6', 'en', 'The ear is a grasper. It is grasped by sound, the over-grasper, for by the ear one hears sounds.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-7', 'en', 'The mind is a grasper. It is grasped by desire, the over-grasper, for by the mind one desires.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-8', 'en', 'The hands is a grasper. It is grasped by action, the over-grasper, for by the hands one acts.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-9', 'en', 'The skin is a grasper. It is grasped by touch, the over-grasper, for by the skin one feels touches. These are the eight graspers and eight over-graspers.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-10', 'en', '''Yajnavalkya, since all this is the food of death, which deity has death as its food?'' ''Fire is death, and it is the food of water. One who knows this conquers repeated death.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-11', 'en', '''Yajnavalkya, when a person dies, do his breaths leave him or not?'' ''No,'' said Yajnavalkya. ''They gather right here. He swells, he puffs up, and puffed up he lies dead.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-12', 'en', '''Yajnavalkya, when a person dies, what does not leave him?'' ''His name. The name is endless, and the All-gods are endless. By it he wins an endless world.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-13', 'en', '''Yajnavalkya, when a dead person''s speech goes into fire, his breath into wind, his eye into the sun, his mind into the moon, his ear into the directions, his body into the earth, his self into space, his body hair into plants, his head hair into trees, and his blood and semen into water, where is that person then?'' ''Artabhaga, my friend, take my hand. We two alone will know this; this is not for a crowd.'' They went aside and talked. What they spoke about was action (karma); what they praised was action. One becomes good by good action and bad by bad action. Then Artabhaga fell silent.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-3-1', 'en', 'Then Bhujyu Lahyayani questioned him. ''Yajnavalkya,'' he said, ''we were travelling as students in the Madra country and came to the house of Patanchala Kapya. His daughter was possessed by a gandharva. We asked him, "Who are you?" He said, "Sudhanvan Angirasa." When we asked him about the ends of the worlds, we said, "Where did the Parikshitas go?" So I ask you, Yajnavalkya, where did the Parikshitas go?''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-3-2', 'en', 'He said, ''He surely told you: they went where those who perform the horse sacrifice go.'' ''Where do they go?'' ''This world is thirty-two days of the sun''s chariot wide. The earth surrounds it on all sides, twice as wide. The ocean surrounds the earth, twice as wide. Between them is a space as fine as the edge of a razor or the wing of a fly. Indra, becoming a bird, handed them to the wind. The wind placed them in itself and took them where those who performed the horse sacrifice were.'' So he praised the wind. Wind is the individual and wind is the whole. One who knows this conquers repeated death. Then Bhujyu fell silent.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-4-1', 'en', 'Then Ushasta Chakrayana questioned him. ''Yajnavalkya,'' he said, ''explain to me the Brahman that is directly present, not hidden, the Self that is within all.'' ''It is your Self, which is within all.'' ''Which one, Yajnavalkya, is within all?'' ''The one who breathes in with the in-breath is your Self within all; the one who breathes out with the out-breath, who breathes through with the diffused breath, who breathes up with the upward breath, is your Self within all. This is your Self within all.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-4-2', 'en', 'Ushasta said, ''This is like saying "That is a cow, that is a horse." Explain to me the Brahman that is directly present, the Self within all.'' ''It is your Self, which is within all.'' ''Which one is within all?'' ''You cannot see the seer of seeing; you cannot hear the hearer of hearing; you cannot think the thinker of thinking; you cannot know the knower of knowing. That is your Self within all. Everything else is suffering.'' Then Ushasta fell silent.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-5-1', 'en', 'Then Kahola Kaushitakeya questioned him. ''Yajnavalkya, explain to me the Brahman that is directly present, the Self within all.'' ''It is your Self within all.'' ''Which one?'' ''The one that goes beyond hunger and thirst, sorrow and confusion, old age and death. Knowing this Self, brahmins give up the desire for sons, for wealth and for worlds, and live as wandering beggars. The desire for sons is the desire for wealth, and the desire for wealth is the desire for worlds; both are just desires. So a brahmin should put learning behind him and live like a child. Having put childlikeness and learning behind him, he becomes a sage. Having put silence and non-silence behind him, he becomes a true brahmin. How does such a brahmin live? However he lives, he is such. Everything else is suffering.'' Then Kahola fell silent.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-6-1', 'en', 'Then Gargi Vachaknavi questioned him. ''Yajnavalkya, since all this is woven back and forth on water, on what is water woven?'' ''On air, Gargi.'' ''On what is air woven?'' ''On the mid-air worlds.'' ''On what are they woven?'' ''On the gandharva worlds.'' ''And those?'' ''On the sun worlds.'' ''And those?'' ''On the moon worlds.'' ''And those?'' ''On the star worlds.'' ''And those?'' ''On the worlds of the gods.'' ''And those?'' ''On the worlds of Indra.'' ''And those?'' ''On the worlds of Prajapati.'' ''And those?'' ''On the worlds of Brahman.'' ''On what are the worlds of Brahman woven?'' He said, ''Gargi, do not ask too much, or your head will fall off. You are asking too much about a deity about which one should not ask too much. Gargi, do not ask too much.'' Then Gargi fell silent.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-1', 'en', 'Then Uddalaka Aruni questioned him. ''Yajnavalkya, we lived in the Madra country in the house of Patanchala Kapya, studying the sacrifice. His wife was possessed by a gandharva. We asked him who he was, and he said he was Kabandha Atharvana. He asked Kapya and the students, "Do you know the thread by which this world, the next world and all beings are strung together?" Kapya said, "I do not know it, sir." He asked, "Do you know the inner controller who controls this world, the next world and all beings from within?" Kapya said, "I do not know it, sir." He said, "Whoever knows that thread and that inner controller knows Brahman, the worlds, the gods, the Vedas, beings, the Self, everything." I know these. If you, Yajnavalkya, take away the cows without knowing that thread and that inner controller, your head will fall off.'' ''Gautama, I do know that thread and that inner controller.'' ''Anyone can say "I know, I know." Tell us what you know.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-2', 'en', 'He said, ''Air, Gautama, is that thread. By air as a thread this world, the next world and all beings are strung together. That is why, when a person dies, people say his limbs have come loose, for they were strung together by air as a thread.'' ''That is right, Yajnavalkya. Now tell us about the inner controller.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-3', 'en', 'He who dwells in the earth, yet is other than the earth; whom the earth does not know; whose body the earth is; who controls the earth from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-4', 'en', 'He who dwells in the waters, yet is other than the waters; whom the waters does not know; whose body the waters is; who controls the waters from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-5', 'en', 'He who dwells in fire, yet is other than fire; whom fire does not know; whose body fire is; who controls fire from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-6', 'en', 'He who dwells in the mid-air, yet is other than the mid-air; whom the mid-air does not know; whose body the mid-air is; who controls the mid-air from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-7', 'en', 'He who dwells in the wind, yet is other than the wind; whom the wind does not know; whose body the wind is; who controls the wind from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-8', 'en', 'He who dwells in heaven, yet is other than heaven; whom heaven does not know; whose body heaven is; who controls heaven from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-9', 'en', 'He who dwells in the sun, yet is other than the sun; whom the sun does not know; whose body the sun is; who controls the sun from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-10', 'en', 'He who dwells in the directions, yet is other than the directions; whom the directions does not know; whose body the directions is; who controls the directions from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-11', 'en', 'He who dwells in the moon and stars, yet is other than the moon and stars; whom the moon and stars does not know; whose body the moon and stars is; who controls the moon and stars from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-12', 'en', 'He who dwells in space, yet is other than space; whom space does not know; whose body space is; who controls space from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-13', 'en', 'He who dwells in darkness, yet is other than darkness; whom darkness does not know; whose body darkness is; who controls darkness from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-14', 'en', 'He who dwells in light, yet is other than light; whom light does not know; whose body light is; who controls light from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-15', 'en', 'Now about beings. He who dwells in all beings, yet is other than all beings; whom all beings do not know; whose body all beings are; who controls all beings from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal. So much about beings.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-16', 'en', 'He who dwells in breath, yet is other than breath; whom breath does not know; whose body breath is; who controls breath from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-17', 'en', 'He who dwells in speech, yet is other than speech; whom speech does not know; whose body speech is; who controls speech from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-18', 'en', 'He who dwells in the eye, yet is other than the eye; whom the eye does not know; whose body the eye is; who controls the eye from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-19', 'en', 'He who dwells in the ear, yet is other than the ear; whom the ear does not know; whose body the ear is; who controls the ear from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-20', 'en', 'He who dwells in the mind, yet is other than the mind; whom the mind does not know; whose body the mind is; who controls the mind from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-21', 'en', 'He who dwells in the skin, yet is other than the skin; whom the skin does not know; whose body the skin is; who controls the skin from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-22', 'en', 'He who dwells in understanding, yet is other than understanding; whom understanding does not know; whose body understanding is; who controls understanding from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-23', 'en', 'He who dwells in semen, yet is other than semen; whom semen does not know; whose body semen is; who controls it from within: he is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal. He is unseen but the seer, unheard but the hearer, unthought but the thinker, unknown but the knower. There is no other seer than he, no other hearer, no other thinker, no other knower. He is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal. Everything else is suffering. Then Uddalaka Aruni fell silent.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-1', 'en', 'Then Vachaknavi (Gargi) said, ''Honoured brahmins, I will ask him two questions. If he answers them, none of you will ever beat him in a debate about Brahman.'' ''Ask, Gargi.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-2', 'en', 'She said, ''Yajnavalkya, as a hero of Kashi or Videha strings his unstrung bow, takes two piercing arrows in hand and rises to face the enemy, so I rise to face you with two questions. Answer them.'' ''Ask, Gargi.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-3', 'en', 'She said, ''Yajnavalkya, that which is above heaven, below the earth, between heaven and earth, and which people call past, present and future: on what is it woven back and forth?''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-4', 'en', 'He said, ''Gargi, that which is above heaven, below the earth, between them, and which people call past, present and future, is woven back and forth on space.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-5', 'en', 'She said, ''I bow to you, Yajnavalkya, for answering this for me. Get ready for the other.'' ''Ask, Gargi.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-6', 'en', 'She said, ''Yajnavalkya, that which is above heaven, below the earth, between them, and which people call past, present and future: on what is it woven back and forth?''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-7', 'en', 'He said, ''Gargi, it is woven back and forth on space.'' ''Then on what is space woven back and forth?''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-8', 'en', 'He said, ''Gargi, the brahmins call that the Imperishable. It is not coarse, not fine, not short, not long, not red, not moist; without shadow, without darkness, without air, without space, without attachment, without taste, without smell, without eyes, without ears, without speech, without mind, without light, without breath, without mouth, without measure, without inside or outside. It eats nothing, and no one eats it.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-9', 'en', '''By the command of this Imperishable, Gargi, the sun and moon are held apart; heaven and earth are held apart; moments, hours, days and nights, half-months, months, seasons and years are held apart. By its command some rivers flow east from the snowy mountains and others west, each in its own direction. By its command people praise the givers, the gods depend on the patron, and the ancestors on the ladle offering.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-10', 'en', '''Whoever offers sacrifices, worships and practises austerities in this world for many thousands of years without knowing this Imperishable, his work comes to an end. Whoever leaves this world without knowing this Imperishable, Gargi, is pitiable. But whoever leaves this world knowing this Imperishable is a true brahmin.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-11', 'en', '''This Imperishable, Gargi, is unseen but sees, unheard but hears, unthought but thinks, unknown but knows. There is no other seer than this, no other hearer, no other thinker, no other knower. On this Imperishable, Gargi, space is woven back and forth.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-12', 'en', 'She said, ''Honoured brahmins, think yourselves lucky if you get away from him by bowing to him. None of you will ever beat him in a debate about Brahman.'' Then Vachaknavi fell silent.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-1', 'en', 'Then Vidagdha Shakalya questioned him. ''How many gods are there, Yajnavalkya?'' He answered with the invocation formula: ''As many as are named in the invocation to the All-gods: three hundred and three, and three thousand and three.'' ''Yes,'' he said. ''But how many gods are there really?'' ''Thirty-three.'' ''Yes. But how many really?'' ''Six.'' ''Yes. But how many?'' ''Three.'' ''Yes. How many?'' ''Two.'' ''Yes. How many?'' ''One and a half.'' ''Yes. How many?'' ''One.'' ''Yes. Which are those three hundred and three and three thousand and three?''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-2', 'en', 'He said, ''Those are just their powers. There are really thirty-three gods.'' ''Which are the thirty-three?'' ''The eight Vasus, the eleven Rudras, the twelve Adityas make thirty-one; Indra and Prajapati make thirty-three.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-3', 'en', '''Which are the Vasus?'' ''Fire, earth, wind, mid-air, sun, heaven, moon and stars. These are the Vasus, for in them all this is placed (vasu).''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-4', 'en', '''Which are the Rudras?'' ''The ten breaths in a person, with the self as the eleventh. When they leave this mortal body, they make people weep (rodayanti); so they are the Rudras.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-5', 'en', '''Which are the Adityas?'' ''The twelve months of the year are the Adityas, for they move along taking (adadana) everything with them.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-6', 'en', '''Which is Indra and which is Prajapati?'' ''Thunder is Indra; the sacrifice is Prajapati.'' ''What is thunder?'' ''The thunderbolt.'' ''What is the sacrifice?'' ''Animals.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-7', 'en', '''Which are the six?'' ''Fire, earth, wind, mid-air, sun and heaven. These are the six, for all this is these six.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-8', 'en', '''Which are the three gods?'' ''These three worlds, for all the gods are in them.'' ''Which are the two gods?'' ''Food and breath.'' ''Which is the one and a half?'' ''The one who blows here (the wind).''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-9', 'en', 'They say, ''Since the wind blows as one, how is it one and a half?'' Because in it all this grew (adhyardhnot); so it is one and a half. ''Which is the one god?'' ''Breath. It is Brahman; they call it "That".''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-10', 'en', '''Whoever knows the person whose home is the earth, whose world is fire, whose light is the mind, the final support of every self, is truly a knower, Yajnavalkya.'' ''I know that person, the final support of every self, of whom you speak. It is the person in the body. Tell me, Shakalya, what is his deity?'' ''The immortal,'' he said.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-11', 'en', '''Whoever knows the person whose home is desire, whose world is the heart, whose light is the mind, the final support of every self, is truly a knower, Yajnavalkya.'' ''I know that person, the final support of every self, of whom you speak. It is the person made of desire. Tell me, Shakalya, what is his deity?'' ''Women,'' he said.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-12', 'en', '''Whoever knows the person whose home is forms, whose world is the eye, whose light is the mind, the final support of every self, is truly a knower, Yajnavalkya.'' ''I know that person, the final support of every self, of whom you speak. It is the person in the sun. Tell me, Shakalya, what is his deity?'' ''Truth,'' he said.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-13', 'en', '''Whoever knows the person whose home is space, whose world is the ear, whose light is the mind, the final support of every self, is truly a knower, Yajnavalkya.'' ''I know that person, the final support of every self, of whom you speak. It is the person in hearing and in the echo. Tell me, Shakalya, what is his deity?'' ''The directions,'' he said.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-14', 'en', '''Whoever knows the person whose home is darkness, whose world is the heart, whose light is the mind, the final support of every self, is truly a knower, Yajnavalkya.'' ''I know that person, the final support of every self, of whom you speak. It is the person made of shadow. Tell me, Shakalya, what is his deity?'' ''Death,'' he said.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-15', 'en', '''Whoever knows the person whose home is forms, whose world is the heart, whose light is the mind, the final support of every self, is truly a knower, Yajnavalkya.'' ''I know that person, the final support of every self, of whom you speak. It is the person in the mirror. Tell me, Shakalya, what is his deity?'' ''Truth,'' he said.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-16', 'en', '''Whoever knows the person whose home is water, whose world is the heart, whose light is the mind, the final support of every self, is truly a knower, Yajnavalkya.'' ''I know that person, the final support of every self, of whom you speak. It is the person in water. Tell me, Shakalya, what is his deity?'' ''Varuna,'' he said.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-17', 'en', '''Whoever knows the person whose home is semen, whose world is the heart, whose light is the mind, the final support of every self, is truly a knower, Yajnavalkya.'' ''I know that person, the final support of every self, of whom you speak. It is the person made of the son. Tell me, Shakalya, what is his deity?'' ''Prajapati,'' he said.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-18', 'en', '''Shakalya,'' said Yajnavalkya, ''have these brahmins made you their coal-scoop?''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-19', 'en', '''Yajnavalkya,'' said Shakalya, ''you have talked down the brahmins of the Kurus and Panchalas. What Brahman do you know?'' ''I know the directions with their gods and their supports.'' ''If you know the directions with their gods and supports,''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-20', 'en', '''What deity do you have in the east?'' ''The sun.'' ''On what does the sun rest?'' ''On the eye.'' ''On what does the eye rest?'' ''On forms, for one sees forms with the eye.'' ''On what do forms rest?'' ''On the heart, for one knows forms with the heart; forms rest on the heart.'' ''That is right, Yajnavalkya.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-21', 'en', '''What deity do you have in the south?'' ''Yama.'' ''On what does Yama rest?'' ''On the sacrifice.'' ''On what does the sacrifice rest?'' ''On the priests'' fee.'' ''On what does the fee rest?'' ''On faith, for when one has faith, one gives the fee.'' ''On what does faith rest?'' ''On the heart, for one knows faith with the heart.'' ''That is right, Yajnavalkya.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-22', 'en', '''What deity do you have in the west?'' ''Varuna.'' ''On what does Varuna rest?'' ''On water.'' ''On what does water rest?'' ''On semen.'' ''On what does semen rest?'' ''On the heart. So people say of a child who looks like his father, "He has come from his heart, he is made from his heart."'' ''That is right, Yajnavalkya.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-23', 'en', '''What deity do you have in the north?'' ''Soma.'' ''On what does Soma rest?'' ''On the consecration.'' ''On what does the consecration rest?'' ''On truth. So they tell one who is consecrated, "Speak the truth."'' ''On what does truth rest?'' ''On the heart, for one knows truth with the heart.'' ''That is right, Yajnavalkya.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-24', 'en', '''What deity do you have in the fixed direction (the zenith)?'' ''Fire.'' ''On what does fire rest?'' ''On speech.'' ''On what does speech rest?'' ''On the heart.'' ''On what does the heart rest?''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-25', 'en', '''You idiot!'' said Yajnavalkya, ''to think it could rest anywhere other than in us! If it were anywhere else, dogs would eat it or birds would tear it apart.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-26', 'en', '''On what do you and your self rest?'' ''On the in-breath.'' ''On what does the in-breath rest?'' ''On the out-breath.'' ''On the out-breath?'' ''On the diffused breath.'' ''On that?'' ''On the upward breath.'' ''On that?'' ''On the balancing breath. This Self is "not this, not this". It cannot be grasped, for it is not grasped; it does not decay, for it does not decay; it does not cling, for it does not cling; it is unbound, it does not tremble, it is not harmed. These are the eight homes, the eight worlds, the eight gods, the eight persons. I ask you about the person of the Upanishads who leads these persons away, brings them back and goes beyond them. If you do not tell me, your head will fall off.'' Shakalya did not know him, and his head fell off. Robbers even stole his bones, thinking they were something else.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-27', 'en', 'Then he said, ''Honoured brahmins, whoever wishes may question me, or all of you may. Or I will question whoever wishes, or all of you.'' But the brahmins did not dare.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-28', 'en', 'He questioned them with these verses: ''A man is just like a great tree: his hairs are leaves, his skin the bark. Blood flows from his skin as sap from bark, so it flows from a wounded man as from a cut tree. His flesh is the inner wood, his sinews the fibre, his bones the heartwood, his marrow is like pith. A tree that is cut down grows again from its root. From what root does a mortal grow when cut down by death? Do not say "from seed", for seed comes from the living. A tree grows from a seed and also, after dying, from its root. But if a tree is pulled out with its root, it does not grow again. From what root does a mortal grow when cut down by death? Once born, he is not born again; who could bring him to birth again? Brahman is awareness and bliss, the final goal of the giver, and of the one who stands firm and knows it.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-1-1', 'en', 'Janaka, king of Videha, was holding court. Yajnavalkya came by. Janaka said, ''Yajnavalkya, why have you come: for cattle, or for subtle questions?'' ''For both, your majesty,'' he said.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-1-2', 'en', '''Let us hear what anyone else has told you.'' ''Jitvan Shailini told me that speech is Brahman.'' ''As one who had a mother, father and teacher would say, so Jitvan said speech is Brahman, for what is one without speech? But did he tell you its home and support?'' ''He did not.'' ''Then it stands on one leg only, your majesty.'' ''Then tell us, Yajnavalkya.'' ''Speech is its home, space its support. One should worship it as wisdom.'' ''What is wisdom, Yajnavalkya?'' ''Speech itself, your majesty. Through speech a friend is known, and the Vedas, sciences, sacrifices, this world and the next, and all beings.'' ''Speech, your majesty, is the highest Brahman. Speech never leaves one who knows and worships it so; all beings come to him; becoming a god, he goes to the gods.'' ''I will give you a thousand cows with a bull like an elephant,'' said Janaka. Yajnavalkya said, ''My father believed one should not accept gifts without completing the teaching.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-1-3', 'en', '''Let us hear what anyone else has told you.'' ''Udanka Shaulbayana told me that breath is Brahman.'' ''As one who had a mother, father and teacher would say, so Udanka said breath is Brahman, for what is one without breath? But did he tell you its home and support?'' ''He did not.'' ''Then it stands on one leg only, your majesty.'' ''Then tell us, Yajnavalkya.'' ''Breath is its home, space its support. One should worship it as dear.'' ''What is dearness, Yajnavalkya?'' ''Breath itself. For love of breath one sacrifices for the unworthy and accepts from those one should not; for love of breath one fears being killed wherever one goes.'' ''Breath, your majesty, is the highest Brahman. Breath never leaves one who knows and worships it so; all beings come to him; becoming a god, he goes to the gods.'' ''I will give you a thousand cows with a bull like an elephant,'' said Janaka. Yajnavalkya said, ''My father believed one should not accept gifts without completing the teaching.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-1-4', 'en', '''Let us hear what anyone else has told you.'' ''Barku Varshna told me that the eye is Brahman.'' ''As one who had a mother, father and teacher would say, so Barku said the eye is Brahman, for what is one without the eye? But did he tell you its home and support?'' ''He did not.'' ''Then it stands on one leg only, your majesty.'' ''Then tell us, Yajnavalkya.'' ''The eye is its home, space its support. One should worship it as truth.'' ''What is truth?'' ''The eye itself. When people ask one who has seen, "Did you see?" and he says "I saw," that is the truth.'' ''The eye, your majesty, is the highest Brahman. The eye never leaves one who knows and worships it so; all beings come to him; becoming a god, he goes to the gods.'' ''I will give you a thousand cows with a bull like an elephant,'' said Janaka. Yajnavalkya said, ''My father believed one should not accept gifts without completing the teaching.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-1-5', 'en', '''Let us hear what anyone else has told you.'' ''Gardabhivipita Bharadvaja told me that the ear is Brahman.'' ''As one who had a mother, father and teacher would say, so Gardabhivipita said the ear is Brahman, for what is one without the ear? But did he tell you its home and support?'' ''He did not.'' ''Then it stands on one leg only, your majesty.'' ''Then tell us, Yajnavalkya.'' ''The ear is its home, space its support. One should worship it as endless.'' ''What is endlessness?'' ''The directions, your majesty. Whatever direction one goes, one never reaches its end, for the directions are endless, and the directions are the ear.'' ''The ear, your majesty, is the highest Brahman. The ear never leaves one who knows and worships it so; all beings come to him; becoming a god, he goes to the gods.'' ''I will give you a thousand cows with a bull like an elephant,'' said Janaka. Yajnavalkya said, ''My father believed one should not accept gifts without completing the teaching.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-1-6', 'en', '''Let us hear what anyone else has told you.'' ''Satyakama Jabala told me that the mind is Brahman.'' ''As one who had a mother, father and teacher would say, so Satyakama said the mind is Brahman, for what is one without the mind? But did he tell you its home and support?'' ''He did not.'' ''Then it stands on one leg only, your majesty.'' ''Then tell us, Yajnavalkya.'' ''The mind is its home, space its support. One should worship it as bliss.'' ''What is bliss?'' ''The mind itself. With the mind a man is drawn to a woman, and a son like him is born of her. That is bliss.'' ''The mind, your majesty, is the highest Brahman. The mind never leaves one who knows and worships it so; all beings come to him; becoming a god, he goes to the gods.'' ''I will give you a thousand cows with a bull like an elephant,'' said Janaka. Yajnavalkya said, ''My father believed one should not accept gifts without completing the teaching.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-1-7', 'en', '''Let us hear what anyone else has told you.'' ''Vidagdha Shakalya told me that the heart is Brahman.'' ''As one who had a mother, father and teacher would say, so Vidagdha said the heart is Brahman, for what is one without the heart? But did he tell you its home and support?'' ''He did not.'' ''Then it stands on one leg only, your majesty.'' ''Then tell us, Yajnavalkya.'' ''The heart is its home, space its support. One should worship it as stability.'' ''What is stability?'' ''The heart itself. The heart is the home and support of all beings; in the heart all beings rest.'' ''The heart, your majesty, is the highest Brahman. The heart never leaves one who knows and worships it so; all beings come to him; becoming a god, he goes to the gods.'' ''I will give you a thousand cows with a bull like an elephant,'' said Janaka. Yajnavalkya said, ''My father believed one should not accept gifts without completing the teaching.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-2-1', 'en', 'Janaka, king of Videha, came down from his seat and said, ''Homage to you, Yajnavalkya. Teach me.'' He said, ''Your majesty, as one about to make a long journey would get a chariot or a ship, so you have equipped yourself with these teachings. You are honoured and rich, you have studied the Vedas and heard the secret teachings. When you leave this world, where will you go?'' ''Sir, I do not know where I will go.'' ''Then I will tell you where you will go.'' ''Tell me, sir.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-2-2', 'en', '''The person in the right eye is called Indha (the kindler). Though he is Indha, people call him Indra, indirectly, for the gods seem to love the indirect and dislike the direct.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-2-3', 'en', '''The form of a person in the left eye is his wife, Viraj. Their meeting place is the space within the heart. Their food is the red lump in the heart. Their covering is the net-like thing in the heart. Their path is the channel that rises up from the heart. Like a hair split a thousand times, the channels called hita lie within the heart. Through them the food flows on. So this self eats finer food than the bodily self.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-2-4', 'en', '''The eastern direction is his eastern breaths; the southern, his southern breaths; the western, his western; the northern, his northern; the upper, his upper; the lower, his lower; all directions, all his breaths. This Self is "not this, not this". It cannot be grasped, for it is not grasped; it does not decay; it does not cling; it is unbound, it does not tremble, it is not harmed. Janaka, you have reached fearlessness,'' said Yajnavalkya. Janaka said, ''May fearlessness come to you, Yajnavalkya, who teach us fearlessness. Homage to you. Here are the Videhas, and here am I, at your service.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-1', 'en', 'Yajnavalkya came to Janaka, king of Videha, intending not to speak. But once, when Janaka and Yajnavalkya had talked about the fire offering, Yajnavalkya had granted him a wish, and he had chosen to ask questions as he liked. Yajnavalkya had granted it. So the king asked first.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-2', 'en', '''Yajnavalkya, what is the light of a person?'' ''The sun, your majesty. By the light of the sun one sits, goes about, does work and returns.'' ''That is so, Yajnavalkya.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-3', 'en', '''When the sun has set, Yajnavalkya, what is the light of a person?'' ''The moon is his light. By the light of the moon one sits, goes about, does work and returns.'' ''That is so.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-4', 'en', '''When the sun has set and the moon has set, what is the light of a person?'' ''Fire is his light. By the light of fire one sits, goes about, does work and returns.'' ''That is so.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-5', 'en', '''When the sun and moon have set and the fire has gone out, what is the light of a person?'' ''Speech is his light. By speech one sits, goes about, does work and returns. So, your majesty, when one cannot see even one''s own hand, if a voice is heard one goes toward it.'' ''That is so.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-6', 'en', '''When the sun and moon have set, the fire has gone out and speech is silent, what is the light of a person?'' ''The Self is his light. By the light of the Self one sits, goes about, does work and returns.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-7', 'en', '''Which is the Self?'' ''The person made of understanding among the breaths, the light within the heart. Remaining the same, he moves between both worlds, as if thinking, as if moving. Becoming a dream, he goes beyond this world and the forms of death.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-8', 'en', 'This person, when born and taking a body, is joined with evils. When he leaves the body and dies, he leaves evils behind.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-9', 'en', 'This person has two states: this world and the other world. A third, the state of dream, is where they meet. Standing in this meeting place, he sees both: this world and the other. Whatever way leads into the other world, entering it he sees both evils and joys. When he falls asleep, he takes material from this all-containing world, breaks it down himself, builds it up himself, and dreams by his own brightness, his own light. There this person becomes self-luminous.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-10', 'en', 'There are no chariots there, no horses, no roads; but he creates chariots, horses and roads. There are no joys, pleasures or delights there; but he creates them. There are no ponds, lakes or rivers there; but he creates them. For he is the creator.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-11', 'en', 'There are these verses: ''Striking down the bodily in sleep, sleepless himself he watches the sleeping senses. Taking their light, he returns to his place: the golden person, the lone swan.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-12', 'en', '''Guarding his low nest with breath, the immortal roams outside the nest. The immortal goes wherever he wishes: the golden person, the lone swan.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-13', 'en', '''In the dream state, moving up and down, the god makes many forms for himself: now as if enjoying himself with women, now as if laughing, now as if seeing frightening things.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-14', 'en', '''People see his playground, but no one sees him.'' So they say one should not wake a sleeper suddenly, for it is hard to cure if he does not find his way back. Others say the dream state is just like the waking state, for what one sees awake one sees asleep. But there the person is his own light. ''I will give you a thousand cows, sir. Teach me beyond this, for my freedom.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-15', 'en', 'Having enjoyed himself and roamed in that state of deep peace, and seen good and bad, he hurries back by the same way to where he started, to the dream state. Whatever he sees there does not follow him, for this person is unattached. ''That is so, Yajnavalkya. I will give you a thousand cows. Teach me beyond this, for my freedom.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-16', 'en', 'Having enjoyed himself and roamed in dream, and seen good and bad, he hurries back by the same way to the waking state. Whatever he sees there does not follow him, for this person is unattached. ''That is so, Yajnavalkya. I will give you a thousand cows. Teach me beyond this, for my freedom.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-17', 'en', 'Having enjoyed himself and roamed in the waking state, and seen good and bad, he hurries back by the same way to the dream state.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-18', 'en', 'As a great fish swims along both banks of a river, the near and the far, so this person moves along both states, dream and waking.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-19', 'en', 'As a hawk or an eagle, tired from flying in the sky, folds its wings and glides down to its nest, so this person hurries to that state where, asleep, he desires nothing and sees no dream.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-20', 'en', 'He has those channels called hita, as fine as a hair split a thousand times, full of white, blue, tawny, green and red. When he dreams that people are killing him, or overpowering him, or an elephant is chasing him, or he falls into a pit, he imagines through ignorance the fears he sees when awake. But when he feels like a god or a king, thinking ''I am all this,'' that is his highest world.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-21', 'en', 'This is his form beyond desire, free from evil, without fear. As a man in the embrace of his beloved wife knows nothing outside or inside, so this person, embraced by the wise Self, knows nothing outside or inside. This is his form in which all desires are fulfilled, in which the Self is the only desire, in which there is no desire, beyond sorrow.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-22', 'en', 'There a father is no father, a mother no mother, worlds no worlds, gods no gods, Vedas no Vedas. There a thief is no thief, a murderer no murderer, an outcast no outcast, a monk no monk, an ascetic no ascetic. He is not followed by good, not followed by evil, for then he has passed beyond all the sorrows of the heart.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-23', 'en', 'When there it does not see, yet it is seeing, though it does not see. For the sight of the seeer can never be lost, since it is imperishable. But there is no second, nothing other than it, separate from it, that it could see.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-24', 'en', 'When there it does not smell, yet it is smelling, though it does not smell. For the smelling of the smeller can never be lost, since it is imperishable. But there is no second, nothing other than it, separate from it, that it could smell.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-25', 'en', 'When there it does not taste, yet it is tasteing, though it does not taste. For the tasting of the tasteer can never be lost, since it is imperishable. But there is no second, nothing other than it, separate from it, that it could taste.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-26', 'en', 'When there it does not speak, yet it is speaking, though it does not speak. For the speech of the speaker can never be lost, since it is imperishable. But there is no second, nothing other than it, separate from it, that it could speak.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-27', 'en', 'When there it does not hear, yet it is hearing, though it does not hear. For the hearing of the hearer can never be lost, since it is imperishable. But there is no second, nothing other than it, separate from it, that it could hear.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-28', 'en', 'When there it does not think, yet it is thinking, though it does not think. For the thought of the thinker can never be lost, since it is imperishable. But there is no second, nothing other than it, separate from it, that it could think.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-29', 'en', 'When there it does not touch, yet it is touching, though it does not touch. For the touch of the toucher can never be lost, since it is imperishable. But there is no second, nothing other than it, separate from it, that it could touch.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-30', 'en', 'When there it does not know, yet it is knowing, though it does not know. For the knowing of the knower can never be lost, since it is imperishable. But there is no second, nothing other than it, separate from it, that it could know.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-31', 'en', 'Where there seems to be another, there one can see another, smell another, taste another, speak to another, hear another, think of another, touch another, know another.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-32', 'en', '''He becomes one, an ocean, the seer without a second. This is the world of Brahman, your majesty,'' Yajnavalkya taught him. ''This is his highest goal, his highest wealth, his highest world, his highest bliss. On a small part of this bliss other beings live.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-33', 'en', 'If someone is successful and rich among people, lord over others, with every human pleasure, that is the highest bliss of humans. A hundred such blisses make one bliss of the ancestors who have won their world. A hundred of those make one bliss in the world of the gandharvas. A hundred of those make one bliss of gods who became gods by their work. A hundred of those make one bliss of gods born as gods, and of a learned person free from crookedness and untouched by desire. A hundred of those make one bliss in Prajapati''s world, and of such a learned person. A hundred of those make one bliss in Brahman''s world, and of such a learned person. And this is the highest bliss. This is the world of Brahman, your majesty,'' said Yajnavalkya. ''I will give you a thousand cows, sir. Teach me beyond this, for my freedom.'' Here Yajnavalkya became afraid: ''This clever king has cornered me on every point.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-34', 'en', 'Having enjoyed himself and roamed in the dream state, and seen good and bad, he hurries back by the same way to the waking state.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-35', 'en', 'As a heavily loaded cart goes creaking along, so the bodily self, ridden by the wise Self, goes creaking along when a person is breathing his last.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-36', 'en', 'When a person becomes weak through old age or illness, as a mango, fig or pipal fruit comes loose from its stem, so this person frees himself from these limbs and hurries back by the same way to where he started, to a new life.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-37', 'en', 'As officials, police, charioteers and village heads wait for a king who is coming, with food, drink and lodgings, saying ''He is coming, he is arriving,'' so all beings wait for one who knows this, saying ''Brahman is coming, Brahman is arriving.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-38', 'en', 'As officials gather around a king who is about to leave, so at the time of death all the breaths gather around this Self, when a person is breathing his last.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-1', 'en', 'When this Self becomes weak and seems to lose consciousness, the breaths gather around him. Taking these particles of light, he goes down into the heart. When the person in the eye turns away, he no longer knows forms.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-2', 'en', '''He is becoming one; he does not see,'' they say. ''He does not smell, taste, speak, hear, think, touch or know,'' they say. The tip of his heart lights up, and by that light the Self leaves, through the eye, the head or another part of the body. As it leaves, life follows, and all the breaths follow life. He becomes conscious and goes to what is conscious. His knowledge, his deeds and his past experience take hold of him.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-3', 'en', 'As a caterpillar, reaching the end of a blade of grass, takes hold of another and draws itself across, so the Self, having struck down this body and made it unconscious, takes hold of another support and draws itself across.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-4', 'en', 'As a goldsmith takes a piece of gold and shapes it into a newer, more beautiful form, so the Self, having struck down this body and made it unconscious, makes for itself a newer, more beautiful form: of an ancestor, a gandharva, a god, of Prajapati, of Brahma, or of other beings.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-5', 'en', 'This Self is Brahman, made of understanding, mind, breath, sight, hearing, earth, water, wind, space, light and no light, desire and no desire, anger and no anger, right and wrong; made of everything. As it is made of this, so it is made of that. As one acts and behaves, so one becomes. Doing good, one becomes good; doing bad, one becomes bad. One becomes pure by pure action, bad by bad action. And they say: a person is made of desire. As his desire is, so is his will; as his will is, so is his action; as his action is, so he becomes.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-6', 'en', 'There is this verse: ''A man attached goes with his deeds to the place where his mind is fixed. Reaching the end of whatever deeds he did here, he returns from that world to this world for more action.'' So it is with one who desires. But one who does not desire, who is free from desire, whose desires are fulfilled, whose only desire is the Self, his breaths do not leave. Being Brahman, he goes to Brahman.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-7', 'en', 'There is this verse: ''When all the desires that dwell in the heart are let go, then the mortal becomes immortal and reaches Brahman here.'' As the cast-off skin of a snake lies dead on an anthill, so lies this body. But this bodiless, immortal life is Brahman, is light. ''I will give you a thousand cows, sir,'' said Janaka of Videha.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-8', 'en', 'There are these verses: ''The narrow, ancient path stretches far; it has touched me, I have found it. By it the wise, the knowers of Brahman, go up from here to the heavenly world, set free.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-9', 'en', '''On it, they say, are white, blue, tawny, green and red. This path was found by Brahman; by it goes the knower of Brahman, one who does good, one full of light.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-10', 'en', '''Into blind darkness enter those who worship ignorance; into even greater darkness those who delight in knowledge alone.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-11', 'en', '''Joyless are those worlds, covered in blind darkness. To them, after death, go people who are ignorant and unawakened.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-12', 'en', '''If a person knows the Self as "I am this", wishing for what, and for whose sake, would he suffer along with the body?''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-13', 'en', '''Whoever has found and awakened to the Self that has entered this dangerous, tangled place is the maker of all, for he is the creator of everything. The world is his; indeed he is the world.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-14', 'en', '''While we are here we may know it; if not, great is the loss. Those who know it become immortal; others go only to sorrow.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-15', 'en', 'When a person directly sees this Self as the shining one, the lord of what has been and what will be, they no longer shrink from anything.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-16', 'en', 'That beneath which the year rolls round with its days, the gods worship as the light of lights, as life, as the immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-17', 'en', 'That in which the five groups of five and space itself are established, that alone I regard as the Self. Knowing it, I am immortal, being the immortal Brahman.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-18', 'en', 'Those who know the breath of the breath, the eye of the eye, the ear of the ear and the mind of the mind have grasped the ancient, primal Brahman.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-20', 'en', 'It must be seen as one only: immeasurable and constant. The Self is stainless, beyond space, unborn, great and unchanging.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-21', 'en', 'Knowing that Self, a wise seeker should practise steady insight. One should not brood over many words, for that only wearies speech.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-22', 'en', 'That great unborn Self, the one made of understanding among the senses, lies in the space within the heart. It is the controller, lord and ruler of all. It does not grow greater by good deeds nor smaller by bad ones. It is the lord of all, the ruler of beings, the protector of beings, the dam that keeps the worlds apart. Seekers try to know it through study of the Vedas, sacrifice, giving, and disciplined fasting. Knowing it, one becomes a sage. Longing for it alone, wanderers leave home.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-23', 'en', 'A verse says this: ''This eternal greatness of the knower of Brahman neither grows nor shrinks by action. One should know its nature; knowing it, one is not stained by wrong deeds.'' So one who knows this becomes calm, self-controlled, withdrawn, patient and collected, and sees the Self in the Self, sees all as the Self. Evil does not overcome them; they overcome all evil. Evil does not burn them; they burn all evil. Free from evil, free from stain, free from doubt, they become a true knower of Brahman. ''This is the world of Brahman, your majesty; you have reached it,'' said Yajnavalkya. Janaka said: ''Sir, I give you the people of Videha, and myself with them, to serve you.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-24', 'en', 'That great unborn Self is the eater of food and the giver of wealth. One who knows this finds wealth.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-25', 'en', 'That great unborn Self is undecaying, deathless, immortal, fearless; it is Brahman. Brahman is fearlessness. One who knows this becomes the fearless Brahman.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-1', 'en', 'Yajnavalkya had two wives, Maitreyi and Katyayani. Maitreyi was given to talk of Brahman; Katyayani had only a woman''s ordinary concerns then. Now Yajnavalkya was about to take up another way of life.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-2', 'en', '''Maitreyi,'' said Yajnavalkya, ''I am going to leave this place to become a wanderer. Let me make a settlement between you and Katyayani.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-3', 'en', 'Maitreyi said: ''Sir, if this whole earth full of wealth were mine, would I become immortal through it?'' ''No,'' said Yajnavalkya. ''Your life would be like that of the wealthy. There is no hope of immortality through wealth.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-4', 'en', 'Maitreyi said: ''What should I do with what will not make me immortal? Tell me, sir, just what you know.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-5', 'en', 'Yajnavalkya said: ''You have always been dear to me, and now you have increased what is dear. Come, I will explain it to you. While I explain, reflect deeply.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-6', 'en', 'He said: ''Truly, a husband is dear not for love of the husband but for love of the Self. A wife is dear not for love of the wife but for love of the Self. Children, wealth, cattle, the priestly class, the ruling class, the worlds, the gods, the Vedas, all beings and everything are dear not for their own sake but for love of the Self. The Self must be seen, heard about, reflected on and meditated upon, Maitreyi. When the Self is seen, heard, thought of and known, all this is known.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-7', 'en', 'The priestly class rejects one who sees it as other than the Self. The ruling class, the worlds, the gods, the Vedas, beings and everything reject one who sees them as other than the Self. This priesthood, this royalty, these worlds, these gods, these Vedas, these beings, all this is this Self.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-8', 'en', 'It is like a drum being beaten: one cannot catch its sounds separately, but by grasping the drum or the drummer, the sound is grasped.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-9', 'en', 'It is like a conch being blown: one cannot catch its sounds separately, but by grasping the conch or the one blowing it, the sound is grasped.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-10', 'en', 'It is like a vina being played: one cannot catch its sounds separately, but by grasping the vina or the player, the sound is grasped.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-11', 'en', 'As smoke pours out in different directions from a fire laid with damp fuel, so the Rig Veda, Yajur Veda, Sama Veda, Atharvangirasa, history, ancient lore, sciences, Upanishads, verses, aphorisms, explanations and commentaries, sacrifice, offering, food, drink, this world and the next and all beings are the breath of this great being. All these are its breath.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-12', 'en', 'As the ocean is the meeting place of all waters, so the skin is of all touches, the nose of all smells, the tongue of all tastes, the eye of all forms, the ear of all sounds, the mind of all intentions, the heart of all knowledge, the hands of all work, the sexual organ of all pleasures, the anus of all excretions, the feet of all journeys, and speech of all Vedas.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-13', 'en', 'As a lump of salt has no inside or outside but is wholly taste, so this Self has no inside or outside but is wholly a mass of awareness. Arising from these elements, it vanishes again into them. After death there is no separate awareness, I tell you. So said Yajnavalkya.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-14', 'en', 'Maitreyi said: ''Here, sir, you have confused me. I do not understand this.'' He said: ''I am not saying anything confusing. This Self is imperishable; its nature is never to be cut off.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-15', 'en', 'Where there is a seeming duality, one sees, smells, tastes, speaks to, hears, thinks of, touches and knows another. But where everything has become one''s own Self, by what and whom should one see, smell, taste, speak to, hear, think of, touch or know? How can one know that by which all this is known? This Self is ''not this, not this''. It cannot be grasped, it does not decay, it does not cling, it is unbound, it does not suffer or come to harm. How can one know the knower? You have been taught, Maitreyi. This much, truly, is immortality. Saying this, Yajnavalkya went away.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-6-1', 'en', 'Now the line of teachers: Pautimashya from Gaupavana, Gaupavana from Pautimashya, Pautimashya from Gaupavana, Gaupavana from Kaushika, Kaushika from Kaundinya, Kaundinya from Shandilya, Shandilya from Kaushika and Gautama, Gautama...', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-6-2', 'en', '...from Agniveshya, Agniveshya from Gargya, Gargya from Gargya, Gargya from Gautama, Gautama from Saitava, Saitava from Parasharyayana, and so on through Uddalakayana, Jabalayana, Madhyandinayana, Saukarayana, Kashayana, Sayakayana and Kaushikayani...', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-4-6-3', 'en', '...from Ghritakaushika, through Parasharya, Jatukarnya, Asurayana and Yaska, Traivani, Aupajandhani, Asuri, Bharadvaja, Atreya, Manti, Gautama, Vatsya, Shandilya, Kaishorya Kapya, Kumaraharita, Galava, Vidarbhi-kaundinya, Vatsanapat Babhrava, Pathas Saubhara, Ayasya Angirasa, Abhuti Tvashtra, Vishvarupa Tvashtra, the Ashvins, Dadhyanch Atharvana, Atharvan Daiva, Mrityu Pradhvamsana, Pradhvamsana, Ekarshi, Viprajitti, Vyashti, Sanaru, Sanatana, Sanaga, Parameshthin, and Brahman. Brahman is self-existent. Homage to Brahman.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-1-1', 'en', 'That is full; this is full. From the full, the full arises. Taking the full from the full, the full alone remains. Brahman is space; the ancient one is space; space is the airy one, said the son of Kauravyayani. This is the Veda, the priests know; by it one knows what is to be known.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-2-1', 'en', 'The three kinds of children of Prajapati, gods, humans and demons, lived as students with their father Prajapati. After their studies, the gods said: ''Teach us, sir.'' He spoke the syllable ''Da'' and asked, ''Did you understand?'' They said, ''We understood. You told us: Damyata, be self-controlled.'' ''Yes,'' he said, ''you have understood.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-2-2', 'en', 'Then the humans said to him: ''Teach us, sir.'' He spoke the same syllable ''Da'' and asked, ''Did you understand?'' They said, ''We understood. You told us: Datta, give.'' ''Yes,'' he said, ''you have understood.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-2-3', 'en', 'Then the demons said to him: ''Teach us, sir.'' He spoke the same syllable ''Da'' and asked, ''Did you understand?'' They said, ''We understood. You told us: Dayadhvam, be compassionate.'' ''Yes,'' he said, ''you have understood.'' The divine voice of thunder repeats this: ''Da, Da, Da'': be self-controlled, give, be compassionate. One should learn these three: self-control, giving and compassion.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-3-1', 'en', 'The heart is Prajapati; it is Brahman; it is all this. ''Hridayam'' has three syllables. ''Hri'': his own people and others bring him offerings, one who knows this. ''Da'': his own people and others give to him. ''Yam'': he goes to the heavenly world, one who knows this.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-4-1', 'en', 'That was indeed this, and it was simply the truth. Whoever knows this great, first-born wonder as ''Brahman is truth'' conquers these worlds. How could one who knows this great, first-born wonder as ''Brahman is truth'' be conquered? For Brahman is truth.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-5-1', 'en', 'In the beginning this world was just water. The water produced truth; truth is Brahman. Brahman produced Prajapati, and Prajapati the gods. The gods worship truth alone. ''Satyam'' has three syllables: ''sa'', ''ti'', ''yam''. The first and last syllables are truth; the middle is untruth. Untruth is held on both sides by truth, so truth prevails. Untruth does not harm one who knows this.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-5-2', 'en', 'That truth is the sun. The person in the sun''s disc and the person in the right eye rest on each other: the one in the sun rests on this one through its rays, and this one rests on that through the breaths. When a person is about to die, they see the disc as plain; the rays no longer come back to them.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-5-3', 'en', 'Of the person in the sun''s disc, ''bhūḥ'' is the head: one head, one syllable. ''Bhuvaḥ'' is the arms: two arms, two syllables. ''Svaḥ'' is the feet: two feet, two syllables. Its secret name is ''day'' (ahar). One who knows this destroys and leaves behind evil.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-5-4', 'en', 'Of the person in the right eye, ''bhūḥ'' is the head: one head, one syllable. ''Bhuvaḥ'' is the arms: two arms, two syllables. ''Svaḥ'' is the feet: two feet, two syllables. Its secret name is ''I'' (aham). One who knows this destroys and leaves behind evil.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-6-1', 'en', 'This person, made of mind, whose nature is light, is inside the heart like a grain of rice or barley. It is the lord of all, the ruler of all, and governs everything that exists.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-7-1', 'en', 'They say lightning is Brahman. It is called lightning (vidyut) because it cuts (vidāna). One who knows lightning as Brahman cuts away their evil. For lightning is Brahman.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-8-1', 'en', 'One should meditate on speech as a cow. It has four teats: the calls ''svāhā'', ''vaṣaṭ'', ''hanta'' and ''svadhā''. The gods live on two of them, svāhā and vaṣaṭ; humans on hanta; the ancestors on svadhā. Breath is its bull, and mind its calf.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-9-1', 'en', 'The universal fire within a person is what digests the food that is eaten. Its sound is what one hears when covering the ears. When one is about to die, one no longer hears that sound.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-10-1', 'en', 'When a person leaves this world, they come to the wind. The wind opens for them a hole like the hole of a chariot wheel, and through it they rise up. They come to the sun, which opens for them a hole like that of a drum frame, and they rise further. They come to the moon, which opens a hole like that of a kettledrum, and they rise further. They come to a world without sorrow and without cold, and live there for endless years.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-11-1', 'en', 'This is the highest austerity: that a sick person suffers. One who knows this wins the highest world. This is the highest austerity: that the dead are carried to the forest. One who knows this wins the highest world. This is the highest austerity: that the dead are placed on the fire. One who knows this wins the highest world.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-12-1', 'en', 'Some say food is Brahman. That is not so, for food rots without breath. Some say breath is Brahman. That is not so, for breath dries up without food. Only when these two deities become one do they reach the highest state. Pratrida asked his father: ''What good could I do to one who knows this, or what harm?'' His father waved his hand: ''Don''t, Pratrida. Who reaches the highest by merging these two?'' And he told him: ''Vi'': food is ''vi'', for all beings enter (viś) food. ''Ram'': breath is ''ram'', for all beings delight (ram) in breath. All beings enter into, and delight in, one who knows this.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-13-1', 'en', 'The Uktha: breath is the Uktha, for breath raises up (utthāpayati) all this. A hero who knows the Uktha rises from him. One who knows this wins union with and the world of the Uktha.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-13-2', 'en', 'The Yajus: breath is the Yajus, for all beings are joined (yujyante) in breath. All beings join with him for his excellence. One who knows this wins union with and the world of the Yajus.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-13-3', 'en', 'The Saman: breath is the Saman, for all beings come together (samyañci) in breath. All beings come together for his excellence. One who knows this wins union with and the world of the Saman.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-13-4', 'en', 'The Kshatra: breath is the Kshatra, for breath protects (trāyate) the body from wounds (kṣaṇitoḥ). One who knows this reaches a protecting power that needs no other protection, and wins union with and the world of the Kshatra.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-1', 'en', 'Earth, mid-space, sky: these make eight syllables. One foot of the Gayatri has eight syllables. So this is its first foot. One who knows this foot of it wins as much as there is in the three worlds.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-2', 'en', 'The Rig, Yajur and Sama verses: these make eight syllables. One foot of the Gayatri has eight syllables. So this is its second foot. One who knows this foot of it wins as much as the threefold knowledge covers.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-3', 'en', 'In-breath, out-breath, diffused breath: these make eight syllables. One foot of the Gayatri has eight syllables. So this is its third foot. One who knows this foot of it wins as much as there are living beings. Its fourth, visible foot, beyond the dust, is the one who shines yonder (the sun). ''Fourth'' is turīya. ''Visible foot'' because it is seen. ''Beyond the dust'' because it shines above all the dust. One who knows this foot of it shines with glory and fame.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-4', 'en', 'This Gayatri rests on that fourth, visible foot beyond the dust. That rests on truth. The eye is truth. So if two people come arguing, one saying ''I saw it'', the other ''I heard it'', we would believe the one who says ''I saw it''. Truth rests on strength. Breath is strength. So people say strength is mightier than truth. Thus the Gayatri rests on what is within the self. It protected the gayas: the gayas are the breaths. Because it protected (tatre) the gayas, it is called Gayatri. The Savitri verse one recites is this very one. It protects the breaths of the one to whom it is recited.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-5', 'en', 'Some teach the Savitri in the Anushtubh metre, saying, ''Speech is Anushtubh; we teach speech.'' One should not do that. One should teach the Savitri in the Gayatri metre. Even if one who knows this accepts a great deal in gifts, it is not equal to a single foot of the Gayatri.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-6', 'en', 'If one accepted these three worlds full of wealth, that would equal the first foot of the Gayatri. If one accepted as much as the threefold knowledge covers, that would equal the second foot. If one accepted as much as there are living beings, that would equal the third foot. Its fourth, visible foot beyond the dust, the one who shines yonder, cannot be reached by anything. How could one accept so much?', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-7', 'en', 'Its worship: ''O Gayatri, you are one-footed, two-footed, three-footed, four-footed. You are footless, for you are not walked on. Homage to your fourth, visible foot beyond the dust!'' If one hates someone: ''May that person not gain that'', or ''May that wish of his not succeed.'' The wish of one against whom this is used does not succeed. Or one may say, ''May I gain that.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-8', 'en', 'Janaka of Videha said to Budila Ashvatarashvi: ''You called yourself a knower of the Gayatri, so how is it that you have become an elephant and are carrying burdens?'' He said: ''Your majesty, I did not know its mouth.'' Its mouth is fire. Even if a great deal is put into fire, it burns it all. So one who knows this, even if they do much wrong, consumes it all and becomes clean, pure, ageless and immortal.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-5-15-1', 'en', 'The face of truth is covered by a golden vessel. Uncover it, O Pushan, so that I who love truth may see. O Pushan, sole seer, Yama, Sun, son of Prajapati, spread out your rays and gather your light. I see your most blessed form. That person yonder, I am he. Let my breath go to the immortal wind, and this body end in ashes. Om. O mind, remember what has been done; remember, O mind, what has been done. O Agni, lead us by the good path to prosperity, O god who knows all our ways. Keep from us crooked wrongdoing. We offer you many words of homage.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-1', 'en', 'One who knows the eldest and best becomes the eldest and best among their own people. Breath is the eldest and best. One who knows this becomes the eldest and best among their people, and among those they wish to lead.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-2', 'en', 'One who knows the richest becomes the richest among their own people. Speech is the richest. One who knows this becomes the richest among their people, and among those they wish to lead.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-3', 'en', 'One who knows the firm base stands firm on level ground and on rough ground. The eye is the firm base, for with the eye one stands firm on level and rough ground. One who knows this stands firm on level and rough ground.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-4', 'en', 'One who knows attainment attains whatever they desire. The ear is attainment, for all the Vedas are attained in the ear. One who knows this attains whatever they desire.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-5', 'en', 'One who knows the resting place becomes the resting place of their own people and of others. The mind is the resting place. One who knows this becomes the resting place of their people and of others.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-6', 'en', 'One who knows procreation is blessed with offspring and cattle. Semen is procreation. One who knows this is blessed with offspring and cattle.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-7', 'en', 'These powers, arguing about who was best, went to Brahman and asked: ''Who is the richest of us?'' He said: ''The one whose departure makes the body seem worst off is the richest among you.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-8', 'en', 'Speech went out. After staying away for a year it came back and asked: ''How were you able to live without me?'' They said: ''Like the dumb who do not speak, but seeing with the eye, hearing with the ear, knowing with the mind and procreating with semen, we lived, breathing with breath, and using the other powers.'' Speech came back in.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-9', 'en', 'The eye went out. After staying away for a year it came back and asked: ''How were you able to live without me?'' They said: ''Like the blind who do not see, but speaking with speech, hearing with the ear, knowing with the mind and procreating with semen, we lived, breathing with breath, and using the other powers.'' The eye came back in.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-10', 'en', 'The ear went out. After staying away for a year it came back and asked: ''How were you able to live without me?'' They said: ''Like the deaf who do not hear, but speaking with speech, seeing with the eye, knowing with the mind and procreating with semen, we lived, breathing with breath, and using the other powers.'' The ear came back in.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-11', 'en', 'The mind went out. After staying away for a year it came back and asked: ''How were you able to live without me?'' They said: ''Like the simple who do not think, but speaking with speech, seeing with the eye, hearing with the ear and procreating with semen, we lived, breathing with breath, and using the other powers.'' The mind came back in.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-12', 'en', 'Semen went out. After staying away for a year it came back and asked: ''How were you able to live without me?'' They said: ''Like the impotent who have no offspring, but speaking with speech, seeing with the eye, hearing with the ear and knowing with the mind, we lived, breathing with breath, and using the other powers.'' Semen came back in.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-13', 'en', 'Then breath, about to leave, tore up the other powers as a great Sindhu horse might tear up the pegs that tether its feet. They said: ''Sir, do not leave! We cannot live without you.'' ''Then bring me tribute.'' ''So be it.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-14', 'en', 'Speech said: ''The richness that is mine is yours.'' The eye: ''The firm base that is mine is yours.'' The ear: ''The attainment that is mine is yours.'' The mind: ''The resting place that is mine is yours.'' Semen: ''The procreation that is mine is yours.'' Breath asked: ''Then what is my food, what is my clothing?'' ''Whatever there is, down to dogs, worms, insects and moths, is your food; water is your clothing.'' For one who knows this as the food of breath, nothing eaten is unfit food and nothing accepted is unfit. So learned priests sip water before and after eating, thinking they thereby clothe breath.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-1', 'en', 'Shvetaketu, son of Aruni, came to the assembly of the Panchalas. He went up to Pravahana Jaivali, who was being attended by his people. Seeing him, the king called out: ''Young man!'' He answered: ''Sir!'' ''Has your father taught you?'' ''Yes,'' he said.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-2', 'en', '''Do you know how people go different ways when they depart? How they come back to this world? Why that world is never filled though so many keep going there? After which offering the waters take on a human voice and rise up and speak? Do you know how one reaches the path of the gods or the path of the ancestors? We have heard the seer''s words: I have heard of two paths for mortals, of the ancestors and of the gods; by these the whole moving world goes, all that lies between father and mother.'' ''I do not know even one of these,'' he said.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-3', 'en', 'The king invited him to stay, but the boy ignored the invitation and ran off. He went to his father and said: ''So you told me before that I had been fully taught!'' ''What is it, wise one?'' ''That fellow of the warrior class asked me five questions and I did not know even one.'' ''What were they?'' He listed their headings.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-4', 'en', 'His father said: ''My son, you know me: whatever I knew, I told you all of it. But come, let us go there and live as students.'' ''You go, sir,'' said the boy. So Gautama went to where Pravahana Jaivali was. The king had a seat brought and water fetched for him, and gave him the guest offering. Then he said: ''We grant a boon to the revered Gautama.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-5', 'en', 'He said: ''That boon is promised to me. Tell me what you said to the boy.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-6', 'en', 'The king said: ''Gautama, that is among divine boons. Ask for a human one.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-7', 'en', 'Gautama said: ''You know I have plenty of gold, cows, horses, servant girls, retinues and clothing. Do not be ungenerous to me with what is great, infinite and boundless.'' ''Then, Gautama, you must ask in the proper way.'' ''I come to you as a student.'' In ancient times people became students just by saying so. So he lived there by declaring himself a student.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-8', 'en', 'The king said: ''Do not hold this against us, Gautama, nor your ancestors. This knowledge has never before lived with any priest. But I will tell it to you, for who could refuse you when you speak like this?''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-9', 'en', 'That world yonder is a fire, Gautama. The sun is its fuel, the rays its smoke, the day its flame, the directions its embers, the in-between directions its sparks. In this fire the gods offer faith. From that offering King Soma arises.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-10', 'en', 'The rain-cloud is a fire, Gautama. The year is its fuel, the clouds its smoke, lightning its flame, thunderbolts its embers, the rumblings its sparks. In this fire the gods offer King Soma. From that offering rain arises.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-11', 'en', 'This world is a fire, Gautama. The earth is its fuel, fire its smoke, night its flame, the moon its embers, the stars its sparks. In this fire the gods offer rain. From that offering food arises.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-12', 'en', 'Man is a fire, Gautama. His open mouth is its fuel, breath its smoke, speech its flame, the eye its embers, the ear its sparks. In this fire the gods offer food. From that offering semen arises.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-13', 'en', 'Woman is a fire, Gautama. In this fire the gods offer semen. From that offering a person is born. He lives as long as he lives. Then, when he dies...', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-14', 'en', '...they carry him to the fire. The fire becomes his fire, the fuel his fuel, the smoke his smoke, the flame his flame, the embers his embers, the sparks his sparks. In this fire the gods offer the person. From that offering the person arises, radiant in colour.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-15', 'en', 'Those who know this, and those who in the forest worship faith as truth, go into the flame, from the flame to the day, from the day to the bright half of the month, from that to the six months when the sun moves north, from those months to the world of the gods, from there to the sun, from the sun to the lightning. A person made of mind comes to those in the lightning and leads them to the worlds of Brahman. They live in those worlds of Brahman for the longest ages. For them there is no return.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-16', 'en', 'Those who win worlds through sacrifice, giving and austerity go into the smoke, from smoke to night, from night to the dark half of the month, from that to the six months when the sun moves south, from those months to the world of the ancestors, and from there to the moon. Reaching the moon they become food, and the gods feed on them there as on King Soma, saying ''grow, wane''. When that is used up, they pass into space, from space to wind, from wind to rain, from rain to earth. Reaching earth they become food. They are offered again in the fire of man, then born in the fire of woman. Rising up to the worlds, they go round in this way. But those who know neither path become worms, insects and biting creatures.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-1', 'en', 'If one wishes ''May I attain greatness'', then in the northern course of the sun, in the bright half of the month, on an auspicious day, after a twelve-day vow, one gathers all herbs and fruits in a cup or bowl of fig wood, sweeps and plasters the ground, lays the fire, spreads grass, prepares the butter and, under a male constellation, mixes the mantha drink and offers, saying: ''O Jatavedas, to all the gods in you who turn aside and thwart a person''s wishes, I offer their share. May they, pleased, satisfy me with all desires, svāhā. To you who lie across, thinking I am the one who decides all, I offer this stream of butter, you who bring success, svāhā.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-2', 'en', 'Saying ''Svāhā to the eldest, svāhā to the best'', one offers into the fire and pours the remainder into the mantha. Likewise with ''Svāhā to breath, svāhā to the richest'', ''Svāhā to speech, svāhā to the firm base'', ''Svāhā to the eye, svāhā to attainment'', ''Svāhā to the ear, svāhā to the resting place'', ''Svāhā to the mind, svāhā to procreation'', and ''Svāhā to semen''.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-3', 'en', 'Saying ''Svāhā to Agni'', one offers into the fire and pours the remainder into the mantha. Likewise ''Svāhā to Soma'', ''Svāhā to bhūḥ'', ''to bhuvaḥ'', ''to svaḥ'', ''to bhūr bhuvaḥ svaḥ'', ''to the priesthood'', ''to the ruling power'', ''to the past'', ''to the future'', ''to the universe'', ''to the all'', and ''Svāhā to Prajapati''.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-4', 'en', 'Then he touches the drink, saying: ''You are moving. You are glowing. You are full. You are steady. You are the one meeting place. You are the sound hiṅ and what is being sounded. You are the Udgitha and what is being sung. You are what is called out and what is answered. You are blazing in the moist. You are pervading, you are mighty. You are food, you are light. You are the end, you are the gathering-in.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-5', 'en', 'Then he raises it, saying: ''You know all, we know your greatness. He is king, lord and ruler. May he make me king, lord and ruler.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-6', 'en', 'Then he sips it, saying: ''That excellent glory of Savitri. Sweet blow the winds for the righteous, sweet flow the rivers; may the plants be sweet to us. Bhūḥ svāhā. Let us meditate on the light of the god. Sweet be the night and the dawns; sweet the dust of the earth; sweet be heaven our father. Bhuvaḥ svāhā. May he inspire our thoughts. Sweet be the tree to us, sweet the sun; may the cows be sweet to us. Svaḥ svāhā.'' He recites the whole Savitri and all the honey verses, saying ''May I become all this. Bhūr bhuvaḥ svaḥ svāhā.'' Finally he sips, washes his hands, and lies down behind the fire with his head to the east. In the morning he worships the sun: ''You are the one lotus of the directions; may I be the one lotus among men.'' Returning as he came, he sits behind the fire and recites the lineage.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-7', 'en', 'Uddalaka Aruni taught this to his pupil Yajnavalkya Vajasaneya and said: ''Even if one were to pour this on a dry stump, branches would grow and leaves would sprout.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-8', 'en', 'Yajnavalkya Vajasaneya taught this to his pupil Madhuka Paingya and said: ''Even if one were to pour this on a dry stump, branches would grow and leaves would sprout.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-9', 'en', 'Madhuka Paingya taught this to his pupil Chula Bhagavitti and said: ''Even if one were to pour this on a dry stump, branches would grow and leaves would sprout.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-10', 'en', 'Chula Bhagavitti taught this to his pupil Janaki Ayasthuna and said: ''Even if one were to pour this on a dry stump, branches would grow and leaves would sprout.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-11', 'en', 'Janaki Ayasthuna taught this to his pupil Satyakama Jabala and said: ''Even if one were to pour this on a dry stump, branches would grow and leaves would sprout.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-12', 'en', 'Satyakama Jabala taught this to his pupils and said: ''Even if one were to pour this on a dry stump, branches would grow and leaves would sprout.'' One should not teach it to anyone who is not one''s son or pupil.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-13', 'en', 'Four things are of fig wood: the ladle, the cup, the fuel and the two stirring sticks. There are ten kinds of village grain: rice and barley, sesame and beans, millet and panic seed, wheat, lentils, pulses and vetch. These are ground and soaked in curd, honey and butter, and an offering of butter is made.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-1', 'en', 'The essence of all these beings is earth; of earth, water; of water, plants; of plants, flowers; of flowers, fruits; of fruits, the human being; of the human being, semen.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-2', 'en', 'Prajapati thought: ''Let me prepare a firm base for it.'' So he created woman. Having created her, he honoured her. Therefore one should honour a woman. He brought forth from himself the stone for pressing Soma, and with it he united with her.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-3', 'en', 'The act of union is compared, part by part, to the altar and the implements of the Soma sacrifice. The world that comes to a sacrificer through the Vajapeya rite comes to one who knows this. One who engages in union knowing this takes on the merit of the woman; but whoever does it without this knowledge, the woman takes his merit.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-4', 'en', 'Uddalaka Aruni, Naka Maudgalya and Kumaraharita, who knew this, said: ''Many mortals, priests in name only, depart this world without strength and without merit because they engage in union without this knowledge.'' Much semen is spilled, whether one is asleep or awake.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-5', 'en', 'One should touch it or address it: ''The semen of mine that has fallen today on the earth, into the plants or the waters, I take back. May vigour, energy and fortune return to me. May the fires return to their places.'' Taking it with the ring finger and thumb, one should rub it between the breasts or the eyebrows.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-6', 'en', 'If one sees one''s reflection in water, one should say: ''In me be energy, vigour, fame, wealth and merit.'' A woman who has finished her period and put aside her soiled clothes is said to be fortunate. So one should approach such a woman, who is radiant, and speak to her.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-7', 'en', 'If she does not consent, he may try to win her with gifts. If she still does not consent, the text says he should overpower her, saying ''With my vigour and glory I take away your glory'', and she loses her glory.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-8', 'en', 'If she consents, he should say: ''With my vigour and glory I give you glory.'' Both then become glorious.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-9', 'en', 'If he wishes, ''May she love me'', he should, in union, join mouth to mouth and say: ''You arise from every limb; you are born from the heart. You are the essence of the limbs. Make her love me, as if struck by a poisoned arrow.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-10', 'en', 'If he wishes, ''May she not conceive'', in union he should breathe into her and draw breath back, saying: ''With my vigour and my seed I take back the seed.'' She then does not conceive.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-11', 'en', 'If he wishes, ''May she conceive'', in union he should draw breath back and then breathe into her, saying: ''With my vigour and my seed I place seed in you.'' She then conceives.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-12', 'en', 'If one''s wife has a lover and one hates him, one should lay a fire in an unbaked pot, spread reed grass in reverse order, and offer reed tips dipped in butter in reverse order, saying: ''You have offered in my kindled fire; I take away your breath, your children and cattle, your sacrifices and good works, your hopes and expectations, so-and-so.'' The one cursed by a priest who knows this leaves the world without strength and merit. So one should not seek to sport with the wife of a learned priest who knows this, for such a one becomes an enemy.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-13', 'en', 'When his wife has her period, for three days she should not drink from a metal cup and should wear fresh clothes. No low-caste man or woman should touch her. At the end of three nights, after bathing, she should have rice threshed.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-14', 'en', 'If one wishes, ''May a fair son be born to me, who will learn one Veda and live a full life'', the couple should have rice cooked in milk cooked and eat it with clarified butter. They will then be able to have such a child.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-15', 'en', 'If one wishes, ''May a son be born to me with a tawny, reddish-brown complexion, who will learn two Vedas and live a full life'', the couple should have rice cooked in curd cooked and eat it with clarified butter. They will then be able to have such a child.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-16', 'en', 'If one wishes, ''May a son be born to me of dark complexion with reddish eyes, who will learn three Vedas and live a full life'', the couple should have rice cooked in water cooked and eat it with clarified butter. They will then be able to have such a child.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-17', 'en', 'If one wishes, ''May a learned daughter be born to me who will live a full life'', the couple should have rice cooked with sesame cooked and eat it with clarified butter. They will then be able to have such a child.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-18', 'en', 'If one wishes, ''May a learned son be born to me, famous, a speaker at assemblies, who speaks words people want to hear, who will learn all the Vedas and live a full life'', the couple should have rice cooked with meat and eat it with clarified butter, the meat of a young or older bull. They will then be able to have such a son.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-19', 'en', 'Then at dawn, having prepared the pot of cooked food and the butter in the manner of the sthālīpāka rite, he offers portions, saying ''Svāhā to Agni, svāhā to Anumati, svāhā to the divine Savitri whose impulse is true.'' Having offered, he takes out the rest and eats it, and gives the rest to his wife. Washing his hands, he fills a water pot and sprinkles her three times, saying: ''Rise up from here, Vishvavasu; seek another young woman. This wife is with her husband.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-20', 'en', 'Then he embraces her, saying: ''I am Ama, you are Sa; you are Sa, I am Ama. I am the Saman, you are the Rik. I am the sky, you are the earth. Come, let us join and place the seed together, for a son, a male, for wealth.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-21', 'en', 'He says ''Separate, O heaven and earth'', and then, in union, joining mouth to mouth, strokes her three times, saying: ''May Vishnu prepare the womb, may Tvashtri shape the forms, may Prajapati pour in, may Dhatri place the embryo in you. Place the embryo, Sinivali; place the embryo, broad-tressed one. May the two Ashvin gods, garlanded with lotuses, place the embryo in you.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-22', 'en', '''The two golden fire-sticks with which the Ashvins churned out fire, that embryo we call into you, to be born in the tenth month. As the earth holds fire within, as the sky is pregnant with Indra''s thunder, as wind is the embryo of the directions, so I place an embryo in you, so-and-so.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-23', 'en', 'When she is about to give birth, he sprinkles her with water, saying: ''As the wind stirs a lotus pond on every side, so may your embryo stir and come out with the afterbirth. This is Indra''s enclosure, made with a bolt and a fence. Indra, drive it out, the child with the afterbirth.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-24', 'en', 'When the child is born, he lights a fire, places the child on his lap, mixes curd and butter in a metal bowl, and offers some, saying: ''May I prosper in my own house, nourishing a thousand. May fortune never leave his line, with offspring and cattle, svāhā. I offer to you in my mind the breaths that are in me, svāhā. Whatever I did too much in this rite, or too little, may wise Agni, maker of good offerings, make it well offered for us, svāhā.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-25', 'en', 'Then, putting his mouth to the child''s right ear, he says ''Speech, speech'' three times. Then he mixes curd, honey and butter and feeds the child with a gold spoon held directly, saying: ''I place bhūḥ in you, I place bhuvaḥ in you, I place svaḥ in you. Bhūr bhuvaḥ svaḥ, I place everything in you.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-26', 'en', 'Then he gives the child a name: ''You are Veda.'' This becomes the child''s secret name.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-27', 'en', 'Then he gives the child to the mother and offers the breast, saying: ''Your breast, Sarasvati, which is ever-flowing, which gives joy, which holds treasures, finds wealth and gives generously, with which you nourish all that is choice, give it here for the child to drink.''', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-28', 'en', 'Then he addresses the mother: ''You are Ila, daughter of Mitra and Varuna. Heroine, you have borne a hero. Be a mother of heroes, you who have made us rich in heroes.'' Of such a child people say: ''You have gone beyond your father! You have gone beyond your grandfather!'' Truly, the son born to a priest who knows this reaches the highest peak of fortune, fame and spiritual radiance.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-5-1', 'en', 'Now the lineage: the son of Pautimashi from the son of Katyayani, the son of Katyayani from the son of Gautami, the son of Gautami from the son of Bharadvaji, from the son of Parashari, from the son of Aupasvasti, from the son of Parashari, from the son of Katyayani, from the son of Kaushiki, from the son of Alambi and the son of Vaiyaghrapadi, from the son of Kanvi and the son of Kapi, the son of Kapi...', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-5-3', 'en', '...from Yajnavalkya, Yajnavalkya from Uddalaka, Uddalaka from Aruna, Aruna from Upaveshi, Upaveshi from Kushri, Kushri from Vajashravas, Vajashravas from Jihvavat Badhyoga, Jihvavat from Asita Varshagana, Asita from Harita Kashyapa, Harita from Shilpa Kashyapa, Shilpa from Kashyapa Naidhruvi, Kashyapa Naidhruvi from Speech, Speech from Ambhini, Ambhini from the Sun. These white Yajus formulas, which come from the Sun, are taught by Yajnavalkya Vajasaneya.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true),
('brihadaranyaka-6-5-4', 'en', 'It is the same up to the son of Sanjivi. The son of Sanjivi from Mandukayani, Mandukayani from Mandavya, Mandavya from Kautsa, Kautsa from Mahitthi, Mahitthi from Vamakakshayana, Vamakakshayana from Shandilya, Shandilya from Vatsya, Vatsya from Kushri, Kushri from Yajnavachas Rajastambayana, Yajnavachas from Tura Kavasheya, Tura from Prajapati, Prajapati from Brahman. Brahman is self-existent. Homage to Brahman.', 'veda-app-ai-draft', true);
insert into explanations (verse_id, language, meaning, explanation, keywords, source_id, status) values
('brihadaranyaka-1-1-1', 'en', 'The whole universe is pictured as the body of the sacred horse.', 'The Brihadaranyaka, the ''Great Forest Upanishad'', opens by turning the horse sacrifice into a meditation on the cosmos.', '[{"word":"aśva","meaning":"horse"},{"word":"medhya","meaning":"sacrificial"},{"word":"uṣas","meaning":"dawn"},{"word":"saṃvatsara","meaning":"year"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-1-2', 'en', 'Day and night are the two glories of the cosmic horse, born from the eastern and western seas.', 'Different names for the horse match the beings it carries.', '[{"word":"ahar","meaning":"day"},{"word":"rātri","meaning":"night"},{"word":"mahiman","meaning":"glory"},{"word":"samudra","meaning":"sea"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-2-1', 'en', 'In the beginning there was only death, which is hunger; from its wish for a self, water was born.', 'A creation story where death and hunger come first. Arka (fire, praise) is linked to arcat (worshipping) and ka (water, joy).', '[{"word":"mṛtyu","meaning":"death"},{"word":"aśanāyā","meaning":"hunger"},{"word":"arka","meaning":"fire, praise"},{"word":"āpaḥ","meaning":"water"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-2-2', 'en', 'The froth of the waters became earth, and from death''s effort came fire.', 'Creation continues: earth from water, fire from effort.', '[{"word":"śara","meaning":"froth"},{"word":"pṛthivī","meaning":"earth"},{"word":"śrānta","meaning":"toiling"},{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-2-3', 'en', 'Life divided itself into fire, sun and wind, and the world is its body.', 'The cosmos as a living creature again.', '[{"word":"tredhā","meaning":"three ways"},{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"breath, life"},{"word":"pratiṣṭhita","meaning":"resting"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-2-4', 'en', 'Death longed for a second self and gave birth to the year; the newborn''s cry became speech.', 'Time itself is born from death, and speech from the first cry.', '[{"word":"dvitīya","meaning":"second"},{"word":"saṃvatsara","meaning":"year"},{"word":"bhāṇ","meaning":"cry"},{"word":"vāc","meaning":"speech"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-2-5', 'en', 'Instead of eating the newborn, death created all things and began to eat them all.', 'Death is the eater of all; word-play on Aditi and atti, ''eats''.', '[{"word":"sṛj","meaning":"create"},{"word":"atti","meaning":"eats"},{"word":"aditi","meaning":"Aditi"},{"word":"anna","meaning":"food"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-2-6', 'en', 'Through effort his life-breaths left him and his body swelled, though his mind remained.', 'This prepares the explanation of the horse sacrifice''s name.', '[{"word":"tapas","meaning":"austerity"},{"word":"yaśas","meaning":"glory"},{"word":"vīrya","meaning":"vigour"},{"word":"śvayitum","meaning":"to swell"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-2-7', 'en', 'The swelling body became the horse; knowing this conquers death.', 'Word-play on ashva (horse) and ashvat (swelled). The sun is the true horse sacrifice.', '[{"word":"aśvamedha","meaning":"horse sacrifice"},{"word":"punarmṛtyu","meaning":"repeated death"},{"word":"tapati","meaning":"shines"},{"word":"medhya","meaning":"fit for sacrifice"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-1', 'en', 'Gods and demons, both children of the creator, fought for the worlds; the gods chose the sacred chant as their weapon.', 'A story about breath, also told in Chandogya 1.2.', '[{"word":"deva","meaning":"gods"},{"word":"asura","meaning":"demons"},{"word":"aspardhanta","meaning":"competed"},{"word":"udgītha","meaning":"chant"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-2', 'en', 'Speech sang for the gods, but the demons spoiled it with evil; that is why we sometimes says wrongly.', 'The gods try each power to sing the sacred chant against the demons. Each one is spoiled because it keeps something for itself.', '[{"word":"vāc","meaning":"speech"},{"word":"bhoga","meaning":"enjoyment"},{"word":"kalyāṇa","meaning":"good"},{"word":"pāpman","meaning":"evil"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-3', 'en', 'The breath of smell sang for the gods, but the demons spoiled it with evil; that is why we sometimes smells wrongly.', 'The gods try each power to sing the sacred chant against the demons. Each one is spoiled because it keeps something for itself.', '[{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"breath, smell"},{"word":"jighrati","meaning":"smells"},{"word":"pāpman","meaning":"evil"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-4', 'en', 'The eye sang for the gods, but the demons spoiled it with evil; that is why we sometimes sees wrongly.', 'The gods try each power to sing the sacred chant against the demons. Each one is spoiled because it keeps something for itself.', '[{"word":"cakṣus","meaning":"eye"},{"word":"paśyati","meaning":"sees"},{"word":"pāpman","meaning":"evil"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-5', 'en', 'The ear sang for the gods, but the demons spoiled it with evil; that is why we sometimes hears wrongly.', 'The gods try each power to sing the sacred chant against the demons. Each one is spoiled because it keeps something for itself.', '[{"word":"śrotra","meaning":"ear"},{"word":"śṛṇoti","meaning":"hears"},{"word":"pāpman","meaning":"evil"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-6', 'en', 'The mind too was spoiled by evil; that is why we imagine wrong things.', 'All five powers fail because they keep part of the reward for themselves.', '[{"word":"manas","meaning":"mind"},{"word":"saṃkalpayati","meaning":"imagines"},{"word":"pāpman","meaning":"evil"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-7', 'en', 'The breath of life could not be spoiled; the demons broke against it like clods against a rock.', 'The central breath (prana) keeps nothing for itself, so evil cannot touch it.', '[{"word":"āsanya prāṇa","meaning":"breath in the mouth"},{"word":"aśman","meaning":"rock"},{"word":"loṣṭa","meaning":"clod"},{"word":"vineśuḥ","meaning":"perished"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-8', 'en', 'The breath that saved them lives in the mouth and is the essence of the body.', 'Word-play: Ayasya (in the mouth) and Angirasa (essence of the limbs).', '[{"word":"āsya","meaning":"mouth"},{"word":"aṅga","meaning":"limb"},{"word":"rasa","meaning":"essence"},{"word":"āṅgirasa","meaning":"Angirasa"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-9', 'en', 'This breath is far from death, and so is the one who knows it.', 'Another name for breath, linked to ''far''.', '[{"word":"dūr","meaning":"far"},{"word":"mṛtyu","meaning":"death"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-10', 'en', 'Breath carried away evil and death to the edge of the world.', 'An old belief that the far edges of the world are places of ill fortune.', '[{"word":"apahatya","meaning":"taking away"},{"word":"diśām anta","meaning":"end of the directions"},{"word":"anvavāyāni","meaning":"meet"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-11', 'en', 'Breath then carried all the senses beyond death.', 'Each sense is now freed in turn.', '[{"word":"ativahat","meaning":"carried beyond"},{"word":"mṛtyu","meaning":"death"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-12', 'en', 'Freed from death, speech first. When speech became fire.', 'Breath carries each power beyond death, where it becomes its cosmic form.', '[{"word":"vāc","meaning":"speech"},{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"},{"word":"dīpyate","meaning":"shines"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-13', 'en', 'Freed from death, breath (smell) became wind.', 'Breath carries each power beyond death, where it becomes its cosmic form.', '[{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"breath"},{"word":"vāyu","meaning":"wind"},{"word":"pavate","meaning":"blows"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-14', 'en', 'Freed from death, the eye became the sun.', 'Breath carries each power beyond death, where it becomes its cosmic form.', '[{"word":"cakṣus","meaning":"eye"},{"word":"āditya","meaning":"sun"},{"word":"tapati","meaning":"burns"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-15', 'en', 'Freed from death, the ear became the directions.', 'Breath carries each power beyond death, where it becomes its cosmic form.', '[{"word":"śrotra","meaning":"ear"},{"word":"diś","meaning":"directions"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-16', 'en', 'Freed from death, the mind became the moon; the knower too is carried beyond death.', 'Each human power, freed, becomes a light of nature.', '[{"word":"manas","meaning":"mind"},{"word":"candramas","meaning":"moon"},{"word":"bhāti","meaning":"shines"},{"word":"ativahati","meaning":"carries beyond"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-17', 'en', 'Breath sang to win food, since all food is eaten by breath.', 'Breath keeps the body alive through food.', '[{"word":"annādya","meaning":"food"},{"word":"adyate","meaning":"is eaten"},{"word":"pratitiṣṭhati","meaning":"rests"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-18', 'en', 'The senses asked for a share of food and entered breath; so eating feeds them all.', 'Breath feeds every sense; the knower likewise becomes the support of his family.', '[{"word":"abhisaṃviśata","meaning":"enter"},{"word":"bhartṛ","meaning":"supporter"},{"word":"śreṣṭha","meaning":"best"},{"word":"bhārya","meaning":"dependant"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-19', 'en', 'Breath is the life of every limb; where it leaves, the limb withers.', 'A simple proof of breath''s importance.', '[{"word":"rasa","meaning":"essence"},{"word":"aṅga","meaning":"limb"},{"word":"śuṣyati","meaning":"dries up"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-20', 'en', 'Breath is the lord of speech, called Brihaspati.', 'Word-play on names of gods.', '[{"word":"bṛhatī","meaning":"speech, a metre"},{"word":"pati","meaning":"lord"},{"word":"bṛhaspati","meaning":"Brihaspati"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-21', 'en', 'Breath is also the lord of sacred speech.', 'Brahman here means the sacred word.', '[{"word":"brahman","meaning":"sacred word"},{"word":"brahmaṇaspati","meaning":"lord of prayer"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-22', 'en', 'Breath is the Saman chant, equal in every creature, small or great.', 'Breath is the same in a gnat and an elephant.', '[{"word":"sāman","meaning":"chant"},{"word":"sama","meaning":"equal"},{"word":"maśaka","meaning":"mosquito"},{"word":"nāga","meaning":"elephant"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-23', 'en', 'The chant is breath holding up the song of speech.', 'Breath and speech together make the sacred chant.', '[{"word":"ut","meaning":"up"},{"word":"uttabdha","meaning":"held up"},{"word":"gīthā","meaning":"song"},{"word":"udgītha","meaning":"chant"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-24', 'en', 'A priest swears that the chant is sung with nothing but speech and breath.', 'An oath confirming the teaching.', '[{"word":"soma","meaning":"Soma"},{"word":"bhakṣayan","meaning":"drinking"},{"word":"mūrdhan","meaning":"head"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-25', 'en', 'The wealth of the chant is its tone; a good voice is a priest''s treasure.', 'Practical advice for chanters.', '[{"word":"sva","meaning":"wealth"},{"word":"svara","meaning":"tone"},{"word":"ārtvijya","meaning":"priestly duty"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-26', 'en', 'The chant''s gold is also its tone.', 'Word-play on suvarna (gold) and svara (tone).', '[{"word":"suvarṇa","meaning":"gold"},{"word":"svara","meaning":"tone"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-3-27', 'en', 'The chant rests on speech.', 'The section ends with a small note of disagreement.', '[{"word":"pratiṣṭhā","meaning":"foundation"},{"word":"vāc","meaning":"speech"},{"word":"anna","meaning":"food"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-1', 'en', 'In the beginning was only the Self, who first said ''I am.''', 'A famous creation story: the first word was ''I''.', '[{"word":"ātman","meaning":"Self"},{"word":"puruṣavidha","meaning":"shaped like a person"},{"word":"aham asmi","meaning":"I am"},{"word":"oṣat","meaning":"burned"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-2', 'en', 'Alone, he felt fear, but realised there was nothing else to fear; fear only comes from another.', 'A deep insight: when one sees there is no ''other'', fear disappears.', '[{"word":"bibheti","meaning":"is afraid"},{"word":"ekākin","meaning":"alone"},{"word":"bhaya","meaning":"fear"},{"word":"dvitīya","meaning":"second"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-3', 'en', 'Lonely, he split into man and woman, and from them humans were born.', 'A creation story of love and union. Each partner is half of a whole.', '[{"word":"ramate","meaning":"has joy"},{"word":"pati","meaning":"husband"},{"word":"patnī","meaning":"wife"},{"word":"ardhabṛgala","meaning":"half a split pea"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-4', 'en', 'She hid in many forms, and he followed in matching forms, creating all the animals.', 'A playful myth explaining the variety of creatures.', '[{"word":"go","meaning":"cow"},{"word":"vṛṣabha","meaning":"bull"},{"word":"mithuna","meaning":"pair"},{"word":"pipīlikā","meaning":"ant"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-5', 'en', 'He realised that he himself was all of creation.', 'The creator and the creation are one.', '[{"word":"sṛṣṭi","meaning":"creation"},{"word":"asṛkṣi","meaning":"I created"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-6', 'en', 'He created fire and Soma; all gods are really his creation.', 'All the many gods are forms of the one Self.', '[{"word":"abhyamanthat","meaning":"churned"},{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"},{"word":"soma","meaning":"Soma"},{"word":"atisṛṣṭi","meaning":"higher creation"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-7', 'en', 'The Self entered everything; each sense shows only part of it, so worship the whole Self.', 'A key teaching: the Self is hidden in all things, and we see only its parts through our senses.', '[{"word":"nāmarūpa","meaning":"name and form"},{"word":"kṣura","meaning":"razor"},{"word":"akṛtsna","meaning":"incomplete"},{"word":"pada","meaning":"footprint"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-8', 'en', 'The Self is dearer than children or wealth, because it is our innermost being.', 'Everything else we love can be lost; the Self cannot.', '[{"word":"preyas","meaning":"dearer"},{"word":"putra","meaning":"son"},{"word":"vitta","meaning":"wealth"},{"word":"antaratara","meaning":"innermost"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-9', 'en', 'How did Brahman itself become everything?', 'A question introduces the answer in the next verse.', '[{"word":"brahmavidyā","meaning":"knowledge of Brahman"},{"word":"sarvam","meaning":"everything"},{"word":"abhavat","meaning":"became"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-10', 'en', 'Brahman knew itself as ''I am Brahman'' and became everything; whoever knows this becomes everything too.', '''Aham brahmasmi'', ''I am Brahman'', is one of the four great sayings (mahavakyas). Seeing oneself as separate from the divine keeps one small.', '[{"word":"ahaṃ brahmāsmi","meaning":"I am Brahman"},{"word":"pratyabudhyata","meaning":"awoke"},{"word":"vāmadeva","meaning":"Vamadeva"},{"word":"paśu","meaning":"domestic animal"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-11', 'en', 'Brahman created ruling power, but priestly wisdom remains its source.', 'The classes of society are traced to Brahman''s creative unfolding. Rulers must respect wisdom.', '[{"word":"kṣatra","meaning":"ruling power"},{"word":"brahman","meaning":"priestly power"},{"word":"yoni","meaning":"source"},{"word":"rājasūya","meaning":"royal consecration"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-12', 'en', 'Next Brahman created the people, matched by groups of gods.', 'The Vaishya class, the people, mirrored in the group gods.', '[{"word":"viś","meaning":"people"},{"word":"gaṇaśas","meaning":"in groups"},{"word":"vasu","meaning":"Vasus"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-13', 'en', 'Then Brahman created the class of workers, linked to the earth that nourishes all.', 'Pushan, ''nourisher'', is the earth that feeds everyone.', '[{"word":"śūdra","meaning":"Shudra"},{"word":"pūṣan","meaning":"nourisher"},{"word":"puṣyati","meaning":"nourishes"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-14', 'en', 'Above all power stands dharma, justice, which is the same as truth; through it the weak can stand against the strong.', 'A key verse: law and truth are higher than any ruler.', '[{"word":"dharma","meaning":"justice, right"},{"word":"kṣatrasya kṣatra","meaning":"power over power"},{"word":"abalīyas","meaning":"weaker"},{"word":"satya","meaning":"truth"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-15', 'en', 'All good works eventually run out; only the Self is an unfailing world.', 'Ritual merit is limited; knowledge of the Self is not.', '[{"word":"svaṃ loka","meaning":"one''s own world"},{"word":"kṣīyate","meaning":"runs out"},{"word":"ātman","meaning":"Self"},{"word":"puṇya karma","meaning":"holy work"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-16', 'en', 'A good householder supports gods, sages, ancestors, people and animals, and all beings wish him well.', 'This passage grounds the ''five great sacrifices'' of daily life: to gods, sages, ancestors, humans and animals.', '[{"word":"loka","meaning":"world, support"},{"word":"aśana","meaning":"food"},{"word":"tṛṇodaka","meaning":"grass and water"},{"word":"ariṣṭi","meaning":"safety"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-4-17', 'en', 'We long for spouse, children, wealth and work, but we already have all of them within ourselves.', 'Completeness is found inside: mind, speech, breath, eye and ear are our family and wealth.', '[{"word":"jāyā","meaning":"wife"},{"word":"prajā","meaning":"children"},{"word":"vitta","meaning":"wealth"},{"word":"pāṅkta","meaning":"fivefold"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-1', 'en', 'The creator made seven kinds of food; they never run out, and the verses ask why.', 'A riddle about food, explained in the following verses.', '[{"word":"sapta anna","meaning":"seven foods"},{"word":"medhā","meaning":"wisdom"},{"word":"akṣiti","meaning":"imperishability"},{"word":"ūrj","meaning":"strength"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-2', 'en', 'The verse is explained: food never runs out because people keep making it through thought and work.', 'A line-by-line explanation of the riddle.', '[{"word":"sādhāraṇa","meaning":"common"},{"word":"huta","meaning":"offering"},{"word":"payas","meaning":"milk"},{"word":"puruṣa","meaning":"person"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-3', 'en', 'Mind, speech and breath are the three foods the creator kept for himself; we are made of them.', 'A clear description of the mind as the seat of all feelings.', '[{"word":"manas","meaning":"mind"},{"word":"vicikitsā","meaning":"doubt"},{"word":"śraddhā","meaning":"faith"},{"word":"hrī","meaning":"shame"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-4', 'en', 'Speech, mind and breath match earth, sky and heaven.', 'The three foods are mapped onto the cosmos.', '[{"word":"vāc","meaning":"speech"},{"word":"manas","meaning":"mind"},{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"breath"},{"word":"loka","meaning":"world"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-5', 'en', 'Speech, mind and breath match the three Vedas.', 'The same triad applied to the Vedas.', '[{"word":"ṛgveda","meaning":"Rig Veda"},{"word":"yajurveda","meaning":"Yajur Veda"},{"word":"sāmaveda","meaning":"Sama Veda"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-6', 'en', 'They also match gods, ancestors and people.', 'The triad continues.', '[{"word":"deva","meaning":"gods"},{"word":"pitaraḥ","meaning":"ancestors"},{"word":"manuṣya","meaning":"humans"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-7', 'en', 'Mind is father, speech mother, breath their child.', 'A family image for our inner powers.', '[{"word":"pitṛ","meaning":"father"},{"word":"mātṛ","meaning":"mother"},{"word":"prajā","meaning":"child"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-8', 'en', 'What is known belongs to speech.', 'Speech, mind and breath match the known, the knowable and the unknown.', '[{"word":"vijñāta","meaning":"known"},{"word":"vāc","meaning":"speech"},{"word":"avati","meaning":"protects"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-9', 'en', 'What is still to be known belongs to mind.', 'Speech, mind and breath match the known, the knowable and the unknown.', '[{"word":"vijijñāsya","meaning":"to be known"},{"word":"manas","meaning":"mind"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-10', 'en', 'What is unknown belongs to breath.', 'Speech, mind and breath match the known, the knowable and the unknown.', '[{"word":"avijñāta","meaning":"unknown"},{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"breath"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-11', 'en', 'Speech has earth as its body and fire as its light.', 'Each inner power has a body and a light in nature.', '[{"word":"pṛthivī","meaning":"earth"},{"word":"śarīra","meaning":"body"},{"word":"jyotīrūpa","meaning":"form of light"},{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-12', 'en', 'Mind is heaven and sun; from mind and speech, breath was born, without rival.', 'Breath (Indra) is the child of mind and speech.', '[{"word":"dyau","meaning":"heaven"},{"word":"āditya","meaning":"sun"},{"word":"indra","meaning":"Indra"},{"word":"sapatna","meaning":"rival"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-13', 'en', 'Breath''s body is water and its light the moon; see them as endless and you win the endless.', 'How we see things shapes what we gain.', '[{"word":"āpaḥ","meaning":"water"},{"word":"candra","meaning":"moon"},{"word":"ananta","meaning":"endless"},{"word":"antavat","meaning":"finite"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-14', 'en', 'The creator, like the moon, has sixteen parts; on the new-moon night he lives in all beings, so harm none that night.', 'A lunar teaching with an ethical rule of non-harm.', '[{"word":"ṣoḍaśakala","meaning":"sixteen parts"},{"word":"amāvāsyā","meaning":"new moon"},{"word":"prāṇabhṛt","meaning":"breathing being"},{"word":"kṛkalāsa","meaning":"lizard"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-15', 'en', 'Wealth comes and goes like the rim of a wheel; the Self is the hub that remains.', 'A comforting image: loss of property is not loss of self.', '[{"word":"vitta","meaning":"wealth"},{"word":"nabhya","meaning":"hub"},{"word":"pradhi","meaning":"rim"},{"word":"jyāni","meaning":"loss"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-16', 'en', 'Children, works and knowledge win three different worlds; knowledge wins the best.', 'Knowledge is ranked highest.', '[{"word":"manuṣyaloka","meaning":"human world"},{"word":"pitṛloka","meaning":"ancestors'' world"},{"word":"devaloka","meaning":"gods'' world"},{"word":"vidyā","meaning":"knowledge"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-17', 'en', 'A dying father hands his learning, duties and world to his son.', 'A tender ritual of passing on one''s life''s work.', '[{"word":"sampratti","meaning":"handing over"},{"word":"putra","meaning":"son"},{"word":"muñcati","meaning":"frees"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-18', 'en', 'The father receives divine speech, whose words always come true.', 'After handing over, the father is filled with divine powers.', '[{"word":"daivī vāc","meaning":"divine speech"},{"word":"pṛthivī","meaning":"earth"},{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-19', 'en', 'He receives a divine mind, always joyful.', 'Divine mind knows no sorrow.', '[{"word":"daiva manas","meaning":"divine mind"},{"word":"ānandin","meaning":"joyful"},{"word":"na śocati","meaning":"does not grieve"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-20', 'en', 'He receives divine breath, becomes the Self of all, and only good reaches him.', 'The knower becomes one with the life of all beings.', '[{"word":"daiva prāṇa","meaning":"divine breath"},{"word":"vyathate","meaning":"is troubled"},{"word":"avanti","meaning":"care for"},{"word":"puṇya","meaning":"good"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-21', 'en', 'The senses tire, but breath never does; so all senses became forms of breath.', 'Like Chandogya 5.1: breath is the untiring centre of life.', '[{"word":"vrata","meaning":"vow"},{"word":"śrama","meaning":"weariness"},{"word":"madhyama prāṇa","meaning":"middle breath"},{"word":"śrāmyati","meaning":"grows weary"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-22', 'en', 'Among the gods of nature, wind never sets, just as breath never tires.', 'Wind is the outer counterpart of breath.', '[{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"},{"word":"āditya","meaning":"sun"},{"word":"vāyu","meaning":"wind"},{"word":"anastamita","meaning":"never setting"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-5-23', 'en', 'Keep one simple vow: breathe in and out with awareness. It guards against death.', 'Conscious breathing as a spiritual practice.', '[{"word":"prāṇyāt","meaning":"breathe in"},{"word":"apānyāt","meaning":"breathe out"},{"word":"vrata","meaning":"vow"},{"word":"sāyujya","meaning":"union"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-6-1', 'en', 'Everything is name, form or action; speech is the source of all names.', 'The world is analysed into three parts.', '[{"word":"nāma","meaning":"name"},{"word":"rūpa","meaning":"form"},{"word":"karma","meaning":"action"},{"word":"uktha","meaning":"source"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-6-2', 'en', 'The eye is the source of all forms.', 'The second of the three.', '[{"word":"cakṣus","meaning":"eye"},{"word":"rūpa","meaning":"form"},{"word":"bibharti","meaning":"supports"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-1-6-3', 'en', 'Name, form and action are one Self. The immortal life is hidden under names and forms.', 'Satya, ''the real'', here means the visible world, which covers the immortal.', '[{"word":"ātman","meaning":"Self"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"satya","meaning":"the real"},{"word":"channa","meaning":"covered"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-1', 'en', 'A proud scholar offers to teach a king about Brahman; the king is delighted.', 'Chapter 2 opens with the proud Gargya and the wise king Ajatashatru. The king hopes to rival King Janaka''s fame as a patron.', '[{"word":"dṛpta","meaning":"proud"},{"word":"gārgya","meaning":"Gargya"},{"word":"ajātaśatru","meaning":"Ajatashatru"},{"word":"sahasra","meaning":"thousand"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-2', 'en', 'Gargya worships the person in the sun; the king already knows this and more.', 'The proud scholar Gargya offers twelve definitions of Brahman; the king has already gone beyond each one.', '[{"word":"āditya","meaning":"sun"},{"word":"atiṣṭhāḥ","meaning":"standing above"},{"word":"mūrdhan","meaning":"head"},{"word":"rājan","meaning":"king"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-3', 'en', 'Gargya worships the person in the moon; the king already knows this and more.', 'The proud scholar Gargya offers twelve definitions of Brahman; the king has already gone beyond each one.', '[{"word":"candra","meaning":"moon"},{"word":"soma","meaning":"Soma"},{"word":"pāṇḍaravāsas","meaning":"white-robed"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-4', 'en', 'Gargya worships the person in lightning; the king already knows this and more.', 'The proud scholar Gargya offers twelve definitions of Brahman; the king has already gone beyond each one.', '[{"word":"vidyut","meaning":"lightning"},{"word":"tejasvin","meaning":"radiant"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-5', 'en', 'Gargya worships the person in space; the king already knows this and more.', 'The proud scholar Gargya offers twelve definitions of Brahman; the king has already gone beyond each one.', '[{"word":"ākāśa","meaning":"space"},{"word":"pūrṇa","meaning":"full"},{"word":"apravartin","meaning":"unmoving"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-6', 'en', 'Gargya worships the person in the wind; the king already knows this and more.', 'The proud scholar Gargya offers twelve definitions of Brahman; the king has already gone beyond each one.', '[{"word":"vāyu","meaning":"wind"},{"word":"indra","meaning":"Indra"},{"word":"aparājita","meaning":"unconquered"},{"word":"jiṣṇu","meaning":"victorious"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-7', 'en', 'Gargya worships the person in fire; the king already knows this and more.', 'Another of Gargya''s partial views of Brahman.', '[{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"},{"word":"viṣāsahi","meaning":"all-conquering"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-8', 'en', 'Gargya worships the person in water; the king already knows this and more.', 'Another of Gargya''s partial views of Brahman.', '[{"word":"āpaḥ","meaning":"water"},{"word":"pratirūpa","meaning":"likeness, fitting"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-9', 'en', 'Gargya worships the person in a mirror; the king already knows this and more.', 'Another of Gargya''s partial views of Brahman.', '[{"word":"ādarśa","meaning":"mirror"},{"word":"rociṣṇu","meaning":"shining"},{"word":"atirocate","meaning":"outshines"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-10', 'en', 'Gargya worships the person in the sound that follows a person as he walks; the king already knows this and more.', 'Another of Gargya''s partial views of Brahman.', '[{"word":"śabda","meaning":"sound"},{"word":"asu","meaning":"life"},{"word":"āyus","meaning":"lifespan"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-11', 'en', 'Gargya worships the person in the directions; the king already knows this and more.', 'Another of Gargya''s partial views of Brahman.', '[{"word":"diś","meaning":"directions"},{"word":"dvitīya","meaning":"companion"},{"word":"anapaga","meaning":"inseparable"},{"word":"gaṇa","meaning":"group"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-12', 'en', 'Gargya worships the person in the shadow; the king already knows this and more.', 'Another of Gargya''s partial views of Brahman.', '[{"word":"chāyā","meaning":"shadow"},{"word":"mṛtyu","meaning":"death"},{"word":"kāla","meaning":"time"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-13', 'en', 'Gargya''s last idea is also already known to the king, and Gargya falls silent.', 'The scholar has run out of answers.', '[{"word":"ātman","meaning":"self"},{"word":"ātmanvin","meaning":"self-possessed"},{"word":"tūṣṇīm","meaning":"silent"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-14', 'en', 'The king says this is not enough to know Brahman, and the scholar asks to become his student.', 'The proud teacher becomes a humble pupil.', '[{"word":"etāvat","meaning":"that much"},{"word":"viditam","meaning":"known"},{"word":"upayāni","meaning":"let me approach"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-15', 'en', 'The king takes Gargya to a sleeping man, who does not wake to grand names but only to a touch.', 'A demonstration about consciousness in sleep.', '[{"word":"pratiloma","meaning":"against the order"},{"word":"supta","meaning":"asleep"},{"word":"bodhayāṃ cakāra","meaning":"woke"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-16', 'en', 'The king asks: where was the conscious self while the man slept?', 'A key question about the nature of the self.', '[{"word":"vijñānamaya","meaning":"made of understanding"},{"word":"kva","meaning":"where"},{"word":"kutas","meaning":"from where"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-17', 'en', 'In sleep, the conscious self gathers in all the senses and rests in the space of the heart.', 'Sleep is the self withdrawing into its own centre.', '[{"word":"antar hṛdaya ākāśa","meaning":"space within the heart"},{"word":"gṛhṇāti","meaning":"takes in"},{"word":"svapiti","meaning":"sleeps"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-18', 'en', 'In dreams the self moves through its own body as a king moves through his kingdom.', 'Dreams are a world the self creates.', '[{"word":"svapna","meaning":"dream"},{"word":"mahārāja","meaning":"great king"},{"word":"janapada","meaning":"land"},{"word":"yathākāmam","meaning":"as he pleases"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-19', 'en', 'In deep dreamless sleep, the self rests in perfect bliss.', 'Deep sleep is pictured as the highest bliss of ordinary life.', '[{"word":"suṣupta","meaning":"deep sleep"},{"word":"hitā","meaning":"hita channels"},{"word":"dvāsaptati sahasra","meaning":"72,000"},{"word":"ānanda","meaning":"bliss"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-1-20', 'en', 'All things come from the Self like sparks from a fire; it is the truth of truth.', 'A famous image of creation. Satyasya satyam, ''the real behind the real''.', '[{"word":"ūrṇavābhi","meaning":"spider"},{"word":"visphuliṅga","meaning":"sparks"},{"word":"satyasya satyam","meaning":"the real behind the real"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-2-1', 'en', 'Breath is like a calf tied in the body, with food as its rope.', 'An image for the life-breath kept in the body.', '[{"word":"śiśu","meaning":"young one"},{"word":"ādhāna","meaning":"house"},{"word":"sthūṇā","meaning":"post"},{"word":"dāma","meaning":"rope"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-2-2', 'en', 'The gods serve life through the parts of the eye.', 'Each part of the eye is linked to a god.', '[{"word":"akṣiti","meaning":"imperishable"},{"word":"rudra","meaning":"Rudra"},{"word":"kanīnakā","meaning":"pupil"},{"word":"indra","meaning":"Indra"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-2-3', 'en', 'The head is like an upside-down bowl; the senses are the seven sages sitting on its rim.', 'A riddle verse explained.', '[{"word":"camasa","meaning":"bowl"},{"word":"yaśas","meaning":"glory"},{"word":"ṛṣi","meaning":"sage"},{"word":"saṃvidānā","meaning":"joined"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-2-4', 'en', 'The ears, eyes, nostrils and mouth are the seven great sages.', 'Each sense is named after one of the ancient seers.', '[{"word":"gotama","meaning":"Gotama"},{"word":"viśvāmitra","meaning":"Vishvamitra"},{"word":"atri","meaning":"Atri"},{"word":"atti","meaning":"eater"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-3-1', 'en', 'Brahman has two forms: one with shape and one without.', 'A key teaching about the two aspects of reality.', '[{"word":"mūrta","meaning":"formed"},{"word":"amūrta","meaning":"formless"},{"word":"martya","meaning":"mortal"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-3-2', 'en', 'Everything solid is the formed side; its essence is the sun.', 'In nature, the visible world is the formed side.', '[{"word":"mūrta","meaning":"formed"},{"word":"sthita","meaning":"still"},{"word":"rasa","meaning":"essence"},{"word":"tapati","meaning":"burns"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-3-3', 'en', 'Wind and sky are the formless side; its essence is the person in the sun.', 'The unseen world is the formless side.', '[{"word":"amūrta","meaning":"formless"},{"word":"vāyu","meaning":"wind"},{"word":"maṇḍala","meaning":"orb"},{"word":"tyat","meaning":"the beyond"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-3-4', 'en', 'In us, the body is the formed side, and its essence is the eye.', 'The same analysis applied to a person.', '[{"word":"adhyātma","meaning":"about the self"},{"word":"cakṣus","meaning":"eye"},{"word":"sat","meaning":"being"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-3-5', 'en', 'Breath and inner space are the formless side; its essence is the person in the eye.', 'The seer within the eye is the inner essence.', '[{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"breath"},{"word":"ākāśa","meaning":"space"},{"word":"dakṣiṇa akṣi","meaning":"right eye"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-3-6', 'en', 'Brahman can only be described as ''Not this, not this''; nothing is higher.', '''Neti neti'' is the famous method of describing the ultimate by denying every limited description.', '[{"word":"neti neti","meaning":"not this, not this"},{"word":"indragopa","meaning":"red beetle"},{"word":"puṇḍarīka","meaning":"white lotus"},{"word":"satyasya satyam","meaning":"real behind the real"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-1', 'en', 'Yajnavalkya is about to become a renunciant and wants to divide his property between his two wives.', 'The famous dialogue of Yajnavalkya and his wife Maitreyi begins.', '[{"word":"maitreyī","meaning":"Maitreyi"},{"word":"yājñavalkya","meaning":"Yajnavalkya"},{"word":"udyāsyan","meaning":"about to leave"},{"word":"kātyāyanī","meaning":"Katyayani"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-2', 'en', 'Maitreyi asks if wealth can make her immortal; Yajnavalkya says no.', 'One of the most famous questions in the Upanishads.', '[{"word":"vitta","meaning":"wealth"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"āśā","meaning":"hope"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-3', 'en', 'She refuses the wealth and asks for his wisdom instead.', 'Maitreyi chooses the deeper treasure.', '[{"word":"kuryām","meaning":"would I do"},{"word":"brūhi","meaning":"tell"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-4', 'en', 'Yajnavalkya is moved by her question and agrees to teach her.', 'Affection and wisdom meet.', '[{"word":"priyā","meaning":"dear"},{"word":"āssva","meaning":"sit"},{"word":"nididhyāsasva","meaning":"meditate deeply"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-5', 'en', 'We love everything because of the Self; so the Self is what we should come to know.', 'One of the most quoted passages: all love is at root love of the Self. Hearing, reflecting and meditating became the classic three steps of spiritual study.', '[{"word":"ātman","meaning":"Self"},{"word":"priya","meaning":"dear"},{"word":"draṣṭavya","meaning":"to be seen"},{"word":"nididhyāsitavya","meaning":"to be meditated on"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-6', 'en', 'Whoever sees anything as separate from the Self loses it; everything is the Self.', 'Seeing things as separate leads to loss.', '[{"word":"parādāt","meaning":"abandons"},{"word":"anyatra","meaning":"other than"},{"word":"sarvam","meaning":"all"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-7', 'en', 'You cannot catch the sounds of a drum one by one, only by holding the drum itself.', 'Yajnavalkya''s images: grasp the source and you grasp everything that comes from it. Here the source is the Self.', '[{"word":"dundubhi","meaning":"drum"},{"word":"śabda","meaning":"sound"},{"word":"grahaṇa","meaning":"catching"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-8', 'en', 'You cannot catch the sounds of a conch one by one, only by holding the conch itself.', 'Yajnavalkya''s images: grasp the source and you grasp everything that comes from it. Here the source is the Self.', '[{"word":"śaṅkha","meaning":"conch"},{"word":"dhmāyamāna","meaning":"being blown"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-9', 'en', 'You cannot catch the sounds of a lute one by one, only by holding the lute itself.', 'Yajnavalkya''s images: grasp the source and you grasp everything that comes from it. Here the source is the Self.', '[{"word":"vīṇā","meaning":"lute"},{"word":"vādyamāna","meaning":"being played"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-10', 'en', 'All sacred knowledge is the breath of the great Being.', 'Even the Vedas are not separate from the Self.', '[{"word":"dhūma","meaning":"smoke"},{"word":"mahad bhūta","meaning":"great Being"},{"word":"niḥśvasita","meaning":"breath"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-11', 'en', 'Each sense gathers its own experiences, as the ocean gathers all waters.', 'Everything flows back to a single centre, as rivers to the sea.', '[{"word":"samudra","meaning":"ocean"},{"word":"ekāyana","meaning":"meeting place"},{"word":"tvac","meaning":"skin"},{"word":"hṛdaya","meaning":"heart"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-12', 'en', 'The great Being is pure awareness, like salt dissolved in water; after death there is no separate awareness.', 'Like Chandogya 6.13, the Self pervades everything. Individual consciousness dissolves at death.', '[{"word":"saindhava","meaning":"salt"},{"word":"vijñānaghana","meaning":"mass of awareness"},{"word":"saṃjñā","meaning":"awareness"},{"word":"pretya","meaning":"after death"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-13', 'en', 'Maitreyi is puzzled; Yajnavalkya says it will become clear.', 'An honest moment of confusion.', '[{"word":"amūmuhat","meaning":"confused"},{"word":"moha","meaning":"confusion"},{"word":"alam","meaning":"enough"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-4-14', 'en', 'Awareness of ''others'' needs two; when all is the Self, there is no other. And no one can know the knower itself.', 'A profound teaching: the knower can never be an object of knowledge.', '[{"word":"dvaita","meaning":"duality"},{"word":"itara","meaning":"other"},{"word":"vijñātṛ","meaning":"knower"},{"word":"kena","meaning":"by what"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-1', 'en', 'This earth and all beings feed each other like honey; the light in it and in us is the same Self.', 'The ''honey doctrine'' (madhu vidya): everything is interdependent, and the same Self shines in all.', '[{"word":"madhu","meaning":"honey"},{"word":"pṛthivī","meaning":"earth"},{"word":"tejomaya","meaning":"shining"},{"word":"amṛtamaya","meaning":"immortal"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-2', 'en', 'These waters and all beings feed each other like honey; the light in it and in us is the same Self.', 'The ''honey doctrine'' (madhu vidya): everything is interdependent, and the same Self shines in all.', '[{"word":"āpaḥ","meaning":"waters"},{"word":"retas","meaning":"semen"},{"word":"madhu","meaning":"honey"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-3', 'en', 'This fire and all beings feed each other; the light in it and the light made of speech in oneself are the same Self.', 'The honey teaching continues: every part of nature is linked to a part of us, and the same Self shines in both.', '[{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"},{"word":"vāṅmaya","meaning":"made of speech"},{"word":"madhu","meaning":"honey"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-4', 'en', 'This wind and all beings feed each other; the light in it and the light that is breath in oneself are the same Self.', 'The honey teaching continues: every part of nature is linked to a part of us, and the same Self shines in both.', '[{"word":"vāyu","meaning":"wind"},{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"breath"},{"word":"madhu","meaning":"honey"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-5', 'en', 'This sun and all beings feed each other; the light in it and the light in the eye are the same Self.', 'The honey teaching continues: every part of nature is linked to a part of us, and the same Self shines in both.', '[{"word":"āditya","meaning":"sun"},{"word":"cākṣuṣa","meaning":"of the eye"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-6', 'en', 'These directions and all beings feed each other; the light in it and the light in the ear and in the echo are the same Self.', 'The honey teaching continues: every part of nature is linked to a part of us, and the same Self shines in both.', '[{"word":"diś","meaning":"directions"},{"word":"śrotra","meaning":"ear"},{"word":"prātiśrutka","meaning":"echo"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-7', 'en', 'This moon and all beings feed each other; the light in it and the light in the mind are the same Self.', 'The honey teaching continues: every part of nature is linked to a part of us, and the same Self shines in both.', '[{"word":"candra","meaning":"moon"},{"word":"mānasa","meaning":"of the mind"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-8', 'en', 'This lightning and all beings feed each other; the light in it and the light in one''s own radiance are the same Self.', 'The honey teaching continues: every part of nature is linked to a part of us, and the same Self shines in both.', '[{"word":"vidyut","meaning":"lightning"},{"word":"taijasa","meaning":"radiant"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-9', 'en', 'This thunder and all beings feed each other; the light in it and the light in sound and tone are the same Self.', 'The honey teaching continues: every part of nature is linked to a part of us, and the same Self shines in both.', '[{"word":"stanayitnu","meaning":"thunder"},{"word":"śabda","meaning":"sound"},{"word":"svara","meaning":"tone"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-10', 'en', 'This space and all beings feed each other; the light in it and the light in the space of the heart are the same Self.', 'The honey teaching continues: every part of nature is linked to a part of us, and the same Self shines in both.', '[{"word":"ākāśa","meaning":"space"},{"word":"hṛdy ākāśa","meaning":"space in the heart"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-11', 'en', 'This dharma and all beings feed each other; the light in it and the light in one''s own sense of right are the same Self.', 'The honey teaching continues: every part of nature is linked to a part of us, and the same Self shines in both.', '[{"word":"dharma","meaning":"right, justice"},{"word":"dhārma","meaning":"righteous"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-12', 'en', 'This truth and all beings feed each other; the light in it and the light in one''s own truthfulness are the same Self.', 'The honey teaching continues: every part of nature is linked to a part of us, and the same Self shines in both.', '[{"word":"satya","meaning":"truth"},{"word":"sātya","meaning":"truthful"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-13', 'en', 'This humanity and all beings feed each other; the light in it and the light in oneself as a human being are the same Self.', 'The honey teaching continues: every part of nature is linked to a part of us, and the same Self shines in both.', '[{"word":"mānuṣa","meaning":"humanity"},{"word":"madhu","meaning":"honey"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-14', 'en', 'The Self and all beings are honey to each other; everything is one Self.', 'The honey teaching reaches its peak: the Self itself.', '[{"word":"ātman","meaning":"Self"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"sarvam","meaning":"all"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-15', 'en', 'The Self holds all things together, like the hub of a wheel holds its spokes.', 'A classic image of unity.', '[{"word":"adhipati","meaning":"ruler"},{"word":"rathanābhi","meaning":"wheel hub"},{"word":"nemi","meaning":"rim"},{"word":"ara","meaning":"spoke"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-16', 'en', 'The Ashvins learned the honey teaching from a sage speaking through a horse''s head.', 'A Rig Veda verse is quoted about the sage Dadhyanch, who taught the honey teaching through a horse''s head.', '[{"word":"madhu","meaning":"honey"},{"word":"dadhyañc","meaning":"Dadhyanch"},{"word":"aśvin","meaning":"Ashvins"},{"word":"śīrṣan","meaning":"head"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-17', 'en', 'The Ashvins gave the sage a horse''s head so he could teach them safely.', 'A Rig Veda verse is quoted about the sage Dadhyanch, who taught the honey teaching through a horse''s head.', '[{"word":"tvāṣṭra","meaning":"of Tvashtri"},{"word":"ṛtāyan","meaning":"keeping his word"},{"word":"dasra","meaning":"wonder-workers"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-18', 'en', 'The Person entered every body; nothing is outside him.', 'Word-play: purusha as puri-shaya, ''dwelling in the city'' of the body.', '[{"word":"pur","meaning":"city, body"},{"word":"puruṣa","meaning":"person"},{"word":"puriśaya","meaning":"dwelling in the body"},{"word":"anāvṛta","meaning":"uncovered"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-5-19', 'en', 'The one Self takes every form; it has no inside or outside. This Self is Brahman.', '''Ayam atma brahma'', ''This Self is Brahman'', is another of the four great sayings.', '[{"word":"pratirūpa","meaning":"counterpart"},{"word":"māyā","meaning":"power"},{"word":"apūrva","meaning":"without before"},{"word":"ayam ātmā brahma","meaning":"this Self is Brahman"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-6-1', 'en', 'A list of the teachers who passed this teaching down.', 'Lineage lists (vamsha) honour the chain of teachers. This is mainly a list of names.', '[{"word":"vaṃśa","meaning":"line of teachers"},{"word":"gaupavana","meaning":"Gaupavana"},{"word":"śāṇḍilya","meaning":"Shandilya"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-6-2', 'en', 'The list of teachers continues.', 'A continuing list of names.', '[{"word":"āgniveśya","meaning":"Agniveshya"},{"word":"bhāradvāja","meaning":"Bharadvaja"},{"word":"pārāśarya","meaning":"Parasharya"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-2-6-3', 'en', 'The chain of teachers goes back through many sages to Brahman itself. Homage to Brahman.', 'The list ends at Brahman, the self-existent source of all teaching.', '[{"word":"svayaṃbhu","meaning":"self-existent"},{"word":"parameṣṭhin","meaning":"the highest"},{"word":"brahmaṇe namaḥ","meaning":"homage to Brahman"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-1', 'en', 'King Janaka offers a thousand cows with gold to the most learned scholar.', 'Chapter 3 begins: the great debate at Janaka''s court, where Yajnavalkya faces many challengers.', '[{"word":"janaka","meaning":"Janaka"},{"word":"videha","meaning":"Videha"},{"word":"anūcānatama","meaning":"most learned"},{"word":"sahasra","meaning":"thousand"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-2', 'en', 'Yajnavalkya boldly takes the prize, joking that he just wants the cows; the others challenge him.', 'Yajnavalkya''s humour and confidence. The questioning begins.', '[{"word":"brahmiṣṭha","meaning":"best knower of Brahman"},{"word":"udaja","meaning":"drive away"},{"word":"cukrudhuḥ","meaning":"were angry"},{"word":"aśvala","meaning":"Ashvala"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-3', 'en', 'Freedom from death comes through speech, which is fire.', 'Each priest of the sacrifice is linked to a power that frees from limitation.', '[{"word":"mṛtyu","meaning":"death"},{"word":"hotṛ","meaning":"reciting priest"},{"word":"vāc","meaning":"speech"},{"word":"atimukti","meaning":"complete freedom"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-4', 'en', 'Freedom from day and night comes through the eye, which is the sun.', 'The second freedom.', '[{"word":"ahorātra","meaning":"day and night"},{"word":"adhvaryu","meaning":"officiating priest"},{"word":"cakṣus","meaning":"eye"},{"word":"āditya","meaning":"sun"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-5', 'en', 'Freedom from the moon''s phases comes through breath, which is wind.', 'The third freedom.', '[{"word":"pūrvapakṣa","meaning":"bright half"},{"word":"aparapakṣa","meaning":"dark half"},{"word":"udgātṛ","meaning":"chanting priest"},{"word":"vāyu","meaning":"wind"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-6', 'en', 'The mind, which is the moon, is the ladder to heaven.', 'The fourth freedom.', '[{"word":"antarikṣa","meaning":"mid-air"},{"word":"ākrama","meaning":"ladder"},{"word":"manas","meaning":"mind"},{"word":"candra","meaning":"moon"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-7', 'en', 'The hotri uses three verses and wins all living beings.', 'Ashvala''s questions move to ritual details.', '[{"word":"puronuvākyā","meaning":"introductory verse"},{"word":"yājyā","meaning":"offering verse"},{"word":"śasyā","meaning":"praise verse"},{"word":"prāṇabhṛt","meaning":"breathing being"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-8', 'en', 'Three kinds of offering win the worlds of gods, ancestors and humans.', 'Each kind of offering matches a world.', '[{"word":"āhuti","meaning":"offering"},{"word":"ujjvalanti","meaning":"blaze up"},{"word":"devaloka","meaning":"world of gods"},{"word":"manuṣyaloka","meaning":"human world"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-9', 'en', 'The mind alone guards the rite, and since it is endless it wins an endless world.', 'The mind is unlimited.', '[{"word":"manas","meaning":"mind"},{"word":"ananta","meaning":"endless"},{"word":"viśve devāḥ","meaning":"All-gods"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-1-10', 'en', 'The three hymns are the three breaths, and they win earth, sky and heaven. Ashvala falls silent.', 'Yajnavalkya answers every question; the first challenger gives up.', '[{"word":"stotriyā","meaning":"hymn of praise"},{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"in-breath"},{"word":"apāna","meaning":"out-breath"},{"word":"upararāma","meaning":"fell silent"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-1', 'en', 'A second challenger asks about the ''graspers'' and ''over-graspers''.', 'The senses and their objects are discussed as captors and captives.', '[{"word":"graha","meaning":"grasper"},{"word":"atigraha","meaning":"over-grasper"},{"word":"ārtabhāga","meaning":"Artabhaga"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-2', 'en', 'Breath (smell) grasps, but is itself held by odour, the out-breath.', 'Each sense ''grasps'' the world but is in turn held captive by its object.', '[{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"smell"},{"word":"gandha","meaning":"odour"},{"word":"jighrati","meaning":"smells"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-3', 'en', 'Speech grasps, but is itself held by name.', 'Each sense ''grasps'' the world but is in turn held captive by its object.', '[{"word":"vāc","meaning":"speech"},{"word":"nāman","meaning":"name"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-4', 'en', 'The tongue grasps, but is itself held by taste.', 'Each sense ''grasps'' the world but is in turn held captive by its object.', '[{"word":"jihvā","meaning":"tongue"},{"word":"rasa","meaning":"taste"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-5', 'en', 'The eye grasps, but is itself held by form.', 'Each sense ''grasps'' the world but is in turn held captive by its object.', '[{"word":"cakṣus","meaning":"eye"},{"word":"rūpa","meaning":"form"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-6', 'en', 'The ear grasps, but is itself held by sound.', 'Each sense ''grasps'' the world but is in turn held captive by its object.', '[{"word":"śrotra","meaning":"ear"},{"word":"śabda","meaning":"sound"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-7', 'en', 'The mind grasps, but is itself held by desire.', 'Each sense ''grasps'' the world but is in turn held captive by its object.', '[{"word":"manas","meaning":"mind"},{"word":"kāma","meaning":"desire"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-8', 'en', 'The hands grasps, but is itself held by action.', 'Each sense ''grasps'' the world but is in turn held captive by its object.', '[{"word":"hasta","meaning":"hands"},{"word":"karman","meaning":"action"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-9', 'en', 'The skin grasps but is held by touch. These are the eight.', 'The list is complete.', '[{"word":"tvac","meaning":"skin"},{"word":"sparśa","meaning":"touch"},{"word":"aṣṭau","meaning":"eight"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-10', 'en', 'Even death has something that consumes it: fire, quenched by water.', 'Death itself can be overcome.', '[{"word":"mṛtyu","meaning":"death"},{"word":"anna","meaning":"food"},{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"},{"word":"āpaḥ","meaning":"water"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-11', 'en', 'At death the breaths do not leave the body; they gather there.', 'An answer about the process of death.', '[{"word":"mriyate","meaning":"dies"},{"word":"utkrāmanti","meaning":"leave"},{"word":"samavanīyante","meaning":"gather"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-12', 'en', 'When a person dies, his name remains.', 'One''s name lives on after death.', '[{"word":"nāman","meaning":"name"},{"word":"ananta","meaning":"endless"},{"word":"jahāti","meaning":"leaves"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-2-13', 'en', 'After death, what remains is one''s karma: good deeds make one good, bad deeds bad.', 'The first clear statement of the law of karma, discussed privately.', '[{"word":"karman","meaning":"action"},{"word":"puṇya","meaning":"good"},{"word":"pāpa","meaning":"bad"},{"word":"sajana","meaning":"in a crowd"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-3-1', 'en', 'A scholar asks Yajnavalkya where the famous Parikshit kings went after death.', 'Bhujyu tests Yajnavalkya with a question learned from a spirit.', '[{"word":"gandharva","meaning":"spirit"},{"word":"pārikṣita","meaning":"Parikshitas"},{"word":"bhujyu","meaning":"Bhujyu"},{"word":"loka","meaning":"world"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-3-2', 'en', 'The Parikshitas went through a narrow gap at the world''s edge, carried by the wind.', 'A cosmological answer; wind is both the part and the whole.', '[{"word":"aśvamedha","meaning":"horse sacrifice"},{"word":"kṣura","meaning":"razor"},{"word":"vyaṣṭi","meaning":"individual"},{"word":"samaṣṭi","meaning":"whole"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-4-1', 'en', 'The Self within everything is the one that breathes in all your breaths.', 'Ushasta (met in Chandogya 1.10) asks for Brahman ''directly, not hidden''.', '[{"word":"sākṣāt","meaning":"directly"},{"word":"aparokṣa","meaning":"not hidden"},{"word":"sarvāntara","meaning":"within all"},{"word":"prāṇiti","meaning":"breathes"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-4-2', 'en', 'You cannot see the seer or know the knower; that is the Self within all.', 'A key point: the Self is the subject and can never become an object.', '[{"word":"draṣṭṛ","meaning":"seer"},{"word":"śrotṛ","meaning":"hearer"},{"word":"vijñātṛ","meaning":"knower"},{"word":"ārta","meaning":"suffering"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-5-1', 'en', 'The Self is beyond hunger, sorrow and death; knowing it, the wise give up desire and live simply, like a child.', 'A classic text on renunciation and on going beyond mere learning.', '[{"word":"eṣaṇā","meaning":"desire"},{"word":"bhikṣācarya","meaning":"living on alms"},{"word":"bālya","meaning":"childlikeness"},{"word":"muni","meaning":"sage"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-6-1', 'en', 'Gargi keeps asking what each world rests on, until Yajnavalkya stops her at Brahman.', 'Gargi, a brilliant woman philosopher, pushes the questioning to its limit. She will return.', '[{"word":"ota","meaning":"woven"},{"word":"prota","meaning":"cross-woven"},{"word":"gārgī","meaning":"Gargi"},{"word":"atipraśna","meaning":"asking too much"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-1', 'en', 'Uddalaka challenges Yajnavalkya: what is the thread that holds everything, and who is the inner controller?', 'Uddalaka, the great teacher of Chandogya 6, now tests Yajnavalkya.', '[{"word":"sūtra","meaning":"thread"},{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"saṃdṛbdha","meaning":"strung together"},{"word":"brahmavid","meaning":"knower of Brahman"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-2', 'en', 'Air, or wind, is the thread that holds all things together.', 'The thread is air, the breath of the world.', '[{"word":"vāyu","meaning":"air"},{"word":"sūtra","meaning":"thread"},{"word":"vyasraṃsiṣata","meaning":"came loose"},{"word":"preta","meaning":"dead"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-3', 'en', 'The Self lives inside the earth and guides it, though the earth does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"pṛthivī","meaning":"earth"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-4', 'en', 'The Self lives inside the waters and guides it, though the waters does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"āpaḥ","meaning":"waters"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-5', 'en', 'The Self lives inside fire and guides it, though fire does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-6', 'en', 'The Self lives inside the mid-air and guides it, though the mid-air does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"antarikṣa","meaning":"mid-air"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-7', 'en', 'The Self lives inside the wind and guides it, though the wind does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"vāyu","meaning":"wind"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-8', 'en', 'The Self lives inside heaven and guides it, though heaven does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"dyau","meaning":"heaven"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-9', 'en', 'The Self lives inside the sun and guides it, though the sun does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"āditya","meaning":"sun"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-10', 'en', 'The Self lives inside the directions and guides it, though the directions does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"diś","meaning":"directions"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-11', 'en', 'The Self lives inside the moon and stars and guides it, though the moon and stars does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"candratāraka","meaning":"moon and stars"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-12', 'en', 'The Self lives inside space and guides it, though space does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"ākāśa","meaning":"space"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-13', 'en', 'The Self lives inside darkness and guides it, though darkness does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"tamas","meaning":"darkness"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-14', 'en', 'The Self lives inside light and guides it, though light does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"tejas","meaning":"light"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-15', 'en', 'The Self dwells in and guides all living beings.', 'The inner controller in all creatures.', '[{"word":"bhūta","meaning":"beings"},{"word":"adhibhūta","meaning":"about beings"},{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-16', 'en', 'The Self lives inside breath and guides it, though breath does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"breath"},{"word":"adhyātma","meaning":"about the self"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-17', 'en', 'The Self lives inside speech and guides it, though speech does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"vāc","meaning":"speech"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-18', 'en', 'The Self lives inside the eye and guides it, though the eye does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"cakṣus","meaning":"eye"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-19', 'en', 'The Self lives inside the ear and guides it, though the ear does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"śrotra","meaning":"ear"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-20', 'en', 'The Self lives inside the mind and guides it, though the mind does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"manas","meaning":"mind"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-21', 'en', 'The Self lives inside the skin and guides it, though the skin does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"tvac","meaning":"skin"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-22', 'en', 'The Self lives inside understanding and guides it, though understanding does not know it.', 'The ''inner controller'' (antaryamin) teaching: the one Self dwells in and rules every part of the world and of us, unseen.', '[{"word":"antaryāmin","meaning":"inner controller"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yamayati","meaning":"controls"},{"word":"vijñāna","meaning":"understanding"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-7-23', 'en', 'The inner controller is the unseen seer and unknown knower; there is no other.', 'The great teaching ends: the Self is the only true witness in all.', '[{"word":"adṛṣṭa","meaning":"unseen"},{"word":"draṣṭṛ","meaning":"seer"},{"word":"vijñātṛ","meaning":"knower"},{"word":"ārta","meaning":"suffering"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-1', 'en', 'Gargi returns with two final questions.', 'Gargi stakes everyone''s reputation on her questions.', '[{"word":"praśna","meaning":"question"},{"word":"brahmodya","meaning":"debate about Brahman"},{"word":"jetṛ","meaning":"winner"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-2', 'en', 'Gargi compares her questions to a warrior''s arrows.', 'A vivid image of intellectual combat.', '[{"word":"dhanus","meaning":"bow"},{"word":"bāṇa","meaning":"arrow"},{"word":"upottiṣṭhet","meaning":"rises against"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-3', 'en', 'Gargi asks: what holds together everything in space and time?', 'Her first question.', '[{"word":"bhūta","meaning":"past"},{"word":"bhavat","meaning":"present"},{"word":"bhaviṣyat","meaning":"future"},{"word":"ota","meaning":"woven"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-4', 'en', 'Everything in space and time rests on space.', 'His first answer.', '[{"word":"ākāśa","meaning":"space"},{"word":"ota prota","meaning":"woven back and forth"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-5', 'en', 'Gargi accepts the answer and prepares her second question.', 'Graceful even in debate.', '[{"word":"namas","meaning":"bow"},{"word":"dhārayasva","meaning":"get ready"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-6', 'en', 'She repeats her question to lead to the deeper one.', 'She sets up the final question.', '[{"word":"ota","meaning":"woven"},{"word":"prota","meaning":"cross-woven"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-7', 'en', 'Gargi asks: and what holds space?', 'Her second, deeper question.', '[{"word":"ākāśa","meaning":"space"},{"word":"kasmin","meaning":"on what"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-8', 'en', 'Space rests on the Imperishable, which can only be described by what it is not.', 'The akshara, ''imperishable'', is Brahman described through negation.', '[{"word":"akṣara","meaning":"the Imperishable"},{"word":"asthūla","meaning":"not coarse"},{"word":"anaṇu","meaning":"not fine"},{"word":"abāhya","meaning":"without outside"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-9', 'en', 'Everything in the universe keeps its order by the command of the Imperishable.', 'The Imperishable is the source of cosmic law and order.', '[{"word":"praśāsana","meaning":"command"},{"word":"vidhṛta","meaning":"held apart"},{"word":"nadī","meaning":"river"},{"word":"parvata","meaning":"mountain"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-10', 'en', 'Without knowing the Imperishable, even endless good works end; knowing it makes one truly wise.', 'Knowledge, not ritual, is what counts.', '[{"word":"antavat","meaning":"having an end"},{"word":"kṛpaṇa","meaning":"pitiable"},{"word":"brāhmaṇa","meaning":"true knower"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-11', 'en', 'The Imperishable is the unseen seer; space itself rests on it.', 'This matches the inner controller of 3.7.23.', '[{"word":"akṣara","meaning":"Imperishable"},{"word":"adṛṣṭa","meaning":"unseen"},{"word":"draṣṭṛ","meaning":"seer"},{"word":"ākāśa","meaning":"space"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-8-12', 'en', 'Gargi tells the others that no one can defeat Yajnavalkya.', 'Gargi graciously concedes.', '[{"word":"namaskāra","meaning":"bowing"},{"word":"mucyedhvam","meaning":"get away"},{"word":"upararāma","meaning":"fell silent"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-1', 'en', 'Asked how many gods there are, Yajnavalkya counts down from thousands to one.', 'A famous passage showing that the many gods are forms of the One.', '[{"word":"deva","meaning":"god"},{"word":"trayastriṃśat","meaning":"thirty-three"},{"word":"eka","meaning":"one"},{"word":"nivid","meaning":"invocation"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-2', 'en', 'The thirty-three gods are the Vasus, Rudras, Adityas, Indra and Prajapati.', 'The traditional list of 33 Vedic gods.', '[{"word":"mahiman","meaning":"power"},{"word":"vasu","meaning":"Vasus"},{"word":"rudra","meaning":"Rudras"},{"word":"āditya","meaning":"Adityas"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-3', 'en', 'The eight Vasus are the parts of nature in which all things dwell.', 'Word-play: vasu, ''dwelling, wealth''.', '[{"word":"vasu","meaning":"Vasus"},{"word":"hita","meaning":"placed"},{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"},{"word":"nakṣatra","meaning":"stars"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-4', 'en', 'The Rudras are the life-breaths, which cause weeping when they leave at death.', 'Word-play: Rudra and rud, ''to weep''.', '[{"word":"rudra","meaning":"Rudras"},{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"breath"},{"word":"rodayanti","meaning":"make weep"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-5', 'en', 'The Adityas are the twelve months, which carry everything away.', 'Word-play: Aditya and adadana, ''taking''.', '[{"word":"āditya","meaning":"Adityas"},{"word":"māsa","meaning":"month"},{"word":"ādadānāḥ","meaning":"taking"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-6', 'en', 'Indra is thunder and Prajapati is the sacrifice.', 'The last two of the thirty-three.', '[{"word":"indra","meaning":"Indra"},{"word":"stanayitnu","meaning":"thunder"},{"word":"aśani","meaning":"thunderbolt"},{"word":"yajña","meaning":"sacrifice"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-7', 'en', 'The six gods are fire, earth, wind, sky, sun and heaven.', 'Counting down continues.', '[{"word":"ṣaṭ","meaning":"six"},{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"},{"word":"pṛthivī","meaning":"earth"},{"word":"dyau","meaning":"heaven"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-8', 'en', 'The three gods are the three worlds; the two are food and breath; the one and a half is the wind.', 'The puzzle of ''one and a half'' is explained next.', '[{"word":"traya","meaning":"three"},{"word":"anna","meaning":"food"},{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"breath"},{"word":"adhyardha","meaning":"one and a half"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-9', 'en', 'The one god is breath, which is Brahman.', 'Word-play on adhyardha and adhyardhnot, ''grew''. In the end, all gods are one.', '[{"word":"eka","meaning":"one"},{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"breath"},{"word":"brahman","meaning":"Brahman"},{"word":"tyat","meaning":"That"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-10', 'en', 'The person based in the earth is the person in the body, and his deity is the immortal.', 'Shakalya tests Yajnavalkya with riddles about eight ''persons''. Yajnavalkya answers each.', '[{"word":"pṛthivī","meaning":"earth"},{"word":"śārīra","meaning":"bodily"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"parāyaṇa","meaning":"final support"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-11', 'en', 'The person based in desire is the person made of desire, and his deity is women.', 'Shakalya tests Yajnavalkya with riddles about eight ''persons''. Yajnavalkya answers each.', '[{"word":"kāma","meaning":"desire"},{"word":"hṛdaya","meaning":"heart"},{"word":"strī","meaning":"women"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-12', 'en', 'The person based in forms is the person in the sun, and his deity is truth.', 'Shakalya tests Yajnavalkya with riddles about eight ''persons''. Yajnavalkya answers each.', '[{"word":"rūpa","meaning":"form"},{"word":"āditya","meaning":"sun"},{"word":"satya","meaning":"truth"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-13', 'en', 'The person based in space is the person in hearing and in the echo, and his deity is the directions.', 'Shakalya tests Yajnavalkya with riddles about eight ''persons''. Yajnavalkya answers each.', '[{"word":"ākāśa","meaning":"space"},{"word":"śrotra","meaning":"ear"},{"word":"diś","meaning":"directions"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-14', 'en', 'The person based in darkness is the person made of shadow, and his deity is death.', 'Shakalya tests Yajnavalkya with riddles about eight ''persons''. Yajnavalkya answers each.', '[{"word":"tamas","meaning":"darkness"},{"word":"chāyāmaya","meaning":"made of shadow"},{"word":"mṛtyu","meaning":"death"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-15', 'en', 'The person based in forms is the person in the mirror, and his deity is truth.', 'Shakalya tests Yajnavalkya with riddles about eight ''persons''. Yajnavalkya answers each.', '[{"word":"ādarśa","meaning":"mirror"},{"word":"rūpa","meaning":"form"},{"word":"satya","meaning":"truth"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-16', 'en', 'The person based in water is the person in water, and his deity is varuna.', 'Shakalya tests Yajnavalkya with riddles about eight ''persons''. Yajnavalkya answers each.', '[{"word":"āpaḥ","meaning":"water"},{"word":"varuṇa","meaning":"Varuna"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-17', 'en', 'The person based in semen is the person made of the son, and his deity is prajapati.', 'Shakalya tests Yajnavalkya with riddles about eight ''persons''. Yajnavalkya answers each.', '[{"word":"retas","meaning":"semen"},{"word":"putramaya","meaning":"made of the son"},{"word":"prajāpati","meaning":"creator"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-18', 'en', 'Yajnavalkya asks Shakalya if the others are using him to handle hot coals for them.', 'A sharp joke: Shakalya is being sent into danger while the others stay safe.', '[{"word":"aṅgārāvakṣayaṇa","meaning":"coal-scoop"},{"word":"śākalya","meaning":"Shakalya"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-19', 'en', 'Shakalya angrily asks what Yajnavalkya knows; he says he knows the directions, their gods and supports.', 'The challenge sharpens.', '[{"word":"atyavādīḥ","meaning":"talked down"},{"word":"diś","meaning":"directions"},{"word":"pratiṣṭhā","meaning":"support"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-20', 'en', 'The east''s god is the sun, which rests on the eye, forms and finally the heart.', 'Each direction leads back, step by step, to the heart.', '[{"word":"prācī","meaning":"east"},{"word":"āditya","meaning":"sun"},{"word":"cakṣus","meaning":"eye"},{"word":"hṛdaya","meaning":"heart"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-21', 'en', 'The south''s god is Yama, resting on sacrifice, gifts, faith and the heart.', 'Each direction leads back, step by step, to the heart.', '[{"word":"dakṣiṇā","meaning":"south, fee"},{"word":"yama","meaning":"Yama"},{"word":"śraddhā","meaning":"faith"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-22', 'en', 'The west''s god is Varuna, resting on water, seed and the heart.', 'Each direction leads back, step by step, to the heart.', '[{"word":"pratīcī","meaning":"west"},{"word":"varuṇa","meaning":"Varuna"},{"word":"retas","meaning":"semen"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-23', 'en', 'The north''s god is Soma, resting on consecration, truth and the heart.', 'Each direction leads back, step by step, to the heart.', '[{"word":"udīcī","meaning":"north"},{"word":"soma","meaning":"Soma"},{"word":"dīkṣā","meaning":"consecration"},{"word":"satya","meaning":"truth"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-24', 'en', 'The zenith''s god is fire, resting on speech and the heart. Then Shakalya asks what the heart rests on.', 'Everything leads to the heart; now the question is what holds the heart.', '[{"word":"dhruvā","meaning":"fixed direction"},{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"},{"word":"vāc","meaning":"speech"},{"word":"hṛdaya","meaning":"heart"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-25', 'en', 'Yajnavalkya rebukes him: the heart rests in us, not elsewhere.', 'A sharp, humorous answer.', '[{"word":"ahallika","meaning":"idiot"},{"word":"śvan","meaning":"dog"},{"word":"vayas","meaning":"birds"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-26', 'en', 'The Self is ''not this, not this'': beyond grasping, decay or harm. Shakalya cannot answer and loses his head.', 'A dramatic end to the debate; the price of arrogant false knowledge.', '[{"word":"neti neti","meaning":"not this, not this"},{"word":"agṛhya","meaning":"ungraspable"},{"word":"aśīrya","meaning":"undecaying"},{"word":"asaṅga","meaning":"unattached"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-27', 'en', 'Yajnavalkya invites anyone to question him, but no one dares.', 'He has won the debate.', '[{"word":"pṛcchatu","meaning":"may ask"},{"word":"dadhṛṣuḥ","meaning":"dared"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-3-9-28', 'en', 'A person is like a tree, but what root does he grow from after death? The answer: Brahman, awareness and bliss.', 'Yajnavalkya''s riddle ends the debates of chapter 3 with the famous phrase ''vijnanam anandam brahma'', ''Brahman is awareness and bliss''.', '[{"word":"vṛkṣa","meaning":"tree"},{"word":"mūla","meaning":"root"},{"word":"vijñāna","meaning":"awareness"},{"word":"ānanda","meaning":"bliss"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-1-1', 'en', 'King Janaka asks if Yajnavalkya came for cattle or for deep questions; he says both.', 'Chapter 4, with the great dialogues between Yajnavalkya and Janaka, begins with gentle humour.', '[{"word":"āsāṃ cakre","meaning":"was holding court"},{"word":"paśu","meaning":"cattle"},{"word":"aṇvanta","meaning":"subtle"},{"word":"samrāj","meaning":"emperor"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-1-2', 'en', 'Speech is Brahman, but only one part; its full nature is wisdom.', 'King Janaka reports six teachings he has heard; Yajnavalkya shows each is only ''one leg'' and completes it.', '[{"word":"vāc","meaning":"speech"},{"word":"prajñā","meaning":"wisdom"},{"word":"ekapād","meaning":"one-legged"},{"word":"āyatana","meaning":"home"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-1-3', 'en', 'Breath is Brahman, but only one part; its full nature is dear.', 'King Janaka reports six teachings he has heard; Yajnavalkya shows each is only ''one leg'' and completes it.', '[{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"breath"},{"word":"priya","meaning":"dear"},{"word":"vadha","meaning":"killing"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-1-4', 'en', 'The eye is Brahman, but only one part; its full nature is truth.', 'King Janaka reports six teachings he has heard; Yajnavalkya shows each is only ''one leg'' and completes it.', '[{"word":"cakṣus","meaning":"eye"},{"word":"satya","meaning":"truth"},{"word":"adrākṣam","meaning":"I saw"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-1-5', 'en', 'The ear is Brahman, but only one part; its full nature is endless.', 'King Janaka reports six teachings he has heard; Yajnavalkya shows each is only ''one leg'' and completes it.', '[{"word":"śrotra","meaning":"ear"},{"word":"ananta","meaning":"endless"},{"word":"diś","meaning":"directions"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-1-6', 'en', 'The mind is Brahman, but only one part; its full nature is bliss.', 'King Janaka reports six teachings he has heard; Yajnavalkya shows each is only ''one leg'' and completes it.', '[{"word":"manas","meaning":"mind"},{"word":"ānanda","meaning":"bliss"},{"word":"putra","meaning":"son"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-1-7', 'en', 'The heart is Brahman, but only one part; its full nature is stability.', 'King Janaka reports six teachings he has heard; Yajnavalkya shows each is only ''one leg'' and completes it.', '[{"word":"hṛdaya","meaning":"heart"},{"word":"sthiti","meaning":"stability"},{"word":"pratiṣṭhā","meaning":"support"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-2-1', 'en', 'Janaka humbly asks to be taught; Yajnavalkya asks where he will go after death, and Janaka does not know.', 'The king becomes a student.', '[{"word":"upāvasarpan","meaning":"coming down"},{"word":"śādhi","meaning":"teach"},{"word":"adhvan","meaning":"journey"},{"word":"gamiṣyasi","meaning":"you will go"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-2-2', 'en', 'The person in the right eye is called Indra.', 'The gods prefer hidden names, a recurring idea.', '[{"word":"indha","meaning":"kindler"},{"word":"indra","meaning":"Indra"},{"word":"parokṣa","meaning":"indirect"},{"word":"pratyakṣa","meaning":"direct"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-2-3', 'en', 'The persons in the two eyes are husband and wife who meet in the heart.', 'An inner anatomy of the subtle self.', '[{"word":"virāj","meaning":"Viraj"},{"word":"patnī","meaning":"wife"},{"word":"hitā nāḍī","meaning":"hita channels"},{"word":"lohitapiṇḍa","meaning":"red lump"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-2-4', 'en', 'The Self is ''not this, not this''; knowing it, Janaka reaches fearlessness and offers his kingdom.', 'Abhaya, fearlessness, is the fruit of knowing the Self.', '[{"word":"neti neti","meaning":"not this, not this"},{"word":"abhaya","meaning":"fearlessness"},{"word":"videha","meaning":"Videha people"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-1', 'en', 'Yajnavalkya planned to stay silent, but he had once promised the king free questions.', 'The great dialogue on light and the self begins.', '[{"word":"vara","meaning":"wish"},{"word":"kāmapraśna","meaning":"asking as he likes"},{"word":"agnihotra","meaning":"fire offering"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-2', 'en', 'By day, the sun is our light.', 'Janaka asks what light a person lives by.', '[{"word":"jyotis","meaning":"light"},{"word":"āditya","meaning":"sun"},{"word":"palyayate","meaning":"goes about"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-3', 'en', 'After sunset, the moon is our light.', 'The questions go deeper.', '[{"word":"astamita","meaning":"set"},{"word":"candramas","meaning":"moon"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-4', 'en', 'When sun and moon are gone, fire is our light.', 'Deeper still.', '[{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"},{"word":"jyotis","meaning":"light"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-5', 'en', 'Without any outer light, a voice guides us.', 'Speech as guiding light in darkness.', '[{"word":"vāc","meaning":"speech"},{"word":"pāṇi","meaning":"hand"},{"word":"upanyeti","meaning":"goes toward"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-6', 'en', 'When all outer lights are gone, the Self is our light.', 'The inner light that never fails.', '[{"word":"ātman","meaning":"Self"},{"word":"jyotis","meaning":"light"},{"word":"śānta","meaning":"silent"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-7', 'en', 'The Self is the light of awareness within the heart, moving between waking and dream.', 'The Self in the waking and dream states.', '[{"word":"vijñānamaya","meaning":"made of understanding"},{"word":"antarjyotis","meaning":"inner light"},{"word":"svapna","meaning":"dream"},{"word":"mṛtyu","meaning":"death"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-8', 'en', 'When born, the self takes on troubles; at death it leaves them behind.', 'Evils belong to the body, not the Self.', '[{"word":"jāyamāna","meaning":"being born"},{"word":"pāpman","meaning":"evil"},{"word":"vijahāti","meaning":"leaves behind"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-9', 'en', 'In dreams the self creates its own world from its own light.', 'Svayamjyoti, ''self-luminous'': in dreams the Self is its own light.', '[{"word":"sandhya","meaning":"meeting place"},{"word":"svapna","meaning":"dream"},{"word":"svayaṃjyotis","meaning":"self-luminous"},{"word":"nirmāya","meaning":"building"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-10', 'en', 'In dreams there is nothing outside, yet the self creates everything.', 'The creative power of the mind in dreams.', '[{"word":"ratha","meaning":"chariot"},{"word":"ānanda","meaning":"joy"},{"word":"puṣkariṇī","meaning":"pond"},{"word":"kartṛ","meaning":"creator"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-11', 'en', 'In sleep the golden Self stays awake, watching over the sleeping senses.', 'The ''lone swan'' (ekahamsa) is a beautiful image of the free Self.', '[{"word":"asupta","meaning":"sleepless"},{"word":"śukra","meaning":"light"},{"word":"hiraṇmaya","meaning":"golden"},{"word":"ekahaṃsa","meaning":"lone swan"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-12', 'en', 'The body is a nest guarded by breath, while the immortal Self roams freely.', 'The Self is free to travel in dreams.', '[{"word":"kulāya","meaning":"nest"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"yatrakāmam","meaning":"wherever he wishes"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-13', 'en', 'In dreams the Self creates many forms, pleasant and frightening.', 'The verses on dreaming continue.', '[{"word":"svapnānta","meaning":"dream state"},{"word":"deva","meaning":"god"},{"word":"rūpa","meaning":"form"},{"word":"bhaya","meaning":"fear"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-14', 'en', 'People see the dream world but never the dreamer. Janaka asks for more teaching about freedom.', 'Advice not to wake a sleeper suddenly comes from this verse.', '[{"word":"ārāma","meaning":"playground"},{"word":"bodhayet","meaning":"should wake"},{"word":"svayaṃjyotis","meaning":"self-luminous"},{"word":"vimokṣa","meaning":"freedom"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-15', 'en', 'The Self passes through deep sleep, dream and waking, but nothing clings to it.', 'Asanga, ''unattached'', is a key quality of the Self.', '[{"word":"saṃprasāda","meaning":"deep peace"},{"word":"ananvāgata","meaning":"not followed"},{"word":"asaṅga","meaning":"unattached"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-16', 'en', 'From dream the Self returns to waking, untouched by what it saw.', 'The same pattern, dream to waking.', '[{"word":"svapna","meaning":"dream"},{"word":"buddhānta","meaning":"waking state"},{"word":"asaṅga","meaning":"unattached"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-17', 'en', 'From waking the Self returns to dream.', 'The cycle of states continues.', '[{"word":"buddhānta","meaning":"waking"},{"word":"svapnānta","meaning":"dream"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-18', 'en', 'Like a big fish moving between two riverbanks, the Self moves between dreaming and waking.', 'A memorable image of the Self passing between states.', '[{"word":"mahāmatsya","meaning":"great fish"},{"word":"kūla","meaning":"bank"},{"word":"anusaṃcarati","meaning":"moves along"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-19', 'en', 'Like a tired bird returning to its nest, the Self longs for deep, dreamless sleep.', 'Deep sleep is the Self''s home of rest.', '[{"word":"śyena","meaning":"hawk"},{"word":"suparṇa","meaning":"eagle"},{"word":"śrānta","meaning":"tired"},{"word":"saṃlaya","meaning":"nest"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-20', 'en', 'Nightmares come from ignorance; feeling ''I am all this'' is the Self''s highest state.', 'The highest dream is one of complete wholeness.', '[{"word":"hitā","meaning":"hita channels"},{"word":"avidyā","meaning":"ignorance"},{"word":"gartam","meaning":"pit"},{"word":"paramo lokaḥ","meaning":"highest world"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-21', 'en', 'Like lovers in an embrace who forget everything else, the self in union with the true Self is beyond desire and sorrow.', 'A tender image of union with the Self in deep sleep and liberation.', '[{"word":"aticchandas","meaning":"beyond desire"},{"word":"prājña ātman","meaning":"wise Self"},{"word":"saṃpariṣvakta","meaning":"embraced"},{"word":"śokāntara","meaning":"beyond sorrow"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-22', 'en', 'In that state all roles and labels vanish; one is beyond good, evil and every sorrow.', 'All social and moral identities fall away in pure Being.', '[{"word":"pitṛ","meaning":"father"},{"word":"stena","meaning":"thief"},{"word":"śramaṇa","meaning":"monk"},{"word":"tīrṇa","meaning":"crossed beyond"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-23', 'en', 'In deep sleep the Self does not see, not because its power is lost, but because there is nothing else to see.', 'In deep sleep awareness itself remains; only the ''other'', the object, disappears.', '[{"word":"draṣṭṛ","meaning":"seer"},{"word":"dṛṣṭi","meaning":"sight"},{"word":"avināśitva","meaning":"imperishability"},{"word":"dvitīya","meaning":"second"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-24', 'en', 'In deep sleep the Self does not smell, not because its power is lost, but because there is nothing else to smell.', 'In deep sleep awareness itself remains; only the ''other'', the object, disappears.', '[{"word":"ghrātṛ","meaning":"smeller"},{"word":"ghrāti","meaning":"smelling"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-25', 'en', 'In deep sleep the Self does not taste, not because its power is lost, but because there is nothing else to taste.', 'In deep sleep awareness itself remains; only the ''other'', the object, disappears.', '[{"word":"rasayitṛ","meaning":"taster"},{"word":"rasayati","meaning":"tastes"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-26', 'en', 'In deep sleep the Self does not speak, not because its power is lost, but because there is nothing else to speak.', 'In deep sleep awareness itself remains; only the ''other'', the object, disappears.', '[{"word":"vaktṛ","meaning":"speaker"},{"word":"vakti","meaning":"speech"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-27', 'en', 'In deep sleep the Self does not hear, not because its power is lost, but because there is nothing else to hear.', 'In deep sleep awareness itself remains; only the ''other'', the object, disappears.', '[{"word":"śrotṛ","meaning":"hearer"},{"word":"śruti","meaning":"hearing"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-28', 'en', 'In deep sleep the Self does not think, not because its power is lost, but because there is nothing else to think.', 'In deep sleep awareness itself remains; only the ''other'', the object, disappears.', '[{"word":"mantṛ","meaning":"thinker"},{"word":"mati","meaning":"thought"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-29', 'en', 'In deep sleep the Self does not touch, not because its power is lost, but because there is nothing else to touch.', 'In deep sleep awareness itself remains; only the ''other'', the object, disappears.', '[{"word":"spraṣṭṛ","meaning":"toucher"},{"word":"spṛṣṭi","meaning":"touch"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-30', 'en', 'In deep sleep the Self does not know, not because its power is lost, but because there is nothing else to know.', 'In deep sleep awareness itself remains; only the ''other'', the object, disappears.', '[{"word":"vijñātṛ","meaning":"knower"},{"word":"vijñāti","meaning":"knowing"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-31', 'en', 'Only where there is an ''other'' can one see, hear or know something.', 'Experience of objects needs duality.', '[{"word":"anya","meaning":"other"},{"word":"paśyet","meaning":"could see"},{"word":"vijānīyāt","meaning":"could know"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-32', 'en', 'The Self becomes like a single ocean, a seer with no other: this is the highest bliss.', 'Other beings live on a fraction of this bliss.', '[{"word":"salila","meaning":"ocean"},{"word":"advaita","meaning":"without a second"},{"word":"parama ānanda","meaning":"highest bliss"},{"word":"mātrā","meaning":"part"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-33', 'en', 'Bliss rises a hundredfold at each level up to Brahman; a wise person free of desire already has it. Janaka keeps asking for more.', 'A famous ''ladder of bliss'', also in the Taittiriya Upanishad. Yajnavalkya jokes that the king has drawn everything out of him.', '[{"word":"ānanda","meaning":"bliss"},{"word":"śrotriya","meaning":"learned"},{"word":"akāmahata","meaning":"untouched by desire"},{"word":"brahmaloka","meaning":"world of Brahman"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-34', 'en', 'The Self returns from dream to waking.', 'A repeated line leading into the teaching on death.', '[{"word":"svapnānta","meaning":"dream state"},{"word":"buddhānta","meaning":"waking"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-35', 'en', 'At death the body groans like an overloaded cart carrying the Self.', 'The teaching on dying begins.', '[{"word":"anas","meaning":"cart"},{"word":"utsarjam","meaning":"creaking"},{"word":"ūrdhvocchvāsin","meaning":"breathing his last"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-36', 'en', 'At death the Self comes loose from the body like ripe fruit from its stem.', 'A gentle image of natural death.', '[{"word":"jarā","meaning":"old age"},{"word":"āmra","meaning":"mango"},{"word":"pippala","meaning":"pipal fruit"},{"word":"pramucyate","meaning":"comes loose"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-37', 'en', 'As officials prepare for a king, all beings welcome the knower of the Self.', 'The knower is honoured by all.', '[{"word":"rājan","meaning":"king"},{"word":"sūtagrāmaṇī","meaning":"charioteers and headmen"},{"word":"pratikalpante","meaning":"prepare"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-3-38', 'en', 'At death the senses gather around the departing Self, like officials around a king.', 'The breaths escort the Self as it leaves.', '[{"word":"prayiyāsat","meaning":"about to leave"},{"word":"antakāla","meaning":"time of death"},{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"breaths"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-1', 'en', 'At death the Self gathers the senses'' light and withdraws into the heart.', 'The senses withdraw one by one.', '[{"word":"ābalya","meaning":"weakness"},{"word":"saṃmoha","meaning":"unconsciousness"},{"word":"tejomātrā","meaning":"particles of light"},{"word":"arūpajña","meaning":"not knowing forms"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-2', 'en', 'As the senses go silent, the heart lights up and the Self leaves, carrying its knowledge, deeds and memories.', 'One''s karma and knowledge travel with the Self after death.', '[{"word":"ekībhavati","meaning":"becomes one"},{"word":"pradyotate","meaning":"lights up"},{"word":"niṣkrāmati","meaning":"leaves"},{"word":"vidyākarmaṇī","meaning":"knowledge and deeds"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-3', 'en', 'Like a caterpillar moving from one blade of grass to the next, the Self moves on to a new body.', 'A famous image of rebirth.', '[{"word":"tṛṇajalāyukā","meaning":"caterpillar"},{"word":"tṛṇa","meaning":"grass"},{"word":"ākrama","meaning":"support"},{"word":"upasaṃharati","meaning":"draws across"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-4', 'en', 'Like a goldsmith remaking gold, the Self takes on a new, more beautiful form.', 'Another image of rebirth.', '[{"word":"peśaskārī","meaning":"goldsmith"},{"word":"navatara","meaning":"newer"},{"word":"kalyāṇatara","meaning":"more beautiful"},{"word":"rūpa","meaning":"form"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-5', 'en', 'We become what we do, and we do what we desire. Desire shapes will, will shapes action, action shapes our destiny.', 'One of the clearest statements of karma: desire leads to will, will to action, action to results.', '[{"word":"yathākārī","meaning":"as one acts"},{"word":"kāmamaya","meaning":"made of desire"},{"word":"kratu","meaning":"will"},{"word":"karma","meaning":"action"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-6', 'en', 'Those with desires keep coming back; one whose only desire is the Self is Brahman and merges with Brahman.', 'The way out of rebirth is freedom from desire.', '[{"word":"sakta","meaning":"attached"},{"word":"akāma","meaning":"desireless"},{"word":"āptakāma","meaning":"desires fulfilled"},{"word":"brahmāpyeti","meaning":"goes to Brahman"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-7', 'en', 'When all desires in the heart are released, the mortal becomes immortal here and now; the body is left like a snake''s old skin.', 'This verse also appears in the Katha Upanishad (6.14).', '[{"word":"kāma","meaning":"desire"},{"word":"pramucyante","meaning":"are released"},{"word":"ahinirlvayanī","meaning":"snake''s skin"},{"word":"aśarīra","meaning":"bodiless"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-8', 'en', 'There is an ancient, narrow path, and the wise who know Brahman travel it to freedom.', 'Verses on the path of liberation.', '[{"word":"aṇu panthā","meaning":"narrow path"},{"word":"purāṇa","meaning":"ancient"},{"word":"dhīra","meaning":"wise"},{"word":"vimukta","meaning":"set free"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-9', 'en', 'The path shines with many colours; the knower of Brahman travels it.', 'The colours recall the heart''s channels in 4.3.20.', '[{"word":"śukla","meaning":"white"},{"word":"nīla","meaning":"blue"},{"word":"harita","meaning":"green"},{"word":"taijasa","meaning":"full of light"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-10', 'en', 'Ignorance leads to darkness, but pride in mere knowledge leads to even more.', 'This verse also appears in the Isha Upanishad (9).', '[{"word":"andha tamas","meaning":"blind darkness"},{"word":"avidyā","meaning":"ignorance"},{"word":"vidyā","meaning":"knowledge"},{"word":"rata","meaning":"delighting"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-11', 'en', 'Those who do not wake up go to joyless, dark worlds.', 'A warning about ignorance.', '[{"word":"ananda","meaning":"joyless"},{"word":"avidvāṃs","meaning":"ignorant"},{"word":"abudha","meaning":"unawakened"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-12', 'en', 'Once you know ''I am the Self'', why suffer with the body?', 'Knowledge of the Self ends suffering.', '[{"word":"ayam asmi","meaning":"I am this"},{"word":"saṃjvaret","meaning":"would suffer"},{"word":"śarīra","meaning":"body"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-13', 'en', 'Whoever finds the Self hidden in the body becomes one with the creator of all.', 'The awakened one is the world itself.', '[{"word":"pratibuddha","meaning":"awakened"},{"word":"gahana","meaning":"tangled place"},{"word":"viśvakṛt","meaning":"maker of all"},{"word":"kartṛ","meaning":"creator"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-14', 'en', 'We must know the Self in this life; otherwise the loss is great.', 'An urgent call: this life is the chance.', '[{"word":"iha","meaning":"here"},{"word":"vinaṣṭi","meaning":"loss"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"duḥkha","meaning":"sorrow"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-15', 'en', 'Seeing the Self as lord of past and future ends all fear and dislike.', 'Yajnavalkya continues to King Janaka. One who truly sees the Self as the ruler of all time stops recoiling from anything, because nothing stands outside that Self to be feared or hated.', '[{"word":"devam","meaning":"the shining one"},{"word":"īśānam","meaning":"lord, ruler"},{"word":"bhūtabhavya","meaning":"past and future"},{"word":"vijugupsate","meaning":"shrinks from, dislikes"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-16', 'en', 'The Self stands above time; the gods honour it as the light behind all lights.', 'Time, measured in days and years, turns below the Self and does not touch it. Even the gods treat it as the deepest light and as deathless life.', '[{"word":"saṃvatsara","meaning":"year"},{"word":"jyotiṣāṃ jyotiḥ","meaning":"light of lights"},{"word":"āyuḥ","meaning":"life"},{"word":"amṛtam","meaning":"immortal"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-17', 'en', 'Everything, even space, rests in the Self; knowing this is immortality.', 'The ''five fives'' are all the categories of beings. The speaker declares that the support of everything, including space, is the Self, and that knowing it makes one deathless.', '[{"word":"pañcajanāḥ","meaning":"the five kinds of beings"},{"word":"ākāśa","meaning":"space"},{"word":"pratiṣṭhita","meaning":"established"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-18', 'en', 'The Self is what makes breath, sight, hearing and thought work.', 'Our senses and mind do not run on their own power. Behind each is the Self that lets it function. Knowing that inner power is knowing the oldest reality, Brahman.', '[{"word":"prāṇasya prāṇam","meaning":"breath of breath"},{"word":"cakṣuṣaś cakṣuḥ","meaning":"eye of the eye"},{"word":"purāṇam","meaning":"ancient"},{"word":"agryam","meaning":"first, primal"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-20', 'en', 'See the Self as one, without parts, unborn and ever steady.', 'The text in this edition skips verse 4.4.19 (a source gap). This verse says the Self cannot be measured, has no stain, is beyond even space, and never comes into being or changes.', '[{"word":"ekadhā","meaning":"as one only"},{"word":"aprameya","meaning":"immeasurable"},{"word":"dhruva","meaning":"constant, firm"},{"word":"aja","meaning":"unborn"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-21', 'en', 'Know the Self and live by that insight; endless words just tire the tongue.', 'Yajnavalkya warns against piling up learned talk. The point is to know the Self and make that knowing one''s settled understanding, not to keep multiplying arguments.', '[{"word":"dhīra","meaning":"wise, steady"},{"word":"prajñā","meaning":"insight"},{"word":"śabda","meaning":"words"},{"word":"viglāpanam","meaning":"wearying"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-22', 'en', 'The Self in the heart rules all and is untouched by good or bad deeds; people renounce everything to know it.', 'This is one of the great statements of the Upanishad. The Self is unaffected by actions; it is the ''dam'' that holds the order of the worlds. Study, sacrifice, charity and austerity are ways of seeking it, and the wish to know it is what makes renunciants leave home.', '[{"word":"vijñānamaya","meaning":"made of understanding"},{"word":"hṛdaya ākāśa","meaning":"space in the heart"},{"word":"setu","meaning":"dam, bridge"},{"word":"pravrājin","meaning":"wandering renunciant"},{"word":"muni","meaning":"sage"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-23', 'en', 'Calm, self-control, patience and focus let one see the Self everywhere and rise above wrongdoing.', 'The teaching to Janaka ends here. The famous list of qualities (calm, restraint, withdrawal, endurance, concentration) describes the knower. Janaka responds by offering his whole kingdom and himself to his teacher.', '[{"word":"śānta","meaning":"calm"},{"word":"dānta","meaning":"self-controlled"},{"word":"titikṣu","meaning":"patient, enduring"},{"word":"samāhita","meaning":"collected"},{"word":"pāpman","meaning":"evil"},{"word":"videha","meaning":"Janaka''s kingdom"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-24', 'en', 'The Self enjoys all and gives all; knowing it brings real abundance.', 'A short verse praising the Self as the one who receives every offering and grants every good thing.', '[{"word":"annāda","meaning":"eater of food"},{"word":"vasudāna","meaning":"giver of wealth"},{"word":"vasu","meaning":"wealth"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-4-25', 'en', 'The Self never ages or dies; knowing it, one becomes fearless.', 'The section closes with its key word: fearlessness. Fear comes from seeing something other than oneself; the one who knows the Self has nothing to fear.', '[{"word":"ajara","meaning":"undecaying"},{"word":"amara","meaning":"deathless"},{"word":"abhaya","meaning":"fearless"},{"word":"brahma","meaning":"the ultimate reality"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-1', 'en', 'Yajnavalkya, about to renounce, had two wives; Maitreyi was a seeker of truth.', 'This repeats the Maitreyi dialogue from 2.4 with small changes. It sets the scene: the sage plans to leave household life, and one wife, Maitreyi, cares about spiritual knowledge.', '[{"word":"bhāryā","meaning":"wife"},{"word":"brahmavādinī","meaning":"one who discusses Brahman"},{"word":"vṛtta","meaning":"way of life"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-2', 'en', 'Before leaving, Yajnavalkya offers to divide his property.', 'He wants to arrange things fairly between his two wives before he goes.', '[{"word":"pravrajiṣyan","meaning":"about to go forth"},{"word":"sthāna","meaning":"place, home"},{"word":"anta","meaning":"settlement"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-3', 'en', 'Wealth cannot buy immortality.', 'Maitreyi asks the key question. Yajnavalkya is honest: riches give comfort, nothing more.', '[{"word":"vitta","meaning":"wealth"},{"word":"amṛta","meaning":"immortal"},{"word":"upakaraṇavat","meaning":"well-provided, wealthy"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-4', 'en', 'Maitreyi refuses wealth and asks for the truth instead.', 'She chooses knowledge over property, which makes her worthy of the teaching.', '[{"word":"kim kuryām","meaning":"what should I do"},{"word":"brūhi","meaning":"tell"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-5', 'en', 'Pleased, Yajnavalkya agrees to teach and asks her to meditate on it.', 'Here, unlike 2.4, he explicitly asks her to contemplate (nididhyāsa) as he speaks.', '[{"word":"priyā","meaning":"dear"},{"word":"vyākhyāsyāmi","meaning":"I will explain"},{"word":"nididhyāsasva","meaning":"meditate, reflect deeply"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-6', 'en', 'All love is ultimately love of the Self; know the Self and you know everything.', 'The famous teaching, with cattle and the Vedas added to the list. It does not call love selfish; it says the Self is the real source of all that is dear. The four steps, seeing, hearing, reflecting and meditating, became the classic path of study.', '[{"word":"ātman","meaning":"Self"},{"word":"priya","meaning":"dear"},{"word":"draṣṭavya","meaning":"to be seen"},{"word":"śrotavya","meaning":"to be heard"},{"word":"mantavya","meaning":"to be reflected on"},{"word":"nididhyāsitavya","meaning":"to be meditated on"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-7', 'en', 'Whoever sees anything as separate from the Self is let down by it; everything is the Self.', 'Treating things as separate from oneself leads to loss. The closing line states unity: all of it is the Self.', '[{"word":"parādāt","meaning":"rejects, abandons"},{"word":"anyatra","meaning":"as other than"},{"word":"idaṃ sarvam","meaning":"all this"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-8', 'en', 'Grasp the source and you grasp all that comes from it.', 'An image for how knowing the Self brings knowledge of all its expressions.', '[{"word":"dundubhi","meaning":"drum"},{"word":"grahaṇa","meaning":"grasping"},{"word":"śabda","meaning":"sound"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-9', 'en', 'Same lesson with a conch: hold the source and you hold the sound.', 'The second of three sound images.', '[{"word":"śaṅkha","meaning":"conch"},{"word":"dhmā","meaning":"to blow"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-10', 'en', 'Same lesson with a lute: know the source, know its notes.', 'The third image completes the set.', '[{"word":"vīṇā","meaning":"lute"},{"word":"vādya","meaning":"playing"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-11', 'en', 'All knowledge, ritual and worlds flow out of the Self as easily as breath.', 'This version adds sacrifice, food, drink and the worlds to the list in 2.4.10. Everything comes from the great being effortlessly, as breathing does.', '[{"word":"niḥśvasita","meaning":"breathed out"},{"word":"ārdraidhāgni","meaning":"fire of damp wood"},{"word":"itihāsa","meaning":"history"},{"word":"purāṇa","meaning":"ancient lore"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-12', 'en', 'Each kind of experience has its gathering point, as all waters gather in the sea.', 'Ultimately, all of these meet in the Self, just as every river ends in the ocean.', '[{"word":"ekāyana","meaning":"meeting place"},{"word":"samudra","meaning":"ocean"},{"word":"tvak","meaning":"skin"},{"word":"saṃkalpa","meaning":"intention"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-13', 'en', 'The Self is pure awareness through and through; after death, separate identity ends.', 'Like salt that is salty all the way through, the Self is awareness all the way through. When the body ends, the sense of being a separate individual dissolves.', '[{"word":"saindhavaghana","meaning":"lump of salt"},{"word":"prajñānaghana","meaning":"mass of awareness"},{"word":"saṃjñā","meaning":"separate awareness"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-14', 'en', 'Yajnavalkya reassures her: the Self never perishes.', 'Maitreyi worries that ''no awareness after death'' means annihilation. He clarifies that the Self itself is indestructible; only the sense of separateness ends.', '[{"word":"moha","meaning":"confusion"},{"word":"avināśin","meaning":"imperishable"},{"word":"anucchitti","meaning":"never cut off"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-5-15', 'en', 'When all is the Self, there is no ''other'' to perceive; the knower itself cannot be made an object.', 'The dialogue ends with the ''neti neti'' teaching. The Self is the subject of all knowing, so it can never be seen like an object. Having shared this, Yajnavalkya leaves for the life of a renunciant.', '[{"word":"dvaita","meaning":"duality"},{"word":"neti neti","meaning":"not this, not this"},{"word":"agṛhya","meaning":"ungraspable"},{"word":"asaṅga","meaning":"unattached"},{"word":"vijñātṛ","meaning":"knower"},{"word":"amṛtatva","meaning":"immortality"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-6-1', 'en', 'The lineage of teachers who passed down this teaching begins.', 'Like 2.6, this list records who received the teaching from whom, honouring the chain of transmission. It continues in the next verses.', '[{"word":"vaṃśa","meaning":"lineage"},{"word":"from","meaning":"received from"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-6-2', 'en', 'The line of teachers continues back through many names.', 'Each name is a teacher who learned from the one named after. The list shows the teaching was carefully handed down.', '[{"word":"vaṃśa","meaning":"lineage"},{"word":"ācārya","meaning":"teacher"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-4-6-3', 'en', 'The lineage reaches back to the gods and finally to Brahman, the self-existent source.', 'The chain moves from human teachers to divine ones and ends at Brahman itself, showing that the teaching''s ultimate source is reality itself. It closes with a salutation.', '[{"word":"svayambhu","meaning":"self-existent"},{"word":"brahmaṇe namaḥ","meaning":"homage to Brahman"},{"word":"parameṣṭhin","meaning":"the highest creator"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-1-1', 'en', 'The infinite whole stays whole no matter what comes from it.', 'This famous verse, used as a peace chant, says the unseen reality (that) and the visible world (this) are both complete. Nothing taken from infinity reduces it. Brahman is then compared to space, which holds everything.', '[{"word":"pūrṇa","meaning":"full, whole"},{"word":"adaḥ","meaning":"that (the unseen)"},{"word":"idam","meaning":"this (the seen)"},{"word":"kham","meaning":"space"},{"word":"veda","meaning":"knowledge"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-2-1', 'en', 'For the gods, ''Da'' means: control yourselves.', 'The famous ''Da Da Da'' teaching. The creator gives one syllable, and each group hears the lesson it most needs. The gods, given to pleasure, need self-restraint.', '[{"word":"prajāpati","meaning":"the creator"},{"word":"brahmacarya","meaning":"life as a student"},{"word":"da","meaning":"the syllable ''da''"},{"word":"dāmyata","meaning":"be self-controlled"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-2-2', 'en', 'For humans, ''Da'' means: give.', 'Humans tend to hold on to what they have, so the lesson they hear in the same syllable is generosity.', '[{"word":"manuṣya","meaning":"humans"},{"word":"datta","meaning":"give"},{"word":"dāna","meaning":"giving"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-2-3', 'en', 'Thunder still teaches all of us three things: self-control, generosity and compassion.', 'The demons, given to cruelty, hear compassion. The verse ends by saying every thunderclap repeats this lesson. Many read all three as meant for every person, since each of us has godlike, human and demonic tendencies.', '[{"word":"asura","meaning":"demons"},{"word":"dayadhvam","meaning":"be compassionate"},{"word":"stanayitnu","meaning":"thunder"},{"word":"dama","meaning":"self-control"},{"word":"dayā","meaning":"compassion"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-3-1', 'en', 'The heart is the centre of everything; each syllable of its name carries a blessing.', 'The word for heart, hṛdayam, is broken into three syllables, linked to bringing (hṛ), giving (da) and going (i/yam). It is a playful way of saying the heart holds the whole of life.', '[{"word":"hṛdaya","meaning":"heart"},{"word":"prajāpati","meaning":"creator"},{"word":"akṣara","meaning":"syllable"},{"word":"svarga","meaning":"heaven"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-4-1', 'en', 'Know that ultimate reality is truth, and you cannot be defeated.', 'Truth (satya) is presented as the first thing to arise and as Brahman itself. Holding to truth makes one invincible.', '[{"word":"satya","meaning":"truth"},{"word":"yakṣa","meaning":"wonder, marvel"},{"word":"prathamaja","meaning":"first-born"},{"word":"jayati","meaning":"conquers"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-5-1', 'en', 'Truth surrounds and overpowers untruth; the one who knows this cannot be hurt by lies.', 'A syllable play: the outer parts of the word ''satyam'' are said to be truth and the middle one untruth. Since truth hems in falsehood on both sides, truth always wins.', '[{"word":"āpaḥ","meaning":"water"},{"word":"satya","meaning":"truth"},{"word":"anṛta","meaning":"untruth"},{"word":"parigṛhīta","meaning":"enclosed"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-5-2', 'en', 'The person in the sun and the person in the eye are linked; at death the link fades.', 'Old Indian thought linked the light of the eye with the light of the sun. The verse says they support each other, and at the time of death the eye no longer receives the sun''s rays.', '[{"word":"āditya","meaning":"sun"},{"word":"maṇḍala","meaning":"disc"},{"word":"akṣi","meaning":"eye"},{"word":"raśmi","meaning":"rays"},{"word":"utkramiṣyan","meaning":"about to depart"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-5-3', 'en', 'The three sacred words form the body of the person in the sun; its secret name is ''day''.', 'The three great utterances (bhūḥ, bhuvaḥ, svaḥ) are matched by syllable count to head, arms and feet. ''Ahar'' is linked to the verb ''to destroy'' (han), so knowing it removes evil.', '[{"word":"bhūḥ","meaning":"earth"},{"word":"bhuvaḥ","meaning":"mid-space"},{"word":"svaḥ","meaning":"heaven"},{"word":"upaniṣad","meaning":"secret name"},{"word":"ahar","meaning":"day"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-5-4', 'en', 'The person in the eye has the same body; its secret name is ''I''.', 'The same pattern applied inside us. The inner person''s secret name is ''aham'', meaning ''I'', again linked to destroying evil.', '[{"word":"dakṣiṇa akṣi","meaning":"right eye"},{"word":"aham","meaning":"I"},{"word":"pāpman","meaning":"evil"},{"word":"jahāti","meaning":"leaves behind"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-6-1', 'en', 'A tiny light within the heart rules the whole universe.', 'The inner person is described as very small and very great at once: as small as a grain, yet the ruler of everything.', '[{"word":"manomaya","meaning":"made of mind"},{"word":"bhāḥsatya","meaning":"whose reality is light"},{"word":"vrīhi","meaning":"rice"},{"word":"yava","meaning":"barley"},{"word":"īśāna","meaning":"lord"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-7-1', 'en', 'Meditate on lightning as Brahman, and your faults are cut away.', 'A word play links vidyut, lightning, with the idea of cutting or splitting. Like lightning splitting darkness, this knowledge splits off wrongdoing.', '[{"word":"vidyut","meaning":"lightning"},{"word":"vidāna","meaning":"cutting"},{"word":"pāpman","meaning":"evil"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-8-1', 'en', 'Speech nourishes gods, people and ancestors like a cow feeding everyone.', 'Each ritual call feeds a different group: offerings to gods, hospitality to people (''hanta'', here, take this), offerings to ancestors. Breath gives speech power and mind follows it like a calf.', '[{"word":"dhenu","meaning":"cow"},{"word":"stana","meaning":"teat"},{"word":"svāhā","meaning":"call for offering to gods"},{"word":"hanta","meaning":"call of hospitality"},{"word":"svadhā","meaning":"call to the ancestors"},{"word":"ṛṣabha","meaning":"bull"},{"word":"vatsa","meaning":"calf"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-9-1', 'en', 'The inner fire of digestion is the universal fire; its hum fades at death.', 'Cover your ears and you hear a low roar. The text calls it the sound of the digestive fire, Vaishvanara. Its silence is a sign that life is leaving.', '[{"word":"vaiśvānara","meaning":"universal fire"},{"word":"anna","meaning":"food"},{"word":"pacyate","meaning":"is digested"},{"word":"ghoṣa","meaning":"sound"},{"word":"karṇa","meaning":"ears"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-10-1', 'en', 'After death the knower rises through wind, sun and moon to a world free of sorrow.', 'A picture of the soul''s upward journey. Each realm opens a gap for the departing person until they reach a lasting world without grief.', '[{"word":"vāyu","meaning":"wind"},{"word":"āditya","meaning":"sun"},{"word":"candramas","meaning":"moon"},{"word":"aśoka","meaning":"without sorrow"},{"word":"śāśvatīḥ samāḥ","meaning":"endless years"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-11-1', 'en', 'Illness, death and cremation can be seen as the highest spiritual discipline.', 'The verse reframes suffering. If one sees sickness and the end of life as austerity, as a spiritual practice, then even these become a path to the highest state.', '[{"word":"tapas","meaning":"austerity"},{"word":"vyāhita","meaning":"sick"},{"word":"araṇya","meaning":"forest"},{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-12-1', 'en', 'Food and breath need each other; neither alone is the highest.', 'Neither food nor breath is the ultimate on its own. The father hints that the knower has gone beyond being helped or harmed, and then gives the meditation ''vi-ram'' linking food and breath.', '[{"word":"anna","meaning":"food"},{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"breath"},{"word":"pūyati","meaning":"rots"},{"word":"śuṣyati","meaning":"dries up"},{"word":"ekadhābhūya","meaning":"becoming one"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-13-1', 'en', 'Breath is the hymn that lifts everything up.', 'The Uktha is a recited hymn. Its name is linked to ''raising up'', which is what breath does for the body.', '[{"word":"uktha","meaning":"recited hymn"},{"word":"utthāpayati","meaning":"raises up"},{"word":"sāyujya","meaning":"union"},{"word":"salokatā","meaning":"same world"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-13-2', 'en', 'Breath is the sacrificial formula that joins all beings together.', 'Yajus, the sacrificial formulas, is linked by sound to ''yuj'', to join. Breath is what holds all living things together.', '[{"word":"yajus","meaning":"sacrificial formula"},{"word":"yujyante","meaning":"are joined"},{"word":"śraiṣṭhya","meaning":"excellence"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-13-3', 'en', 'Breath is the chant that unites all beings.', 'Saman, the sung chant, is linked to ''sam'', together. Breath brings all beings into harmony.', '[{"word":"sāman","meaning":"chant"},{"word":"samyañc","meaning":"coming together"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-13-4', 'en', 'Breath is the protecting power that guards the body.', 'Kshatra, ruling power, is explained as ''protecting from harm''. Breath is the body''s natural guardian.', '[{"word":"kṣatra","meaning":"ruling power"},{"word":"trāyate","meaning":"protects"},{"word":"kṣaṇitu","meaning":"wound"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-1', 'en', 'The first foot of the Gayatri is the three worlds.', 'The Gayatri metre has three feet of eight syllables. In Sanskrit, bhūmi, antarikṣa, dyauḥ total eight syllables, so they make up its first foot.', '[{"word":"gāyatrī","meaning":"the sacred metre and mantra"},{"word":"pada","meaning":"foot, line"},{"word":"bhūmi","meaning":"earth"},{"word":"antarikṣa","meaning":"mid-space"},{"word":"dyauḥ","meaning":"sky"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-2', 'en', 'The second foot of the Gayatri is the three Vedas.', 'The names of the three Vedas also add up to eight syllables, so they form the second foot. Knowing this brings all that the Vedas contain.', '[{"word":"ṛc","meaning":"verse"},{"word":"yajus","meaning":"formula"},{"word":"sāman","meaning":"chant"},{"word":"trayī vidyā","meaning":"threefold knowledge"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-3', 'en', 'The third foot is breath; the hidden fourth foot is the shining sun beyond all.', 'The three breaths make the third foot. Then a fourth foot is named: the sun, shining above the ''dust'' of the world. This fourth, beyond the three, points to the highest.', '[{"word":"prāṇa","meaning":"in-breath"},{"word":"apāna","meaning":"out-breath"},{"word":"vyāna","meaning":"diffused breath"},{"word":"turīya","meaning":"fourth"},{"word":"parorajas","meaning":"beyond the dust"},{"word":"darśata","meaning":"visible"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-4', 'en', 'The Gayatri protects life-breath, which is why it is called Gayatri.', 'A chain of support: Gayatri rests on the sun, which rests on truth (the eye), which rests on strength (breath). The name is explained as ''protector of the breaths''. The Savitri mantra taught to students is this Gayatri.', '[{"word":"satya","meaning":"truth"},{"word":"cakṣus","meaning":"eye"},{"word":"bala","meaning":"strength"},{"word":"gaya","meaning":"breaths"},{"word":"trāyate","meaning":"protects"},{"word":"sāvitrī","meaning":"the Gayatri mantra to Savitri"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-5', 'en', 'Teach the Savitri in the Gayatri metre; no gift equals even one foot of it.', 'A ruling on how to teach the mantra, followed by praise: knowing the Gayatri so outweighs gifts that accepting them does the knower no harm.', '[{"word":"anuṣṭubh","meaning":"a metre of four eights"},{"word":"pratigṛhṇāti","meaning":"accepts gifts"},{"word":"pada","meaning":"foot"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-6', 'en', 'Even all the worlds as a gift would equal only one foot of the Gayatri.', 'The worth of the Gayatri is beyond measure. Its fourth foot, the highest, cannot be matched by any gift at all.', '[{"word":"pratigṛhṇīyāt","meaning":"might accept"},{"word":"āpya","meaning":"attainable"},{"word":"turīya","meaning":"fourth"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-7', 'en', 'A prayer to the Gayatri as having all feet and yet none.', 'The Gayatri is honoured as both having parts and being beyond all parts. The text then notes the old belief that this worship could be used to block an enemy''s wish or to gain one''s own.', '[{"word":"upasthāna","meaning":"worship"},{"word":"apad","meaning":"footless"},{"word":"namaḥ","meaning":"homage"},{"word":"samṛdhyate","meaning":"succeeds"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-14-8', 'en', 'Fire is the mouth of the Gayatri; knowing it burns away all wrongdoing.', 'A story: a man who claimed to know the Gayatri was reborn as a beast of burden because he missed one key part, its ''mouth'', fire. The lesson is that full knowledge purifies, as fire consumes everything put into it.', '[{"word":"mukha","meaning":"mouth"},{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"},{"word":"hastī","meaning":"elephant"},{"word":"śuddha","meaning":"clean"},{"word":"pūta","meaning":"pure"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-5-15-1', 'en', 'A dying person''s prayer to see the truth behind the sun''s glare and be led on the good path.', 'These verses also appear at the end of the Isha Upanishad. The golden glare of the world hides the truth; the speaker asks the sun to draw back its rays, recognises ''I am that person'', and asks fire to guide them after death.', '[{"word":"hiraṇmaya pātra","meaning":"golden vessel"},{"word":"pūṣan","meaning":"the nourisher, the sun"},{"word":"so ''ham asmi","meaning":"I am he"},{"word":"bhasmānta","meaning":"ending in ashes"},{"word":"kratu","meaning":"mind, will"},{"word":"supathā","meaning":"by the good path"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-1', 'en', 'Breath is the eldest and best of the body''s powers.', 'The last chapter opens with the contest of the senses. Breath is named first as the most senior and most important.', '[{"word":"jyeṣṭha","meaning":"eldest"},{"word":"śreṣṭha","meaning":"best"},{"word":"svānām","meaning":"among one''s own"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-2', 'en', 'Speech is the richest power; knowing this brings standing among people.', 'Part of a list praising each of the body''s powers in turn. Each power brings a matching blessing to the person who understands it.', '[{"word":"vasiṣṭha","meaning":"richest"},{"word":"vāk","meaning":"speech"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-3', 'en', 'Sight gives a firm footing in easy and hard places alike.', 'The eye lets us see where to step, so it is the ''firm base''. Knowing this gives steadiness in good times and hard ones.', '[{"word":"pratiṣṭhā","meaning":"firm base"},{"word":"sama","meaning":"level ground"},{"word":"durga","meaning":"rough ground"},{"word":"cakṣus","meaning":"eye"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-4', 'en', 'Hearing is attainment; through it all sacred knowledge is gained.', 'The Vedas were learned by listening, so hearing is the power through which knowledge is attained.', '[{"word":"saṃpad","meaning":"attainment"},{"word":"śrotra","meaning":"ear"},{"word":"kāma","meaning":"desire"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-5', 'en', 'The mind is the home where everything rests.', 'All the senses report to the mind, so it is their resting place. One who knows this becomes a support for others.', '[{"word":"āyatana","meaning":"resting place, abode"},{"word":"manas","meaning":"mind"},{"word":"jana","meaning":"people"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-6', 'en', 'The power of procreation brings children and plenty.', 'The last power in the list is the generative one, which brings family and wealth.', '[{"word":"prajāti","meaning":"procreation"},{"word":"retas","meaning":"semen"},{"word":"paśu","meaning":"cattle"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-7', 'en', 'The powers ask Brahman which of them is greatest; the test is who the body misses most.', 'The famous contest begins. The test is simple: whichever power, when it leaves, makes the body suffer most is the most important.', '[{"word":"vivadamāna","meaning":"arguing"},{"word":"ahaṃśreyas","meaning":"who is best"},{"word":"utkrānta","meaning":"departed"},{"word":"pāpīyas","meaning":"worse off"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-8', 'en', 'The body lives on without speech; it is not the most essential power.', 'Each sense power leaves for a year to test its importance. The body survives without speech, only unable to speak. This builds toward the lesson that breath is the greatest.', '[{"word":"utkrāma","meaning":"went out"},{"word":"saṃvatsara","meaning":"a year"},{"word":"proṣya","meaning":"having stayed away"},{"word":"praviveśa","meaning":"entered again"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-9', 'en', 'The body lives on without sight; it is not the most essential power.', 'Each sense power leaves for a year to test its importance. The body survives without sight, only unable to see. This builds toward the lesson that breath is the greatest.', '[{"word":"utkrāma","meaning":"went out"},{"word":"saṃvatsara","meaning":"a year"},{"word":"proṣya","meaning":"having stayed away"},{"word":"praviveśa","meaning":"entered again"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-10', 'en', 'The body lives on without hearing; it is not the most essential power.', 'Each sense power leaves for a year to test its importance. The body survives without hearing, only unable to hear. This builds toward the lesson that breath is the greatest.', '[{"word":"utkrāma","meaning":"went out"},{"word":"saṃvatsara","meaning":"a year"},{"word":"proṣya","meaning":"having stayed away"},{"word":"praviveśa","meaning":"entered again"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-11', 'en', 'The body lives on without the mind; it is not the most essential power.', 'Each sense power leaves for a year to test its importance. The body survives without the mind, only without thought. This builds toward the lesson that breath is the greatest.', '[{"word":"utkrāma","meaning":"went out"},{"word":"saṃvatsara","meaning":"a year"},{"word":"proṣya","meaning":"having stayed away"},{"word":"praviveśa","meaning":"entered again"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-12', 'en', 'The body lives on without the power of procreation; it is not the most essential power.', 'Each sense power leaves for a year to test its importance. The body survives without the power of procreation, only without children. This builds toward the lesson that breath is the greatest.', '[{"word":"utkrāma","meaning":"went out"},{"word":"saṃvatsara","meaning":"a year"},{"word":"proṣya","meaning":"having stayed away"},{"word":"praviveśa","meaning":"entered again"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-13', 'en', 'When breath starts to leave, all the other powers are pulled out with it; breath is the greatest.', 'The climax of the contest. A powerful horse yanks up its tethering pegs; in the same way, breath''s departure would uproot every other power. They all accept breath as their chief.', '[{"word":"utkramiṣyan","meaning":"about to leave"},{"word":"saindhava","meaning":"horse from Sindh"},{"word":"śaṅku","meaning":"pegs"},{"word":"bali","meaning":"tribute"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-1-14', 'en', 'All the powers give their qualities to breath; all food is breath''s food, and water is its clothing.', 'Every power admits that its gift really belongs to breath. The custom of sipping water before and after a meal is explained as ''clothing'' the breath.', '[{"word":"bali","meaning":"tribute"},{"word":"anna","meaning":"food"},{"word":"vāsas","meaning":"clothing"},{"word":"āpaḥ","meaning":"water"},{"word":"ācāmanti","meaning":"sip water"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-1', 'en', 'A proud young scholar, Shvetaketu, meets King Pravahana.', 'This story, also told in the Chandogya, begins the teaching of the five fires. Shvetaketu thinks his education is complete.', '[{"word":"śvetaketu","meaning":"son of Aruni"},{"word":"pariṣad","meaning":"assembly"},{"word":"anuśiṣṭa","meaning":"taught"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-2', 'en', 'The king asks five questions about death and rebirth; Shvetaketu cannot answer any.', 'These questions about what happens after death form the framework of the teaching that follows. The young man''s ignorance humbles him.', '[{"word":"prayat","meaning":"departing"},{"word":"devayāna","meaning":"path of the gods"},{"word":"pitṛyāṇa","meaning":"path of the ancestors"},{"word":"āhuti","meaning":"offering"},{"word":"sṛti","meaning":"path"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-3', 'en', 'Shvetaketu rushes home upset that his father''s teaching left gaps.', 'He is embarrassed and blames his father. The father calmly asks what the questions were.', '[{"word":"vasati","meaning":"lodging"},{"word":"rājanyabandhu","meaning":"fellow of the warrior class"},{"word":"praśna","meaning":"questions"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-4', 'en', 'The humble father goes to the king to learn; the king receives him with honour.', 'Gautama (Aruni) admits he does not know and is willing to become a student again. His son is too proud to go. The king welcomes him as an honoured guest.', '[{"word":"brahmacarya","meaning":"life as a student"},{"word":"āsana","meaning":"seat"},{"word":"arghya","meaning":"guest offering"},{"word":"vara","meaning":"boon"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-5', 'en', 'Gautama asks for knowledge, not wealth.', 'He chooses the teaching the king mentioned, rather than any material gift.', '[{"word":"pratijñāta","meaning":"promised"},{"word":"vāc","meaning":"words"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-6', 'en', 'The king tries to offer worldly gifts instead.', 'The king hesitates to share this rare knowledge and suggests wealth.', '[{"word":"daiva","meaning":"divine"},{"word":"mānuṣa","meaning":"human"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-7', 'en', 'Gautama refuses wealth and formally asks to become the king''s student.', 'He already has riches; he wants the infinite. By declaring ''I come to you as a student'', he accepts the humble place needed to receive the teaching.', '[{"word":"upaimi","meaning":"I approach (as a student)"},{"word":"ananta","meaning":"infinite"},{"word":"tīrtha","meaning":"proper way"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-8', 'en', 'The king admits this knowledge was kept among the warrior class, and agrees to share it.', 'An unusual moment: a king teaches a priest. The text notes this knowledge had not been held by any Brahmin before.', '[{"word":"vidyā","meaning":"knowledge"},{"word":"brāhmaṇa","meaning":"priest"},{"word":"pratyākhyātum","meaning":"to refuse"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-9', 'en', 'The first fire is the heavenly world, where faith offered becomes Soma.', 'The five-fire teaching sees the cycle of life as a chain of sacrifices. In the first, faith is offered in the sky and becomes Soma, the moon''s nectar.', '[{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire"},{"word":"samidh","meaning":"fuel"},{"word":"arcis","meaning":"flame"},{"word":"aṅgāra","meaning":"embers"},{"word":"śraddhā","meaning":"faith"},{"word":"soma","meaning":"Soma, the moon"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-10', 'en', 'The second fire is the rain-cloud, where Soma becomes rain.', 'The chain continues: what came from the first fire is offered in the second.', '[{"word":"parjanya","meaning":"rain-cloud"},{"word":"abhra","meaning":"clouds"},{"word":"vidyut","meaning":"lightning"},{"word":"vṛṣṭi","meaning":"rain"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-11', 'en', 'The third fire is this world, where rain becomes food.', 'Rain falling on earth becomes the food that grows from it.', '[{"word":"pṛthivī","meaning":"earth"},{"word":"rātri","meaning":"night"},{"word":"nakṣatra","meaning":"stars"},{"word":"anna","meaning":"food"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-12', 'en', 'The fourth fire is the man, where food becomes the seed of life.', 'Food eaten by a person is transformed into the power to create new life.', '[{"word":"puruṣa","meaning":"man"},{"word":"vyātta","meaning":"open mouth"},{"word":"retas","meaning":"semen"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-13', 'en', 'The fifth fire is the woman, where seed becomes a new person.', 'The last of the five fires completes the cycle: from faith in heaven to a living human being. The account of what happens after death follows.', '[{"word":"yoṣā","meaning":"woman"},{"word":"puruṣa","meaning":"person"},{"word":"jīvati","meaning":"lives"},{"word":"mriyate","meaning":"dies"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-14', 'en', 'At death the body is offered to the cremation fire, and the person rises shining.', 'Cremation is seen as the final sacrifice. The person offered into the fire emerges in a bright new form, ready for the journey on.', '[{"word":"bhāsvaravarṇa","meaning":"radiant in colour"},{"word":"juhvati","meaning":"offer"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-15', 'en', 'Those who know and live by truth take the path of light to Brahman and do not return.', 'This is the ''path of the gods'', the way of light. It leads beyond the cycle of birth and death.', '[{"word":"devayāna","meaning":"path of the gods"},{"word":"arcis","meaning":"flame"},{"word":"udagayana","meaning":"northern course of the sun"},{"word":"brahmaloka","meaning":"world of Brahman"},{"word":"punar āvṛtti","meaning":"return"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-2-16', 'en', 'Those who rely only on good deeds take the path of smoke and return to birth again.', 'This is the ''path of the ancestors''. Good deeds bring a stay in a higher world, but when their merit is used up, one returns to be born again. Those who follow neither path fall to the lowest births.', '[{"word":"pitṛyāṇa","meaning":"path of the ancestors"},{"word":"dhūma","meaning":"smoke"},{"word":"dakṣiṇāyana","meaning":"southern course of the sun"},{"word":"candra","meaning":"moon"},{"word":"anuparivartante","meaning":"go round and round"},{"word":"kīṭa","meaning":"insects"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-1', 'en', 'A rite for someone seeking greatness begins with offerings to win over the powers that block desires.', 'Chapter 6.3 describes the ''mantha'' rite, a mixed drink of herbs offered with prayers for success. These are ritual instructions, preserved as part of the Upanishad.', '[{"word":"mantha","meaning":"a mixed drink"},{"word":"udagayana","meaning":"northern course of the sun"},{"word":"audumbara","meaning":"of fig wood"},{"word":"jātavedas","meaning":"Agni, the fire"},{"word":"svāhā","meaning":"call of offering"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-2', 'en', 'Offerings are made to each life power named in the contest of the senses.', 'Each offering honours one of the powers from 6.1, and the drops left on the ladle are poured into the drink, so it gathers their strength.', '[{"word":"saṃsrava","meaning":"remaining drops"},{"word":"avanayati","meaning":"pours"},{"word":"svāhā","meaning":"offering call"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-3', 'en', 'More offerings: to fire, Soma, the three worlds, past, future and the whole.', 'The offerings now widen to cover the gods, the worlds and all of time, filling the drink with everything.', '[{"word":"agni","meaning":"fire god"},{"word":"soma","meaning":"moon god"},{"word":"bhūta","meaning":"past"},{"word":"bhaviṣyat","meaning":"future"},{"word":"viśva","meaning":"universe"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-4', 'en', 'The drink is praised as everything, the whole cycle of the chant and of life.', 'Touching the drink, the worshipper names it with all the stages of the sacred chant and with the beginning and end of things, treating it as a symbol of breath.', '[{"word":"bhramat","meaning":"moving"},{"word":"jvalat","meaning":"glowing"},{"word":"pūrṇa","meaning":"full"},{"word":"udgītha","meaning":"the sung chant"},{"word":"nidhana","meaning":"the end"},{"word":"saṃvarga","meaning":"gatherer"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-5', 'en', 'Lifting the drink, he prays to gain leadership.', 'The drink, standing for breath, is honoured as a king, and the worshipper asks to share its power.', '[{"word":"rājan","meaning":"king"},{"word":"īśāna","meaning":"lord"},{"word":"adhipati","meaning":"ruler"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-6', 'en', 'He drinks while chanting the Gayatri and the honey verses, asking that all the world be sweet.', 'The Gayatri mantra is interwoven, line by line, with the ''madhu'' verses wishing sweetness on wind, rivers, plants, day and night. The rite ends with a prayer to the sun at dawn.', '[{"word":"savitṛ","meaning":"the sun as inspirer"},{"word":"madhu","meaning":"honey, sweetness"},{"word":"bhargas","meaning":"light"},{"word":"dhī","meaning":"thought"},{"word":"puṇḍarīka","meaning":"lotus"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-7', 'en', 'The rite is so powerful it could make a dead stump grow leaves.', 'Part of the chain showing how the mantha rite was handed down, here from Uddalaka Aruni to Yajnavalkya Vajasaneya. The saying about the dry stump praises its life-giving power.', '[{"word":"antevāsin","meaning":"pupil"},{"word":"śuṣka sthāṇu","meaning":"dry stump"},{"word":"śākhā","meaning":"branches"},{"word":"palāśa","meaning":"leaves"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-8', 'en', 'The rite is so powerful it could make a dead stump grow leaves.', 'Part of the chain showing how the mantha rite was handed down, here from Yajnavalkya Vajasaneya to Madhuka Paingya. The saying about the dry stump praises its life-giving power.', '[{"word":"antevāsin","meaning":"pupil"},{"word":"śuṣka sthāṇu","meaning":"dry stump"},{"word":"śākhā","meaning":"branches"},{"word":"palāśa","meaning":"leaves"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-9', 'en', 'The rite is so powerful it could make a dead stump grow leaves.', 'Part of the chain showing how the mantha rite was handed down, here from Madhuka Paingya to Chula Bhagavitti. The saying about the dry stump praises its life-giving power.', '[{"word":"antevāsin","meaning":"pupil"},{"word":"śuṣka sthāṇu","meaning":"dry stump"},{"word":"śākhā","meaning":"branches"},{"word":"palāśa","meaning":"leaves"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-10', 'en', 'The rite is so powerful it could make a dead stump grow leaves.', 'Part of the chain showing how the mantha rite was handed down, here from Chula Bhagavitti to Janaki Ayasthuna. The saying about the dry stump praises its life-giving power.', '[{"word":"antevāsin","meaning":"pupil"},{"word":"śuṣka sthāṇu","meaning":"dry stump"},{"word":"śākhā","meaning":"branches"},{"word":"palāśa","meaning":"leaves"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-11', 'en', 'The rite is so powerful it could make a dead stump grow leaves.', 'Part of the chain showing how the mantha rite was handed down, here from Janaki Ayasthuna to Satyakama Jabala. The saying about the dry stump praises its life-giving power.', '[{"word":"antevāsin","meaning":"pupil"},{"word":"śuṣka sthāṇu","meaning":"dry stump"},{"word":"śākhā","meaning":"branches"},{"word":"palāśa","meaning":"leaves"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-12', 'en', 'The rite is passed on, and should be taught only to one''s son or student.', 'The chain of transmission ends with a rule of secrecy: this knowledge is shared only within close teaching bonds.', '[{"word":"antevāsin","meaning":"pupil"},{"word":"putra","meaning":"son"},{"word":"brūyāt","meaning":"should tell"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-3-13', 'en', 'The rite uses fig-wood tools and ten kinds of grain mixed with curd, honey and butter.', 'Practical details of the mantha rite: what the utensils are made of and what goes into the drink.', '[{"word":"audumbara","meaning":"fig wood"},{"word":"sruva","meaning":"ladle"},{"word":"camasa","meaning":"cup"},{"word":"dhānya","meaning":"grain"},{"word":"dadhi","meaning":"curd"},{"word":"ghṛta","meaning":"clarified butter"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-1', 'en', 'Life passes its essence upward from earth to plants to people to new life.', 'A chain of essences: each stage of nature gathers the best of the one before, ending in the seed of new human life. This sets the scene for the rites of conception.', '[{"word":"rasa","meaning":"essence"},{"word":"pṛthivī","meaning":"earth"},{"word":"oṣadhi","meaning":"plants"},{"word":"puṣpa","meaning":"flowers"},{"word":"phala","meaning":"fruits"},{"word":"retas","meaning":"semen"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-2', 'en', 'The creator made woman as a foundation for life and honoured her.', 'Union between man and woman is described in the language of the Soma sacrifice, treating it as something sacred. The text says woman should be honoured as the creator honoured her.', '[{"word":"prajāpati","meaning":"creator"},{"word":"pratiṣṭhā","meaning":"foundation"},{"word":"strī","meaning":"woman"},{"word":"upāsta","meaning":"honoured"},{"word":"grāvan","meaning":"pressing stone"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-3', 'en', 'Union approached with understanding is like a great sacrifice; without it, one loses merit.', 'The text treats married union as a ritual act. Doing it mindfully, as something sacred, is said to bring blessing; doing it carelessly brings loss.', '[{"word":"vedi","meaning":"altar"},{"word":"vājapeya","meaning":"a great Soma sacrifice"},{"word":"sukṛta","meaning":"merit"},{"word":"vidvān","meaning":"one who knows"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-4', 'en', 'Teachers warn that careless union drains strength and merit.', 'Three teachers are cited in support. The verse moves on to the case of involuntary loss of seed, handled in the next verse.', '[{"word":"nirindriya","meaning":"without strength"},{"word":"visukṛta","meaning":"without merit"},{"word":"skandati","meaning":"is spilled"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-5', 'en', 'A short prayer to recover lost vitality.', 'A ritual remedy for involuntary loss, asking that strength and good fortune come back. Chapter 6.4 is an ancient household rite for married couples about conception and childbirth. It is preserved as part of the text and reflects the customs of its time.', '[{"word":"indriya","meaning":"vigour"},{"word":"tejas","meaning":"energy"},{"word":"bhaga","meaning":"fortune"},{"word":"anāmikā","meaning":"ring finger"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-6', 'en', 'A blessing on seeing one''s reflection, and guidance on the right time for a couple to come together.', 'The text reflects ancient beliefs about purity and the right time for conception. Chapter 6.4 is an ancient household rite for married couples about conception and childbirth. It is preserved as part of the text and reflects the customs of its time.', '[{"word":"teja","meaning":"energy"},{"word":"yaśas","meaning":"fame"},{"word":"draviṇa","meaning":"wealth"},{"word":"śrī","meaning":"good fortune"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-7', 'en', 'An ancient verse reflecting attitudes to consent that are rejected today.', 'This verse reflects the harsh social norms of its age, and it is reproduced here only because it is part of the text. Modern readers, and traditional teachers too, do not treat it as guidance: consent and respect within marriage are essential, and the same chapter (6.4.2) tells us to honour women.', '[{"word":"yaśas","meaning":"glory"},{"word":"indriya","meaning":"vigour"},{"word":"ayaśas","meaning":"without glory"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-8', 'en', 'When both are willing, both share in honour.', 'A mutual blessing: when the couple come together willingly, both are said to gain glory.', '[{"word":"yaśasvin","meaning":"glorious"},{"word":"ādadhāmi","meaning":"I give"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-9', 'en', 'A love charm for a husband hoping to win his wife''s affection.', 'An old love verse that treats desire as coming from the heart and every limb. Chapter 6.4 is an ancient household rite for married couples about conception and childbirth. It is preserved as part of the text and reflects the customs of its time.', '[{"word":"aṅga","meaning":"limb"},{"word":"hṛdaya","meaning":"heart"},{"word":"kaṣāya","meaning":"essence"},{"word":"mādaya","meaning":"make love, delight"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-10', 'en', 'A rite for a couple who do not wish to conceive.', 'The text includes a ritual for avoiding pregnancy, showing that ancient households thought about family planning. Chapter 6.4 is an ancient household rite for married couples about conception and childbirth. It is preserved as part of the text and reflects the customs of its time.', '[{"word":"garbha","meaning":"embryo"},{"word":"retas","meaning":"seed"},{"word":"aretā","meaning":"without seed"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-11', 'en', 'A rite for a couple hoping to conceive.', 'The mirror of the previous verse, for couples who want a child.', '[{"word":"garbhiṇī","meaning":"pregnant"},{"word":"ādadhāmi","meaning":"I place"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-12', 'en', 'A curse against a wife''s lover, warning people not to violate a marriage.', 'An ancient rite of curse. Its point is a warning about respecting marriage. Chapter 6.4 is an ancient household rite for married couples about conception and childbirth. It is preserved as part of the text and reflects the customs of its time.', '[{"word":"jāra","meaning":"lover"},{"word":"pratiloma","meaning":"in reverse order"},{"word":"śapati","meaning":"curses"},{"word":"śrotriya","meaning":"learned priest"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-13', 'en', 'Customs observed during a wife''s monthly period.', 'These rules reflect the purity customs of the time. Chapter 6.4 is an ancient household rite for married couples about conception and childbirth. It is preserved as part of the text and reflects the customs of its time.', '[{"word":"ārtava","meaning":"monthly period"},{"word":"kaṃsa","meaning":"metal cup"},{"word":"ahatavāsas","meaning":"in fresh clothes"},{"word":"āplutya","meaning":"having bathed"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-14', 'en', 'A traditional diet is prescribed for the hope of a particular kind of child.', 'Chapter 6.4 is an ancient household rite for married couples about conception and childbirth. It is preserved as part of the text and reflects the customs of its time. Verses 14 to 18 link particular foods with hopes for a child''s qualities and learning.', '[{"word":"odana","meaning":"cooked rice"},{"word":"sarpis","meaning":"clarified butter"},{"word":"īśvarau janayitavai","meaning":"they will be able to beget"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-15', 'en', 'A traditional diet is prescribed for the hope of a particular kind of child.', 'Chapter 6.4 is an ancient household rite for married couples about conception and childbirth. It is preserved as part of the text and reflects the customs of its time. Verses 14 to 18 link particular foods with hopes for a child''s qualities and learning.', '[{"word":"odana","meaning":"cooked rice"},{"word":"sarpis","meaning":"clarified butter"},{"word":"īśvarau janayitavai","meaning":"they will be able to beget"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-16', 'en', 'A traditional diet is prescribed for the hope of a particular kind of child.', 'Chapter 6.4 is an ancient household rite for married couples about conception and childbirth. It is preserved as part of the text and reflects the customs of its time. Verses 14 to 18 link particular foods with hopes for a child''s qualities and learning.', '[{"word":"odana","meaning":"cooked rice"},{"word":"sarpis","meaning":"clarified butter"},{"word":"īśvarau janayitavai","meaning":"they will be able to beget"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-17', 'en', 'A traditional diet is prescribed for the hope of a particular kind of child.', 'Chapter 6.4 is an ancient household rite for married couples about conception and childbirth. It is preserved as part of the text and reflects the customs of its time. Verses 14 to 18 link particular foods with hopes for a child''s qualities and learning.', '[{"word":"odana","meaning":"cooked rice"},{"word":"sarpis","meaning":"clarified butter"},{"word":"īśvarau janayitavai","meaning":"they will be able to beget"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-18', 'en', 'A diet is prescribed in hope of a learned and eloquent son.', 'Chapter 6.4 is an ancient household rite for married couples about conception and childbirth. It is preserved as part of the text and reflects the customs of its time. The verse shows the high value placed on learning and eloquence.', '[{"word":"paṇḍita","meaning":"learned"},{"word":"samitiṃgama","meaning":"one who speaks at assemblies"},{"word":"māṃsa","meaning":"meat"},{"word":"odana","meaning":"cooked rice"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-19', 'en', 'A dawn offering and blessing before the couple''s union.', 'Offerings are made to Agni, Anumati and Savitri, and a playful verse asks the spirit Vishvavasu to leave the wife to her husband.', '[{"word":"sthālīpāka","meaning":"pot of cooked food"},{"word":"anumati","meaning":"goddess of favour"},{"word":"savitṛ","meaning":"the sun"},{"word":"abhyukṣati","meaning":"sprinkles"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-20', 'en', 'Husband and wife are pictured as sky and earth, chant and verse, complete together.', 'The couple are compared to pairs that belong together: the melody and its words, sky and earth. Each completes the other.', '[{"word":"sāman","meaning":"melody"},{"word":"ṛc","meaning":"verse"},{"word":"dyauḥ","meaning":"sky"},{"word":"pṛthivī","meaning":"earth"},{"word":"saṃrabhāvahai","meaning":"let us join"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-21', 'en', 'A prayer to many gods to bless conception.', 'A verse from the Rig Veda tradition asks the gods of creation, shaping and birth to help form the child.', '[{"word":"viṣṇu","meaning":"preserver god"},{"word":"tvaṣṭṛ","meaning":"the divine shaper"},{"word":"dhātṛ","meaning":"the placer, creator"},{"word":"sinīvālī","meaning":"goddess of the new moon"},{"word":"aśvinau","meaning":"twin healer gods"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-22', 'en', 'The coming child is compared to fire within the earth.', 'The images of fire, thunder and wind inside the world express the mystery of new life forming. ''Tenth month'' counts lunar months.', '[{"word":"araṇī","meaning":"fire-sticks"},{"word":"garbha","meaning":"embryo"},{"word":"daśame māsi","meaning":"in the tenth month"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-23', 'en', 'A prayer for an easy childbirth.', 'A blessing at the time of labour asks that the baby come out smoothly, as wind ripples water.', '[{"word":"soṣyantī","meaning":"about to give birth"},{"word":"puṣkariṇī","meaning":"lotus pond"},{"word":"jarāyu","meaning":"afterbirth"},{"word":"indra","meaning":"king of the gods"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-24', 'en', 'A father''s offering of thanks and prayer for prosperity at a child''s birth.', 'After the birth, the father makes an offering, prays for the family''s future, and asks Agni to correct any mistakes in the rite.', '[{"word":"pṛṣadājya","meaning":"curd mixed with butter"},{"word":"edhamāna","meaning":"prospering"},{"word":"sviṣṭakṛt","meaning":"maker of good offerings"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-25', 'en', 'The newborn is welcomed with the gift of speech and a first taste of honey.', 'This birth rite (jātakarma) is still practised in some families: the word ''speech'' is whispered and a taste of honey and butter given, with a blessing of the three worlds.', '[{"word":"vāc","meaning":"speech"},{"word":"dadhi","meaning":"curd"},{"word":"madhu","meaning":"honey"},{"word":"jātarūpa","meaning":"gold"},{"word":"dadhāmi","meaning":"I place"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-26', 'en', 'The child is given a secret name meaning ''knowledge''.', 'Besides the everyday name, the child receives a hidden name, ''Veda'', a wish that they become a person of knowledge.', '[{"word":"nāma","meaning":"name"},{"word":"veda","meaning":"knowledge"},{"word":"guhya","meaning":"secret"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-27', 'en', 'The mother nurses the child as the goddess Sarasvati nourishes the world.', 'The first feeding is blessed by likening the mother''s milk to the bounty of Sarasvati, goddess of learning and flowing waters.', '[{"word":"stana","meaning":"breast"},{"word":"sarasvatī","meaning":"goddess of learning"},{"word":"dhātave","meaning":"to drink"},{"word":"mayobhū","meaning":"giving joy"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-4-28', 'en', 'The mother is honoured as a heroine, and the child is blessed to surpass his elders.', 'The rite ends with praise for the mother and a hope that the child will grow even greater than his father and grandfather.', '[{"word":"iḍā","meaning":"Ila, goddess of nourishment"},{"word":"vīra","meaning":"hero"},{"word":"atipitā","meaning":"surpassing the father"},{"word":"brahmavarcasa","meaning":"spiritual radiance"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-5-1', 'en', 'The final list of teachers begins, each named after his mother.', 'Unusually, this lineage names teachers by their mothers (son of Katyayani, son of Gautami), showing the honour given to mothers in this line.', '[{"word":"vaṃśa","meaning":"lineage"},{"word":"putra","meaning":"son"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-5-3', 'en', 'The lineage reaches back through Yajnavalkya to the Sun, the source of the White Yajur Veda.', 'The source edition skips verse 6.5.2 (a gap). This verse traces the White Yajur Veda, to which this Upanishad belongs, back to the Sun, which tradition says revealed it to Yajnavalkya.', '[{"word":"yājñavalkya","meaning":"the great sage of this Upanishad"},{"word":"śukla yajus","meaning":"the White Yajur Veda"},{"word":"āditya","meaning":"the Sun"},{"word":"vāc","meaning":"Speech"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft'),
('brihadaranyaka-6-5-4', 'en', 'The Upanishad ends by tracing its teaching back to Brahman itself.', 'The final words of the Brihadaranyaka bring the chain of teachers back to the creator and then to Brahman, the self-existent source, with a closing salutation.', '[{"word":"samānam","meaning":"the same"},{"word":"svayambhu","meaning":"self-existent"},{"word":"brahmaṇe namaḥ","meaning":"homage to Brahman"}]'::jsonb, 'veda-app-ai-draft', 'draft');

